There were still reserves here," said P. K. Subban, one of the defenders.
Czech goalkeeper Ondřej Pavelec fended off thirty shots from Tampa Bay, contributing significantly to Winnipeg's 5:2 victory.
Five different shooters took part in the gates of the jets, which ended a series of five defeats.
Travel prices development since 1989
Until 1989, there were only five approved state travel agencies in ČSSR: Čedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
At that time, the travel agencies sold mainly only trips to the socialist eastern bloc countries, to the West at that time only the chosen ones were allowed.
And so Czech tourists travelled to the Baltic Sea (to the German Democratic Republic - the GDR), to the Soviet Union (Sochi on the Crimean peninsula on the Black Sea), to Bulgaria and Romania to the Black Sea and to Hungary to Lake Balaton.
A clear exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when one was also allowed to travel to Yugoslavia, then Yugoslavia embarked on a pro-Western course and therefore the trips to Yugoslavia were severely restricted.
It was a great paradox of those socialist times that not even citizens of the Eastern Bloc (i.e. the Czechoslovaks) were allowed to travel freely within the framework of the socialist countries.
If, for example, a citizen of the ČSSR wanted to travel individually to Poland or even to the Soviet Union, then he needed a so-called invitation letter, otherwise individual trips were not possible.
Even then, the travel agencies also offered trips to the west, but then you had to accept huge queues or spend the night in the sleeping bag in front of the travel agency, or at least have "good acquaintances" in the travel agency.
Even so, things hadn't gone long ago.
The so-called foreign exchange commitment - the condition for an exit to the West - was granted only "politically reliable" people - the others were not allowed to travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely exchangeable currency, so it would have been impossible to raise the necessary financial resources (Western currency) for a trip to the West.
Most Czechs, therefore, spent their holidays domestically, no wonder that the Czechs bought countless weekend houses and dachen - a habit that continues to this day.
The velvet revolution of 1989 was followed by a travel revolution of the Czechs.
While until 1989 a holiday in the GDR or in Bulgaria was the measure of all things, last year the Czechs spent 4.5 million of their foreign holidays in countries around the world.
A huge paradox, of which one is no longer aware today, is the fact that you could not travel at all to the currently most popular holiday country of the Czech - Croatia until 1989, because it was part of the former Yugoslavia.
Immediately from the beginning of the 990s of the last century, the foreign exchange obligation in the neighboring Western European countries was abolished, which provoked a huge wave of short visits mainly to Vienna in Austria and Germany.
The Czechoslovaks simply couldn’t get enough for a long time from their journeys to the West, everyone wanted to have been there for at least a few days.
So the Czech holidaymakers slept while traveling to Paris in the tent in the park, because who would have already had the money for accommodation in the hotel or in a guesthouse at that time.
At that time, a typical feature of foreign trips from Czech was a trunk packed with canned food, beer and permanent sausage, etc.
Another important milestone was the introduction of the freely exchangable Czech krona - after decades, our tourists were finally able to buy the western currency freely.
Our level of living and thus also the average salaries increased, which led to another important moment in 1997.
Thanks to the increased demands for travel, the travel agencies regularly began to prepare trips through charter flights after 1997, as a result of which air trips, thanks to the possibility of negotiating volume discounts, were incredibly cheaper compared to the past.
At the end of the 1990s, with the exception of the United States, the Czechs already had currency-free payment transactions with all highly developed countries in the world, which made travelling even easier.
Another interesting moment in the history of "post-velvet" travel came in 2000, when thanks to the high demand, the first direct charter flight to an exotic country was handled - Thailand.
Since then, direct charter aircraft with Czech passengers have been flying to distant sunny countries every year; this year, twelve exotic destinations are flied directly from the Czech Republic.
Around 100 thousand Czechs travel to exotic countries every year!
Another important moment in the epoch that characterized our "post-revolutionary" development came in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S. revoked the visa requirement for Czech tourists, which definitively placed our country among the world’s highly developed countries.
In 2008, the Czechs spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%) for the first time and for the first time in their history they spent more than 15 Tsd per capita for a single holiday stay, including the costs at the holiday resort.
The annual general meeting is to find a new chairman - again a stalemate looms.
For almost five months, Czech football has no chairman and threatens that Ivan Hašek's successor at the head of the Czech Football Association (FAČR) won't be known even after Thursday's Extraordinary General Meeting in Nymburk.
To date, there is still no agreement on which electoral order should be elected.
And without this, no vote on the three potential successors is possible at all.
Three interested parties would like to manage Czech football - Jindřich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomáš Paclík.
In June, Hašek resigned from his function, prompting his colleagues at his resignation to reach an agreement and finding a strong personality that could guide football in the future.
Only - in the post-Hašek era, the old quarrels between the two association chambers erupted again, which cannot agree on a common candidate.
This was clearly evident on 16 September, when the new chairman was to be selected.
The delegates of the Annual General Meeting had come for nothing to the Volkshaus in the Smíchov district of Prague, because the negotiation led to no result and it was not only about the election itself.
You couldn't even agree on who is actually entitled to vote in the full collection - only the statute-related representatives or even the functionaries endowed with a power of attorney.
On this single point, at least partial agreement was reached, to which the recommendation of the Institute for State and Law also contributed, the opinion of which, in the opinion of a representation on a power of attorney basis, was not permissible.
Disputes over the electoral order, however, continue.
Also, the Executive Committee did not bring more clarity to the matter, at whose meetings - regardless of whether ordinary or extraordinary - in principle there is nothing else on the agenda at all.
Even if the views in relation to the election changed a few times - the neuralgic point remained the same.
The question is: how to proceed in round 3 to avoid a stalemate situation?
"The point of contention concerns the question of whether to proceed in accordance with the statutes, or whether a completely different model must be found," Dušan Svoboda, one of the three vice-chairmen of the FAČR, who represents professional football in management, noted.
According to the statutory model, in the third round the candidate who receives the greater percentage support in the chamber for which he is running wins.
Moravia is reportedly willing to respond to the compromise that the candidate who receives the higher number of votes from both chambers prevails.
The Czech side, in turn, insists on the condition that the victorian receives at least a two-thirds majority in this percentage ballot count.
"Then the two preliminary rounds no longer make sense at all, this mode would question the entire two-chamber concept," Svoboda understands the objections of the Moravian side, which it would be best not to bend the election regulations, but to vote according to valid statutes.
Surprisingly, Tomáš Paclík agrees with this, even if it becomes clear that the statutory electoral order of all candidates is most likely to accommodate Pelta.
"My wish is to be largely elected according to the statutes."
"So that nonsensical replacement variants are devised, as at the last annual general meeting," explained the owner (of the football club) Victoria Plzeň, who is running for the first time and delineated in his statements, Pelta in particular.
Pelta, however, remains optimistic.
In his opinion, the new chairman will be known in Nymburk and he believes the candidates will work together constructively in the future as well.
"The possible qualification of the national team for today's European Football Championship would certainly contribute to reassuring the spirits."
"If football first rides the euphoric wave, then the willingness to cooperate is also greater," says the functionary from Jablonec, who has the greatest experiences of all three adepts, both on a professional and regional level.
Rajchl's more restrained.
"I'm hoping for an agreement, but unfortunately the situation is ambiguous."
"This is not a matter between the people of Bohemia and Moravia, but there are certain efforts to "bend" the regulations for their own purposes."
"Therefore, a renewed blocking at the Annual General Meeting is not excluded."
"However, I hope that common sense ultimately prevails," Rajchl said.
Only - a lot of time to understand all sides is no longer.
Some negotiations were supposed to take place directly in Montenegro, but who knows whether the football generality will be complete until the qualifying match.
The charter aircraft, with which Dalibor Kučera and Rajchl were supposed to fly to Podgorice, was unable to take off in the morning in Prague due to a technical malfunction and so one was feverishly looking for a replacement variant, such as to bring a part of the executive committee still to the venue of the Barrage first leg.
Ultimately, the meeting at ministerial level, which is supposed to take place on Wednesday, is crucial.
Josef Dobeš, the Minister of Education, Youth and Physical Education, would have liked to move the divided parties within the country's largest sports federation to an agreement.
There is a lot at stake: state grants, the trustworthiness of Czech football and also the upcoming Annual General Meeting of the ČSTV, which is intended to point the new direction to Czech sport after the financial problems of Sazka.
Paclík goes into the fight for the chairman's position because he doesn't see a suitable candidate
If Tomáš Paclík, the head of Pil's footballers before the Extraordinary General Meeting, could have identified a strong candidate who would have fulfilled all his ideas about the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR), he wouldn't have embarked on the fight for this top position at all.
"If there was a good contender, I'd fight for him and give up the election."
"But in the current statement it failed to find such an adept," Paclík ČTK said.
The main reason for Paclík to go into a duel with Miroslav Pelta and Jindřich Rajchl is therefore to prevent the alleged favorite of the election, Pelta, from getting to the head of FAČR.
Paclík also emphasizes that in the event of his victory, he intends to carry out the function of chairman for only one year, i.e. until 2013, until the date of the Annual General Meeting.
In the position of the chairman, he would have liked to see an experienced top manager, who is not too entangled in football.
“I’ve spoken personally with some people in question.”
"It's hard to believe that some told me they didn't want to identify with Czech football at the moment.
"Such views would have liked to change," he cited.
Most of all, Paclík allegedly interferes with Pelta's connection with the chairman of Pilsn district football's governing body Roman Berbr, a man with a controversial ruse who is considered a powerful lobbyist behind the scenes, with the ability to influence delegate votes at general meetings.
For Paclík, it is unacceptable for Pelta would have held the position of chairman.
"In his victory, today's condition would be conserved and in Czech football it would not go ahead at all."
"The support by Berbr, a clever businessman, would be doubly bad for football," Paclík stressed.
He also alludes to the fact that Pelta's name fell some time ago in connection with telephone wiretaps for the investigation in a corruption affair, from which Pelta emerged free of punishment.
"I don't know what prestige a victory by Pelta should bring to football."
"And how would we stand in the eyes of UEFA and FIFA," said řekl Paclík, who said his earlier statement that "he will sell the Pilsner football club if Pelta becomes chairman," but already sees it a little more relaxed.
If Paclík wins in the Thursday election, however, he could imagine a future cooperation with Pelta.
That's what another candidate - Rajchl, whom Paclík had backeded before the annual general meeting in October that ended with a fiasco, thinks the same.
“He (Pelta) would be the first one I would go to.”
"He's got great skills."
"I could think of him well, for example, as head of the national team."
"But I don't think he's able to lead the association as a manager."
"I don't put my nose into matters that I don't understand," said Paclík, who had caused a stir in September that he was behind the removal of the refereeing commission with Luďěk Macela at the helm, which Dagmar Damková replaced.
Paclík himself claims that he feels the role of the chairman of the association.
And invokes his skills in the management of private companies, since last summer he has also been the head of Pilsen, who celebrated the first triumph in the league with his support in May.
"I have the power to change something in the football milieu and contribute to a certain cultivation of it."
"In order to limit the influence of certain interest groups who tend to influence disciplinary and appointmental commissions, which degenerates into disgusting situations," Paclík cited, who makes no secret of his antipathy, for example, towards Sparta chief Daniel Křetínský.
As one of his priorities, he sees the consolidation of relations between the FAČR and the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which he would lobby the top political politicians to ensure that more money from the lottery tax flows into the sport.
"That's one of the crucial things the new chairman should dedicate himself to."
"If it comes to the taxation of games of gay, as the political parties declare, then I understand their reasoning, but then they also have to say clearly where the money is going," he said.
Paclík also does not see his connection to the Pilsner club as a handicap, although he himself stresses that the chairman of FAČR must be "non-partisan."
Pelta, in turn, is associated with Jablonec, Rajchl with the Prague Dukla.
"That's not exactly ideal."
"However, it does not make much sense in my opinion to send my shares at the Pilsner Klub hypocrise to a third person in order to return after a year and a half," Paclík clarified.
Pelta promised new statutes of the FAČR, which will exclude a stalemate situation in the future.
Two days before the general meeting of the Football Association, one of the candidates for the chairman’s office, Miroslav Pelta, promised that in the event of his election, he would campaign for the FAČR to get new statutes, which in the future exclude disputes about the electoral regulations from the outset.
The functionary from Jablonec is confident before the Thursday trial in Nymburk and believes that the election campaign will certainly defuse itself in the eventual success of the Czech selection.
Pelta had already promised before the unrealized election in September that he wanted to focus on the national team and the acquisition of funding in the event of his election.
He decided only at the last moment to participate in the election before the last Annual General Meeting and so the two-month break would help him to defend his ideas about the management of football and present them in a broader context.
"People at all levels are devoting themselves to football, they are impatient and therefore expect the chairman to be known on Thursday."
"He feels it's high time for the association to be led by someone, says the man, who is mainly affiliated with the Jablonecer Club, but who also acted for many years at Sparta and on the executive committee.
This is also why he thinks that he has sufficient experience to lead Czech football.
He is convinced that today's problems with the election are responsible for the euphoria that prevailed at the time of Ivan Hašek.
"Unfortunately, statutes were endorsed at the annual general meeting in June that weren't as perfect as everyone had assumed.
If they hadn't allowed different interpretations, we'd had a chairman from September," Pelta said.
Especially the revision of the most important document of the Civic Association he used as a lure for the delegates.
"Should I become chairman, I personally accept it and am willing to take personal responsibility if it doesn't work out."
"That's a principled thing he can't be without," Pelta claims.
Furthermore, he does not try to delineate himself from his opponents.
Earlier, he had announced that he could envision a collaboration with Jindřich Rajchl, whom he recognizes because of his legal education, and he knows well that his opponent would be his first deputy.
Tomáš Paclík hasn't attacked him for a long time either, who in turn didn't leave a dry thread on Pelta.
In football, he has been moving for over a year and can already point to certain successes."
"However, his attacks were degrading and a lot of people were shocked by his election campaign."
"This is also why many now doubt his real character," Pelta addressed his opponent, who probably remains true to his proclamations, which he said in September.
In his opinion, his strongest weapon is the knowledge of football from the lowest playing classes to the national team.
And this should be the figurehead of Czech football.
"I'd also strive to end all affairs - from the Causa Bohemians, to the reverberations of last year's corruption scandal."
"Right after that, I would pounce on negotiations with the private and state sector to ensure sufficient funding for the whole of football," the forty-six-year-old candidate ČTK said two months ago.
At that time, he also promised that his entry into the top of Czech football would not mean any personnel purges.
"I would first have to familiarize myself with the work content of all employees," he said in the direction of Strahov, where due to the long anarchy there is comprehensibly nervousness.
Despite the current catastrophic scenarios that accompany the upcoming general meeting, he believes that the delegates will not go to Nymburk on Thursday for nothing.
"If it's really the only contentious point on how to vote in the third round, we'll get over it."
"Football needs credibility and he only wins it back when he finally has a new leader," Pelta remains optimistic two days ahead of the potentially decisive day of his functionary career.
Rajchl promises that the FAČR will start working hard under him
If Jindřich Rajchl were to be elected as the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic at the annual general meeting on Thursday, this would supposedly spoil the mood of many people in the association.
Rajchl promises that in the case of his choice you start working hard.
He wants to introduce system changes and blame the members of the executive committee for individual football areas.
In addition, he promised to solve Bohemian's case, improve relations with UEFA and FIFA, bring more money to football, and otherwise continue the work begun by the previous presidential Ivan Hašek.
"As chairman, I'd like to devote myself to football as a whole."
Starting with international contacts with UEFA and FIFA, through the national selection, professional football and performance football for the youth."
"I don't want to say that I want to do everything alone," Rajchl said in an interview for ČTK.
"I am a team player and would therefore like to distribute the competences in the executive committee in such a way that each member is dedicated to a certain area."
And I'm going to distribute hard tasks so that football is progressing with mileages in every segment.'
Many wouldn't exactly be delighted about this because, instead of just criticizing other people's work, they'd now have to start working really hard themselves," he added.
Because of the critical atmosphere in the football movement, he was considering no longer running at all.
"It's known I'll work 20 hours a day."
"But I need to know that it makes sense and that people stand behind me who don't constantly throw sticks between my legs and question every step I'm taking," he said as an explanation of why he hesitated.
"A lot of people told me that I shouldn't give up, because otherwise others would."
"That's confidence you're not allowed to squander," he said.
Supposedly he is also motivated to continue the work he started under Hašek.
"In two years we've mastered a big piece of work and I don't just want to throw that away now."
"Proceedings were started, which will probably only come into effect in a few years and I would be reluctant to see someone stop," he said, expressing his concerns that in the event that the other candidate - Miroslav Pelta would be elected, the situation could return to the times before Hašek.
"I believe in systematic action and that's something Mirek Pelta doesn't bring."
"Behind him there are especially people who would do football in such a way that he would only be there for the chosen ones."
I guess I've been blamed for a lot, but it's not that I'm partisan.'
“I strive to be objective.”
"I want football for everyone, because that's why everyone's doing well and not just the chosen one," Rajchl noted.
He also disagrees with the claim that he is only a chairman of the Czechs.
"Even though some people claim the opposite, I've really planned to do what's best for Moravia."
"For example, it's time to consider whether you shouldn't divide the league into a Czech and a Moravian part, the winners of whose winners then each ascended to the first league."
"Then it couldn't happen that only three Moravian clubs play in the league today, which is a problem especially for the young players in Moravia," says Rajchl.
Because the head of the Pilsner club Tomáš Paclík has now also joined the fight for the function of chairman, Rajchl does not dare to assess his chances that he will be elected.
"I feel strong support in the Czech Chamber, which has elected me its vice-president and now recognizes that clear successes are behind me," Rajchl cited, who also sees the saving of tens of millions of kronors for unfavourable treaties, the agreement of television contracts and the negotiations surrounding the lottery law as his success.
On the other hand, he blames himself in part for ensuring that the association does not pull together in a single strand.
While he says of Pelta that "he's disqualed from the outset to give Czech football new confidence," he thinks the second rival - Paclík is a strong candidate who really has something to offer football.
"Nobody can question that he's done an excellent job in Pilsen in just one year."
"Should he be elected, then he would probably continue the things that Ivan Hašek initiated and he would obviously not fundamentally change the staffing in the commissions."
He certainly wouldn't want to bend things in his favor," Rajchl judges his rival.
Europe's most beautiful pools
Leap into the blue
By Julia Stanek
Paddling pool in Ufo look, pomp pools in Budapest: If you travel through Europe, you can experience its blue marvel - and after a city tour or a hike, rest the tired legs in bubbly pools.
A book shows where wellness in the water is most beautiful.
If Iris Meder went on a research trip in the past year and a half, one thing could not be missing in her luggage: her swimsuit.
Returning on the water, she looked heaving up into artfully designed domes, she swam towards marble putties waiting for her at the edge of the pool, and marveled at meticulously restored Art Nouveau swimming pools.
If she liked a bathroom, she picked it up in her notebook - including interesting details about the construction and history of the building.
More than 200 swimming pools were explored by architectural historian Meder in this way - and now presents them in her recently published book "Badefreuden": Volksschwimmhallen in Munich, historical bathing palaces in the Black Forest, "Hardcore concrete buildings" in the Taunus.
Through 13 countries she led her "journey to the most extraordinary baths in Central Europe": in addition to Germany, Austria and Switzerland, she visited places in Italy, France, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia, Hungary, Romania, Poland - and one city each in Luxembourg, Serbia and Croatia.
The 46-year-old German is a true bathing culture enthusiast.
She doesn't have much to do with sports, says Meder - only in the water it's different.
Annoying only that swimming fans have to come to terms with blue tiles and chlorine stench, while joggers can inhale the beguiling scent of spruce forests or listening to cheerful birdsong in the park.
"I wondered one day why swimming pools must actually always be so ugly," says Meder, who lives in Vienna.
"So I started collecting beautiful baths."
Water surface doubling architecture
Splashes in the diving pool and essential sauna oils - the wellness factor in baths is so great because all senses are served, according to Meder.
And if the builders have made an effort, swimming pools can be a true treat: "Instead of a floor, you have a water surface in baths."
"On the one hand, it is transparent, but on the other hand reflects the beauty of the whole building."
Everything doubles visually: high vaults, colorful tiles, springboards.
The small-format picture book "Badefreuden" conveys interesting facts about the architectural and cultural history of historical wellness oases on 190 pages - but above all makes you want to jump into the blue.
Remarkable specimens were discovered by the pool collector Meder during her research in Hungary.
In addition to magnificent spas such as the 100-year-old Széchenyi bath in Budapest, she found a nitrate-containing body of water in a quaint karst cave (cave bath in Miskolc-Tapolca) as well as a thermal bath, whose pools are filled with alkaline water and are located in a bottle-shaped building (Városi Termálfürdö in Jászberény).
Spectacular views, on the other hand, offer many heated outdoor swimming pools in Switzerland and Austria: While you have the whole city in your eye from the Zurich roof pool, you can look out over the snow-covered Arlberg in the outdoor pool of a bathroom in St. Anton - and: In the steam bath there is a window from which you can watch the hustle and bustle on the ski slope.
Perhaps the most extraordinary pool design awaits bathers in Längenfeld: The Aquadome Ötztal looks like a UFO just landed in the Alps.
It steams from three shells open upwards in the winter, while in the background the mountains of the Ötztal protrude into the sky.
Alhambra in the Black Forest
Not in every swimming pool the pool collector Meder climbed the smell of chlorine into the nose.
Many thermal baths smell of sulphur, in the Árpád bath in Békescsaba in Hungary it stinks so much like heating oil, which is hardly believed to have landed in a health bath.
The smell comes from the alkaline-hydrocarbonate-containing medicinal water, which is almost black and whose silt sticks to your skin as soon as you slide into the pelvis - a wellness experience of a special kind.
At one place, however, Iris Meder was also disappointed in her quest for beautiful bathing spots: When she wanted to visit the outdoor and sunbathing in the Czech town of Bad Luhatschowitz, she found a blocked-off terrain.
Designed in 1902 by architect Du�áan Jurkovic, the bathroom has since been closed.
As Meder glanced through the weathered windows of the red-white-yellow Art Nouveau building, she saw weed rampant between the tiles.
On the other hand, one has counteracted the decay in an enchanting swimming pool in the Black Forest: the Palais Thermal in Bad Wildbad.
Three construction phases have already passed this bathroom; you can discover not only neo-Romanese elements, which date from its erection in 1844, but also a Moorish hall in Alhambra style, which was added around 1900.
Since the careful modernization in 1995, the Palais Thermal has been the favorite bath of the pool connoisseur Iris Meder - and not only because she originally comes from the Black Forest.
"It's already a feat to merge such different architectural styles into such a great unit," says Meder.
With such a palace, the dive in the thermal pool is apparently only a minor matter.
Agric Speculation
Deutsche Bank illuminates commodity business
By Christian Teevs
Is Deutsche Bank giving up its business with agricultural commodities?
According to SPIEGEL-ONLINE information, Josef Ackermann has commissioned a working group to examine the consequences of speculation for the world's poor.
In January, the CEO wants to draw consequences.
Hamburg -
For German citizens, the matter is clear: 84 percent of Germans, according to a study, find it not acceptable for banks to speculate with agricultural commodities such as wheat or maize.
Two-thirds of the respondents even demand that Deutsche Bank and other credit institutions get out of this business because it exacerbates the problems of the world’s poorest.
This is the result of a Forsa survey on behalf of the consumer organization Foodwatch.
The study is based on a report presented by Foodwatch in mid-October.
In it, author Harald Schumann shows that bets on the global commodity exchanges are driving up prices - and the banks are therefore complicated with the global hunger problem.
The campaign of consumer advocates is specifically directed against Deutsche Bank CEO Josef Ackermann, even if big banks such as Goldman Sachs or Mranglar Stanley act similarly.
Originally, commodity bets were intended to protect traders against price fluctuations.
But meanwhile, most experts agree that gamblers have decoupled from supply and demand - and make profits at the expense of the poorest.
Contrary than the U.S. competitors, Ackermann reacted to the criticism: He promised to examine the allegations and said no business was "valuable to jeopardize Deutsche Bank's good reputation."
According to SPIEGEL ONLINE, an international working group is currently examining the allegations from the Foodwatch report.
By the end of the year, the examination is to be completed, then the results will be presented to the Executive Board of Deutsche Bank - including a recommendation on how to deal with it.
At the end of January, the top of the bank then wants to announce the consequences she
In the end, there could be a reduction in the raw materials business, or even the complete departure.
Ackermann tinkers with his image
Foodwatch CEO Thilo Bode welcomed the news, but at the same reiterated his criticism of the financial industry: "Betting on rising prices of food shows particularly drastically how ruthless banks are damaging the common good today."
Ackermann, as the top banking lobbyist, is now particularly committed.
"Deutsche Bank needs to swipe all investments out of its portfolio where money is used for betting on food prices," Bode said.
"Whether Mr. Ackermann is interested in starvating people or the reputation of Deutsche Bank."
Ackermann’s insight comes as a surprise at first glance, but it is clear that the Group CEO would like to give a perfect picture a few months before leaving office a few months.
On Monday, Deutsche Bank announced that the CEO will not move to the supervisory board unlike planned.
With a departure from the controversial agricultural speculation, he could improve his image.
And in order to potential losses, he hardly needs to worry - after all, his successor would have to do with it.
One in two people would terminate raw material plants
Foodwatch strives to keep the pressure on the manager high.
So far, more than 30,000 Internet users have already participated in the organization's campaign.
On Tuesday, Bode released the survey, for which the Institut Forsa surveyed 1001 people on 7 and 8 November.
Just eleven percent think it is legitimate for Deutsche Bank to offer investments in which prices for fooding are wagered.
Many bank customers would also draw personal consequences if they learned that their bank participates in such transactions: according to the survey, one in two would terminate all investments where money flows into commodity speculation.
43 percent would discourage acquaintances from such a bank, 49 percent said they were thinking about canceling their account and switching to another institution.
21 house in Vienna
The relocated pavilion
By Ingeborg Wiensowski
An architectural history with highs, depths and happy endings: The Austrian world exhibition pavilion of Brussels in 19558 won prizes, became a museum in Vienna, stood empty and lost.
Now it is reopened as an exhibition house.
When raved about modern post-war architecture, of clear shapes, glass halls, courageous curves and new building materials that are typical of the belief in technical progress, then the Austrian pavilion of the architect Karl Schwanzer is an apt and famous example for the 1958 Brussels World Exhibition.
At that time Schwanzer's pavilion received the Grand Prix d'Architecture for its light and floating steel-glass construction.
To this day, construction remains a milestone in contemporary architecture.
He made the architect so well known that he also built Austria’s pavilion at the next world exhibition.
With his legendary BMW four-cylinder house plus bowl-like substructure in Munich, he finally became internationally famous in 1973.
His pavilion, planned as a temporary exhibition building, had been erected after the world exhibition in the Swiss garden at Vienna's Südbahnhof, rebuilt it and opened a museum of the 20th century in it in 1962 - it was called the "20er Haus".
However, the building was neither spatially and technically suitable as a museum - no walls for hanging the art, not a good climate.
Nevertheless, it was used until contemporary art received a new house in Vienna.
Since 2001, the building stood empty and came down more and more.
Now the architect Adolf Krischanitz has rebuilt the pavilion, it is now called "21er Haus" and will be used in the future as a museum for contemporary art.
On Tuesday evening, the Belvedere Museum, to which the house belongs, opens the first exhibition there - a stroke of luck in every way.
Agnes Husslein-Arco, who became the new director of the Austrian Gallery Belvedere in 2007.
Husslein-Arco, an art historian with a considerable career, among other things as a figure skater, later at Sotheby's, the Guggenheim Museum and as the founding director of the Museum der Moderne Salzburg, enforced the reconstruction strongly among politicians and preservationists, procured public funds and private sponsor funds and determined the use.
In addition to contemporary art, the works of the sculptor Fritz Wotruba were to be exhibited by the foundation of the same name, a café and a bookstore were to exist, and an office building had to be built.
Strict oversight, small budget
Krischanitz was then the ideal occupation for the implementation.
Not only because he had sensibly rebuilt and expanded Joseph Olbrich's Secession in Vienna and won the competition, but because he studied with Schwanzer at the Technical University of Vienna and the pavilion was an "eegly important place" for him, where he had looked at the exhibitions "almost every weekend."
He knew the original design, and he knew the problems of the building, which lost generosity and lightness even during his transfer to Vienna and his conversion.
There were far larger problems with the current conversion: The building was "an energy slingshot," says Krischanitz.
The window profiles of the glass façade were replaced, glass-fiber-reinforced insulating glass was used and the glass roof was replaced by special safety glass.
Refrigeration bridge steel beams have been reinforced or replaced, and asbestos-filled ceilings have been refurbished.
Two basements brought space for the new use, a lowered ditch of light between the house and the street ensures the exposure, a bridge leads to the entrance - almost a new building, but still the light tacker pavilion.
The original exterior doors have remained, the house is painted in the old red-brown rust protection shade.
And the cinema hall is even exactly as it used to be.
And all this under strict supervision of the monument authority and with a small budget.
"Often a material had to be found, which is good and cheap and also liked the monument protection office," says Krischanitz, pointing to a rough floor in the basement.
Despite all the constraints, problems and conversions, the building has remained flexible and easy.
It was difficult with the pavilion, "which came from Brussels", Krischanitz recently said, a typical "drop-in-the-city story".
Such an architecture "always stands in the wrong place" and still wants to "awaken a real awareness."
In this case, however, the 21er Haus is not in the wrong place, because it has occurred an urbatinal stroke of luck: Around the Swiss garden, the completely newly planned "main railway station district", with office and residential buildings, is currently being created.
And another stroke of luck will perhaps still occur: The 21er Haus could get reinforcements from another Krischanitz building, because the collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berlin Temporary Kunsthalle, and as you can hear, she is already negotiating with the city for a neighborly location in the Swiss garden.
Rail plans winter crisis package in the millions
The railway has devised a crisis plan that is intended to prevent train cancellations in winter.
The number of employees should be doubled.
It'll cost millions.
In the fight against winter-related train cancellations and delays, Deutsche Bahn spends more than 70 million euros this year, according to a newspaper report.
The "Bild" newspaper reported in advance, citing an intra-group crisis plan; by 2015, investments of a total of around 300 million euros had been planned.
This is intended to achieve better availability of vehicles and rail networks even in extreme weather conditions.
Among other things, a doubling of internal and external employees is planned for the removal of snow on railway facilities and platforms to 16,000 people.
The aim is to free platforms from snow and ice within four to five hours by the start of operation and 90 percent of the necessary operating switches.
Two months ago, railway boss Rüdiger Grube had warned of train cancellations and delays this winter after a top meeting with government and industry.
Last winter, the railway had considerable problems in passenger traffic, as it lacks reserves in difficult weather conditions, among others.
For example, numerous new regional trains have been waiting for approval by the Federal Railway Office for years.
There is also a delay in the delivery of ICE trains.
The railway has also been struggling for years that it has to check axles of ICE trains significantly more often in the workshop, after an ICE in Cologne's central station had an axle fracture.
Deutsche Bank pays multi-million-dollar fine in U.
After the tumult around boss Josef Ackermann comes new trouble from the U.S.: Deutsche Bank has to pay a million-dollar fine there.
As if the problems at home were not enough for Deutsche Bank, the industry giant is now also catching up with its past in the U.S.: In a comparison, the Frankfurt-based institute pays 145 million dollars (106 million euros) to settle disputes because of the bankruptcy of five large cooperative banks during the financial crisis.
It's about the sale of mortgage securities.
The financial market regulator NCUA accuses a number of large banks of enticing the cooperative banks with false promises to buy the financial products; they would have downplayed the risks.
Such papers lost great value during the financial crisis and swept the banks into the depths.
"We are satisfied that we were able to solve the issue without the parties having to go to court," a Deutsche Bank spokesman said in New York.
The bank admitted no guilt with the settlement.
This also applies to Citigroup, which committed itself to the payment of 20.5 million dollars.
NCUA chairwoman Debbie Matz welcomed the accommodation of the two money houses.
The National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) is responsible for U.S. co-operative banks and jumps in at bankruptcies to protect customer deposits.
The NCUA is trying to replace the billion-dollar damages incurred.
The now-closed comparisons are the first of their kind.
The regulator had also pre-buttoned other major banks and sued JPMorgan Chase, the Royal Bank of Scotland and Goldman Sachs in the summer.
Mortgage-backed securities in question are so-called mortgage-backed securities.
These are based on home loans.
When the U.S. real estate bubble burst in 2007, this became the doom of a variety of financial firms.
In September 2008, at the height of the financial crisis, U.S. investment bank Lehman Brothers overturned.
Even if the events date back a few years ago, a sharp wind is currently blowing towards the banks in the USA.
Investors and regulators have brought countless lawsuits to make amends or punishing misconduct.
The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) sparked the biggest wave of lawsuits.
It accuses 18 major international banks of over-advantaging the two state-owned U.S. real estate financiers Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac in mortgage transactions in the volume of around 200 billion dollars.
Deutsche Bank also has to face a lawsuit brought by the FHFA, which involves several transactions worth a total of 14.2 billion dollars from the years 2005 to 2007.
The supervisory authority demands that the people of Frankfurt stand for "substantial losses", but without mentioning an exact sum.
Deutsche Bank had rejected the claims as baseless and declared that it wanted to defend itself.
Warren Buffett joins IBM with 10 Billion
Finanzguru Buffett invests heavily in the IT company IBM.
Since March, he has bought shares worth more than ten billion dollars.
Until now, the large American investor Warren Buffett had always declared that he would not invest in IT and computer companies, since the long-term development of the companies could only be poorly predicted.
However, he has now read IBM’s annual reports and changed his opinion about investments in the industry.
He had to understand much earlier that IBM primarily offered services and procured computer systems for the IT departments of other companies.
An investment by Warren Buffett is considered a knighthood in the financial world.
As a result, the IT primordial IBM may now call itself "Sir IBM".
Because Buffett now revealed on U.S. economic broadcaster CNBC that he had bought 10.7 billion-dollar IBM shares since March.
His investment holding Berkshire Hathaway has thus risen to one of IBM's largest shareholders with a 5.5 per cent stake.
Buffett said that not even IBM has known anything about the commitment so far.
He praised the management, which, even in the economic crisis, had still ensured decent gains.
"They've done a great job," Buffett said of the strategy.
IBM has been relying on lucrative IT services such as the operation of data centers for quite some time.
The more than 100-year-old computer pioneer also offers software and consulting as well as high-performance company computers.
Buffetten had rather shunned technology companies to date.
He says he's only investing in corporations whose business he understands, too.
Instead, Buffett intervened, for example, on a freight railway, at a lubricant manufacturer or mechanical engineers.
In addition to around 80 its own subsidiaries, his holding company Berkshire Hathaway also owns shares in a number of large corporations such as Coca-Cola or Munich Re, the former Munich back.
The 81-year-old had taken over the small textile company Berkshire Hathaway in the 1960s and expanded it into one of the most valuable companies in the world with clever investments.
Nevertheless, his lifestyle is modest.
It is therefore a cult figure for countless investors all over the world.
His almost unmistakable sense of earning money has earned him the nickname "Oracle of Omaha."
Following the Buffett investment, IBM's stock rose one percent pre-market.
"Big Blue", as IBM is also known, is worth more than 220 billion dollars on the stock market and is one of the most valuable technology companies in the world together with Apple and Microsoft.
At Microsoft, Buffett said, however, he did not want to step in.
Founder Bill Gates is a close friend.
The threat is palpable
After the uncovering of the "Zwickauer Zelle", Turkish media doubt the German rule of law.
A newspaper even speaks of a "bloody ideology", which is now revived.
The murders of eight Turkish-born and a Greek small business owner in the years 2000 to 2006 had a right-wing extremist background.
This news is reacted with fear and concern in Turkey and within the German-Turkish population in Germany.
For many German-Turks, this returns a specter that has long since believed: since the arson attacks in Mölln in November 1992 and in Solingen in May 1993, there has been no comparable violence with a right-wing extremist background against Turks.
That there is xenophobia in Germany was nevertheless never in question.
However, the exposed right-wing extremist network reveals dimensions whose dimension is far from foreseeable.
"Is the bloody ideology revived?" headlined the Internet edition of the Turkish newspaper "Haberturk."
The newspaper "Sabah", on the other hand, emphasizes that one of the perpetrators is said to have been a V-man of the protection of the constitution, and is concerned that German authorities could also be involved in the right-wing swamp.
Prudently, the Turkish community of Germany reacted cautiously, recalling the victims of racist violence over the weekend with a vigil in front of the Brandenburg Gate.
Only two weeks earlier, the Turkish community in Berlin, together with German and Turkish politicians, had commemorated in a ceremony of the fiftieth anniversary of the German-Turkish recruitment agreement.
In cheerful colors, togetherness had been staged - as a story of success.
The coverage of some Turkish mediaries seems like a slap: "Germany's gift for the fiftieth" is the headline of an article in the Turkish Internet platform "Haber X", which refers to the background of the murder series.
Even the Germany edition of the "Hürriyet" does not hesitate to draw this mental connection.
"It starts to stink," is the title of Ahmet Külahci's column.
He emphasizes that people have been murdered who have paid taxes and made an important contribution to the reconstruction of Germany with their work.
In German-Turkish Internet forums, the action of the police and their later manhunt success is discussed very critically.
The authors wonder why the German authorities were not much more likely to look for suspects in right-wing extremist circles.
Some, at least rhetorically, are ready for drastic means: "If there is sloping here, the migrants have nothing but self-defense and armament".
"This will probably be the best self-protection."
"The goodness of enlightenment and adequacy of the punishment of the entire network depends very strongly on the inner peace in Germany," writes "Selen".
What it means when internal peace in Germany is in jeopardy were illustrated by the attacks by Mölln and Solingen in the 1990s.
The photo of Solingen's fire ruins is deeply rooted in German and Turkish remembrance.
Because no other event showed the Turks living here that there is a palpable threat, and to the Germans that distrust and distancing can lead to right-wing violence.
The attacks, however, sharpened the we-feelings on the Turkish side.
Don't repeat the mistakes of the past
In the Turkish press at that time, the failure of German politics after the attacks was criticized: Helmut Kohl refused to visit the survivors of Mölln.
After the arson attack by Solingen, he sent a condolence to the Turkish president, but was represented at the funeral ceremony in Cologne by his foreign minister Klaus Kinkel - Kinkel enumerated in his speech at that time how many levies the local Turks performed.
Just as if the value of a person according to the amount of their tax levies is calculated.
The current coverage of the Turkish media shows once again how deep the distrust of many German-Turks in the local rule of law has been since.
In addition to the efforts of an extensive enlightenment and reassessment of far-right terrorism in Germany, open words are needed this time.
The Scandalbank of Lake Zürisee
Europe's most adventurous bank does not sit in Italy or France, but in Switzerland.
UBS went almost bankrupt in the financial crisis and has been slipping from one crisis to the next ever since.
Now it's supposed to be judged by a German.
Axel Weber certainly hadn't imagined it that way.
After his spectacular departure from the head of the Bundesbank in July, it was announced that he would start an almost equally spectacular new job in 2013: Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Swiss big bank UBS - an unexpected and lucrative career step.
Axel Weber rejoiced publicly, praised Switzerland for its beauty and UBS because of its newed solidity and dropped friendly remarks about the future colleagues.
"UBS has made the turnaround."
"I am looking forward to working with Kaspar Villiger and Oswald Grübel."
But as early as September it became clear that everything would be very different.
A UBS trader in London had circumvented all of the bank's hedging systems and gambled around 1.7 billion euros.
UBS CEO Oswald Grübel, a banker who was not only praised in Switzerland, had to leave.
The Chairman of the Board of Directors of UBS has also been considered battered since then - and everyone asks: When will Axel Weber finally arrive?
It has already been announced that Weber will be able to deliberate the bank from February 2012.
And he will take over the chairmanship of the board of directors as early as May - not only in 2013, as the bank announced on Tuesday.
It's going to be tough for Axel Weber.
He is not a banker, rather an economics professor and central banker and would certainly have needed a training period.
With one blow, he then assumes responsibility for the strategy of a bank that has just proven once again that it has long since earned the title of "largest scandal bank in Europe".
UBS, on the one hand, is huge - until recently it has been the world's largest asset manager - and, on the other hand, has been slipping from one scandal to the next for years.
Nothing, it seems, will be spared the once-proud Swiss bank.
It began in the financial crisis.
Until 2007, UBS had an excellent reputation.
She had combined the image of the secretive and solid, but also exhausted Swiss asset manager with ambitious goals: she also wanted to become a very big number in investment banking.
UBS, on the one hand, managed 1.6 trillion dollars more wealthy customers than any other bank in the world in 2007.
And, on the other hand, as an investment bank, she had advanced into the league of the world's largest banks.
That made an impression.
The former banker and later long-standing banking professor in Zurich Hans Geiger says: "I have always portrayed UBS as a pattern of a good bank to my students at that time."
"From today's point of view, one wonders: how could I be so wrong?"
He was greatly mistaken.
In 2007 it became apparent for the first time that something went wrong at UBS.
In the middle of the year, CEO Peter Wuffli surprisingly resigned, who until then was considered a highly intellectual and equally ingenious banker.
A few months later, they knew why he had left.
The bank's investment division had gambled with sub-prime papers and had to write off billions.
In order to shoulder this, UBS announced a capital increase of 13 billion Swiss francs.
But she actually found investors, but those over whom some would have ruffled their noses until shortly before: a non-transparent Asian sovereign wealth fund from Singapore and an Arab sovereign wealth fund, which was so concealed that you didn’t even get your name.
But that was only the first blow.
Things got worse in April 2008.
The bank had to announce new billion-dollar losses, Chairman of the Board of Directors Marcel Ospel, in 2006 the second-best-checked board of directors in Switzerland, resigned.
In October 2008, the disaster followed: UBS, in view of its malinvestment in the financial crisis, was about to collapse and had to be rescued by the state
The National Bank bought UBS for tens of billions of problematic securities, the Swiss Confederation issued six billion Swiss francs for a forced convertible bond, through which it could become co-owner of the bank.
The whole of Switzerland was shattered - because it noticed how dramatic it is for a small country if it harbors such large banks.
After all, UBS's 2007 balance sheet total was more than five times higher than Switzerland's economic output (see chart).
"Again, we couldn't save UBS," says banking researcher Geiger.
The first disaster was followed by the second.
Just a few months later, UBS surrendered in the tax dispute with America and agreed to release the data of wealthy customers, who may have evaded taxes with the help of the bank.
In addition, she agreed to a payment of 780 million dollars - as a repayment of unjustified profits from these transactions, including a penalty surcharge.
In doing so, UBS more or less openly confessed to having helped with tax evasion.
At the same time, she lost something that had always been her capital in the competition for rich customers: the nimbus of secrecy.
After the scandal, Swiss banking secrecy was no longer the same.
This was followed by the resignation of CEO and Chairman of the Board of Directors
And the exodus of customer funds.
Thus, within a few months, the bank had lost its credibility in both central divisions: in investment banking due to incorrect speculations and in asset management through tax evasion assistance.
And just when things got a little quieter again, just when it was thought that UBS would get it done again, this unfortunate trader story follows: A single trader gambles 1.7 billion euros before the bank realizes that something is going wrong.
And it becomes obvious: With the risk management in investment banking, it obviously still does not work out.
"With the Adoboli case, it has been found that risk control has changed too little," says Martin Janssen, an economist and finance professor at the banking institute at the University of Zurich.
The Chief Financial Officer of a major German bank is also certain: "UBS's problem is that it does not have risk control under control."
But it had been precisely the idea of caution with which the Swiss bankers had long ago begun to become guardians of the world's great fortunes: not too great risks, but security for money - even beyond the Alpine republic the world might collapse.
The vaults of Swiss banks had become a symbol of unshakable reliability such as Lindt and Sprüngli for particularly noble chocolate.
The headquarters of UBS in Zurich still fits the cliché of the dignified Swiss private bank - it's just a little bigger.
Even the situation is classical.
The bank is headquartered on Züricher Bahnhofstraße, which leads directly to Lake Zurich, which is framed by the snow-capped peaks of the Swiss mountains.
The buildings here are ostentatious, the shop rents are among the highest in Europe.
Jewellers such as Tiffany's can be found here between traditional Swiss shops such as Confiserie Sprüngli.
Swiss banks have resided here for centuries: from the National Bank to Credit Suisse Palace to elitist private banks such as Julius Baer.
UBS's reception room is entered through a heavy revolving door.
Inside, the walls are marble-clapped, in front of them are thick leather armchairs, next to the bench logo a large sign to the "safe".
UBS knows what the customer is looking for here: safety.
This idea of the Swiss bank has both major Swiss banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, but eventually supplanted - or revealed how the boss of a small Swiss private bank finds.
He prefers not to read his name in the newspaper, after all, like all Swiss bankers, he also does business with the two major banks.
UBS has become far too big, he finds - especially in investment banking, where the Anglo-Saxons have also spread.
The Swiss would have nothing more to say there.
"And if they did, they've long since adapted to the customs of the Anglo-Saxons."
In fact, it was Anglo-Saxon investment banking that changed UBS, gave it high profits and then the debacle: no European financial institution has lost as much money in the subprime jungle as the Swiss big bank.
On October 15, 2008, the disaster became apparent.
"On that Wednesday, the governing coalition of the small seven-million country decided the largest bailout in their nation's history," author Lukas Hässig writes in his book "The UBS Crash."
Up to 68 billion Swiss francs, or around 45 billion euros, were approved by the politicians to save the major bank facing bankruptcy.
How could this have happened?
If you talk to Swiss bankers, they see the problem especially in the size of UBS.
In June 1998, two of Switzerland's three major banks merged into a formidable corporation.
A bank was created, which was already too big during the merger for small Switzerland - and continued to grow.
This helped the fact that the two different predecessor banks of UBS complemented each other: a rich but sleepy bank with a focus on asset management in Zurich and a poorer, but already more internationalized.
All of a sudden, the internationalized investment bankers came up cheap with a lot of money, with which they could spin a huge wheel.
Two names are linked to this rise of UBS: Peter Wuffli, CEO of UBS from 2001 to 2007, and Marcel Ospel, chairman of the board of directors at the time.
"I've always thought Wuffli and Ospel were a dream team," says banking researcher Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the highly intellectual, modest, who understands a lot about models and statistics, and Ospel, the belly person who has worked his way up from the banking doctrine to the very top."
But Ospel relied on Wuffli in the end.
And Wuffli probably made a decisive mistake in the end: "He thought his models were reality," Geiger says.
“It’s a tragic case.”
Tragic, maybe.
It is clear that arrogance came before the fall.
Employees of UBS clearly remember the management meetings on which Group CEO Wuffli repeatedly issued the same parole: Goldman Sachs pushed off the throne.
The American bank was Earth's most powerful investment bank.
She cited the "League Tables," the rankings that documented the success of the banks of that time.
That's where UBS wanted to get to the top.
A few steps had already been successful for her.
In the foreign exchange business and in stock trading, she played at the top.
However, management consultants who have been hired emphasized a weakness: in the international interest rate business, in the "Fixed Income" banking language, there is a huge distance from the largest banks.
UBS has gaps in American mortgage products.
To close this gap, UBS embarked on an adventure that almost cost them existence.
Hungry for growth, both the bank itself and a hedge fund founded before it invested in American subprime investments.
It developed into a formidable machine that packaged and forwarded securities to American mortgage loans - but also retained risks itself.
Even when other banks were already looking to pull out.
"Even in the summer of 2007, when the American real estate market was on fire, UBS bought there," says banking expert Geiger.
That's how much euphoria, so deeply the case.
One manager after another had to leave when the attempts to clean up were more difficult than expected.
Posts at UBS became a slingshot seat and are still shaky to this day.
Whoever runs the big bank now has an almost impossible job.
He needs to find a strategy for a bank that has lost all the obvious business models.
Banking researcher Geiger already has an idemant: Stop investment banking, focus on asset management, return to Swiss roots.
The bank doesn’t want to (yet) go that far.
But investment bankinging is expected to shrink sharply, that much is already clear.
New bank CEO Sergio Ermotti will present his future strategy to the world next Thursday.
The 51-year-old Italo-Swissman was actually intended only as an interim solution, when the bank surprisingly separated itself from predecessor Grübel.
Meanwhile, he has prevailed: He is to lead the business of UBS permanently, the Zurich bank announced on Tuesday.
Ermotti wants the investment bank to return to the mid-nineties level.
He cuts 3500 jobs.
But that won't be enough.
UBS needs a vision.
Where's her?
How does she want to make money?
These are the questions that Axel Weber certainly already asks.
So far publicly, he only says that he will look for an apartment in Zurich with his wife.
next year.
Fear of recession in Europe weighs on U.S. exchange
The looming recession in Europe has taken the courage of US investors at the start of the week.
The change of government in Athens and Rome remained without clout.
Throttled industrial production on the continent nipped hopes for a stabilisation of the debt crisis in the bud.
The general relief about the change of government in Rome and Athens could not prevail.
The euro fell against the dollar.
Italian and Greek glass were considered half-full in the past week, not half empty - but now the problems have come to the foreground again, said Mark Luschini of Janney Montgomery Scott.
Financial stocks in particular were on the sales lists.
The Dow-Jones index of defaults notched down 0.6 per cent at 12,078 points at the close of trading.
In the course of trade, the market barometer commuted between 12,027 and 12,170 points.
The broader S&P-500 index closed at 1251 points, a minus of one percent.
The index of technology exchange Nasdaq lost 0.8 percent and went out of trading at 2657 points.
In Frankfurt, the Dax bid farewell with a minus of 1.2 percent at 5985 points.
The industry in the 17 euro countries has significantly reduced its production and adjusts to the end of the upturn.
The companies produced two percent less in September than in the previous month, according to the statistics office Eurostat.
"We're not an island, we're dependent," Steve Goldman of Goldman Management said.
Europe will not be able to free itself from a recession so quickly, and there are further signs of weakness in the banks.
Investment legend Warren Buffett only confirmed this caution: it is not yet clear whether Europe is strong enough to do anything for an end to the crisis, he said in a CNBC interview.
Therefore, in his view, it is too early to invest in European government bonds or banks.
Instead, Buffett overthrew a decades-old principle and entered the U.S. technology industry.
His investment of more than ten billion dollars in IBM propped up the share price of the traditional group and initially listed it almost one percent higher against the trend in the course of trading.
The paper closed almost unchanged at 187.35 dollars.
Buffett has paid an average of 170 dollars per piece and now holds 5.5 percent.
The share price of his investment firm Berkshire Hathaway lost 1.3 percent.
Shares from Bank of Ameria declined sharply more sharply than the market at 2.6 percent.
The house silvers almost the rest of its shares in China Construction Bank and collects 6.6 billion dollars for it.
The institute intends to cushion its capital ratio above all with the net profit from the business and thus meet tougher regulatory requirements.
The aircraft manufacturer Boeing increased and thus limited the losses in the Dow.
The stock gained 1.5 percent after the group garnered the largest order in its history.
Airline Emirates ordered 50 long-haul 777 aircraft on Sunday and put the value of the order at 18 billion dollars.
Including options to 20 more machines, the volume even amounts to 26 billion dollars.
On the New York Stock Exchange, about 710 million shares changed hands.
719 values increased, 2281 declined and 79 remained unchanged.
On Nasdaq, sales of 1.38 billion shares closed 630 in the plus, 1913 in the minus and 82 unchanged.
Smart crows are spreading in cities
In many places, seed crows are regarded as "problem birds": But the highly intelligent animals can no longer be expelled from the cities, experts claim.
Some like them, others want to get rid of them very quickly: crows have been part of the cityscape for centuries, but in many communes, residents’ complaints about dirt and noise of the animals accumulate.
A remedy against the "problem birds" is not yet in sight.
Seed crows aren't available from the cities, experts said at the first crowing symposium in East Frisian Leer.
There, experts from all over Germany discussed possible ways out.
Are you looking for alternatives in cities
Conservationists also attributed the problems in cities to the rural exodus of the particularly protected seed crows: the use of chemicals in agriculture increasingly destroys the traditional habitats.
The hunting, increasing development and the felling of typical brooding trees made life difficult for the birds.
Crows are under nature protection.
Large colonies with more than 600 breeding pairs exist, among other things, in the Westphalian Soest, in the north-west, among others in Jever, Diepholz and Achim near Bremen as well as Leer.
In 2005, a controversial project for the mass killing of crows in the district caused negative headlines there.
Critics had described the catching of the animals in bird traps and the slaying with clubs as particularly brutal.
"Normal expulsion, burying, is of nothiness," city and landscape planner Werner Klöver from Leer has noted.
Splashing nests with water or cutting down trees often has undesirable consequences, said the environmental expert Manfred Kaiser from Lahr in Baden-Württemberg.
"The animals dodge, large colonies split up and spread elsewhere."
Distortion is only conceivable as an exception at hospitals, nursing homes or schools.
"We have to live with them, we can't get them away anymore."
Without the surrounding area, the problem in the cities cannot be solved, said Mayor Wolfgang Kellner from Leer.
A combination of digestion of the animals from sensitive living areas and locking offers is conceivable in order to resettle them elsewhere.
"Information boards instead of chainsaws", on the other hand, the psychologist and ethnologist Uta Maria Jürgens advocated for a completely different handling of the animals: "After all, we can endure a piggle on the face best self-confident."
Jürgens had co-founded the Ascheberg crow's educational trail in Schleswig-Holstein, on which signs inform about the lives of the highly intelligent seed crowns.
The project has already been accepted by tourists.
Jürgens also had his own opinion on criticizing the noise of the birds: "There's a lot of communication, they have a lot to tell each other."
The intelligence and skill of the animals are legendary: seed crows not only use aids to get a treat, but even produce their own tools as aids.
Behavioural scientists at the University of Cambridge recently tested on four seed crows how far the intelligence of the birds extends.
In an experiment, the birds quickly learned that they had to collapse scaffolding with stones in order to get to a moth larva.
Without a lot of training, they single-mindedly realized what size and shape a stone must have in order to collapse the platform.
In a second attempt, the clever crows then had to solve a trickier problem.
In a vertically positioned cylinder, the researchers had placed a small bucket with a moth larva.
With the beak, the birds could not reach their prey.
However, the researchers had put wire pieces on them.
From this, all four animals formed a hook, with the help of which they then fished the bucket on the handle from the tube.
Three of the four seed crows even managed this immediately.
Cristiano Ronaldo blasts Portugal to Euro 2012
The hopes of the Bosnians and the Turks for a coup were lacking.
The favourites Portugal, Croatia, the Czech Republic and also Ireland will be present at the European Championship 2012.
Portugal, the Czech Republic, Croatia and Ireland have released the last tickets to the 2012 European Championship in Poland and Ukraine in the play-offs.
Led by the double-successful superstar Cristiano Ronaldo (8th, 53rd), Portugal beat Bosnia-Herzegovina 6:2 (2:1) and, after the 0:0 from the first leg, made the fifth European Championship participation in a row perfect.
In the play-off rematch with outsider Montenegro, the Czechs scored a 1:0 (0:0), after the first leg (2:0) had already been a clear thing.
Croatia didn't let anything else burn in the 0:0 against Turkey, Ireland took a loose 1:1 (1:0) against Estonia.
Nani (24.), Helder Postiga (72., 82.) and Miguel Veloso (80.) scored the other goals for Portugal.
Former Wolfsburg Bundesliga professional Zvjezdan Misimovic with a converted trade metre (41st) and captain Emir Spahic (65th) had twice shortened for Bosnia.
Sena Lulic saw the yellow-red card (54).
Bosnia had already failed Portugal in the play-offs for the 2010 World Cup in South Africa.
At the Estadio da Luz in Lisbon, Portugal got a tailor-made start.
Ronaldo chased a free-kick from around 30 yards to the early lead into the stitches.
A good quarter of an hour later Nani showed his long-shot qualities and scored from 25 meters to 2:0.
The Bosnians, who didn't take place in the first half-hour, had their first clear chance by goal-scorer Edin Dzeko.
The ex-Wolfsburger from the English league leader Manchester City put a header to the lower edge of the bar, but the ball did not cross the goal line (33.).
Jiracek scores for Czech Republic
Subsequently, referee Wolfgang Stark from Ergolding took centre stage.
After an attack against Helder Postiga in the Bosnian penalty area, Stark decided on Schwalbe and showed the Portuguese the yellow card (36.).
On the other hand, Stark gave up a controversial trade-meter after an action by Fabio Coentrao, which Misimovic safely converted.
But after the break, the runner-up champion from 2004 made everything clear with four hits.
For the Czech Republic, Petr Jiracek also ended the last doubts in the 81st minute.
Thus, for the fifth time in a row, the European champion from 1976 goes to the European Championships.
The team of coach Michal Bilek with the Bundesliga legionnaires Michael Kadlec (Bayer Leverkusen) and Tomas Pekhart (1. FC Nuremberg), however, was also lucky and had to survive in the initial phase for a scare second.
Closing man Petr Cech prevented with a reflex at the last moment a possible own goal by Tomas Sivok (9).
Montenegro had its greatest opportunities when duo Stevan Jovetic and Dejan Damjanovic were in action.
Damjanovic only narrowly shot past Cech's goal in the 40th minute from the spin from the penalty area boundary.
After the break, the crossbar had to save for the already beaten world-class goalkeeper of the Czechs, again the striker of FC Seoul had withdrawn (50.).
The goal shortly before the end finally made everything clear.
Turkey, meanwhile, missed Zagreb's football miracle.
Four days after the 0:3 in Istanbul, the Turks in Zagreb did not get beyond a 0:0.
While national coach Guus Hiddink almost certainly has to vacate his place on the bench of the World Cup third from 2002, the Croatians can look forward to their fourth participation in a European Championship final.
In front of 31,000 spectators at Maksimir Stadium, the Turks almost caught a dream start.
Selcuk Inan only scored the post in the seventh minute with a distance shot, attacker Kazim Kazim also couldn't sink the bouncer in goal.
After the scare second, however, Croatia took more and more control.
Even after the break, the Croatians didn't let anything burn.
In addition to Mario Mandzukic from VfL Wolfsburg, who made room for Dortmund's Ivan Perisic from the 62nd minute, Bundesliga legionnaires Danijel Pranjic (Bayern Munich) and Gordon Schildenfeld (Eintracht Frankfurt) were still in action.
Trapattoni goes to the European Championships
In Dublin, the Irish celebrated their first European Championship participation in Germany since 1988.
Stephen Ward put the squad of coach Giovanni Trapattoni, who had already made a pre-decision with the 4:0 success in the first leg, into the lead in the 32nd minute.
Konstantin Vassiljev (57.) equalized for the guests.
Ireland pressed on the pace from the start, but had to wait until the 23rd minute before Robbie Keane of Los Angeles Galaxy had the first good chance.
Only at the last moment did the Estonian back team prevent the possible leadership goal.
But in the 32nd minute it was time.
After an accidental defense from closing man Pavel Londak, Ward was on hand and pushed the ball over the line at 1:0.
Woman critically injured in crash in tram
In a fall in a tram, a 52-year-old woman was life-threateningly injured on Monday in Berlin-Lichtenberg.
She had to have emergency surgery in a hospital, police said on Tuesday.
The tram had to brake abruptly in Herzbergallee when a vehicle suddenly stopped in front of her.
The car then turned and drove away, after the driver is searched.
The other occupants of the tram were uninjured.
Armed man raids Friedenau pharmacy
An armed man ambushed a pharmacy in Bundesallee in Berlin-Friedenau on Monday evening.
He threatened a 25-year-old employee with a firearm and demanded the surrender of cash, the police said.
After the cash drawer had been handed to him with change, the robber fled unrecognized with his prey at unknown heights.
The robber came away with the horror.
Drunken motorist seriously injured
A 21-year-old drunk driver was seriously injured early Tuesday morning in the Märkische Allee in Berlin-Marzahn.
He suddenly tried to swerve from a gas station onto the road truck with attached construction trucks.
As a result, he skidded his vehicle, drove over the middle strip and the oncoming carriageway and bounced against several trees.
The 21-year-old arrived at the hospital.
The 40-year-old truck driver was uninjured.
Cyclist hit and seriously injured
A 61-year-old cyclist was hit by a car and seriously injured on Monday in Berlin-Mitte.
She had wanted to cross the road at a pedestrian crossing at the intersection Karl-Liebknecht-Straße/Alexanderstraße with her bicycle, the police announced on Tuesday.
She captured a 72-year-old motorist with his vehicle while turning.
The woman crashed and was seriously injured for inpatient treatment to a hospital.
On the run: Car thief becomes ghost driver
A man was traveling with a stolen car in Charlottenburg at night.
While turning, he rammed a parked vehicle, pushed it together with two other cars and three wheels.
Police began the persecution.
On the run, the perpetrator raced onto the highway.
Contrary to the driving direction!
He scrammed a concrete plank, lost vehicle parts.
The car stopped and the man fled on foot.
Pedestrian shot from balcony with air rifle
A pedestrian has been bombarded in Schwedt from a balcony with an air rifle.
The 32-year-old was traveling near her apartment building on Sunday when she was hit in the back by the shot, police said.
She had to be taken to the hospital with a laceration.
Police subsequently investigated four suspects, of whom a 19-year-old is considered the main accused.
There is no connection between the woman and the perpetrators, according to a police spokesman.
25-year-old seriously injured in car accident
During the collision with an oncoming truck, a motorist was seriously injured in the district of Potsdam-Mittelmark.
The 25-year-old had come off the road on Monday morning on a federal road near Wiesenburg with his van from previously unexplained cause and slipped into a guardrail, the police said.
Due to the force of the impact, the car was hurled onto the oncoming carriageway and collided with the truck there.
The 25-year-old had to be freed from his vehicle by the fire
He was taken to a hospital by rescue helicopter.
Fire in cinema at Alexanderplatz
At Kino Cubix am Alexanderplatz there was a fire on Monday night.
Shortly after midnight, about 70 visitors to the late performance had to leave the cinema in Rathausstraße, the police said.
No one was injured.
According to previous findings, there was probably a fire in the electricity room due to a technical defect.
At the same time, the electricity fell out, and there was heavy smoke.
Woman seriously injured in attempted handbag robbery
One woman has been seriously injured in an attempted handbag robbery in Niedergörsdorf in Teltow-Fläming district.
The hooded perpetrator had surprised the woman on Friday in a parking lot in front of a shop and grabbed her handbag, police said.
When the victim resisted, he slapped her hands and her head with a stick.
He then fled without prey.
The woman was admitted to hospital with serious injuries.
Cemetery visitors robbed during grave care
In recent weeks, older people have been robbed at a cemetery in Fürstenwalde.
In the total of twelve thefts since mid-October, handbags, cash, mobile phones, money cards or keychains have been stolen, the police said.
The victims, according to the information, are mostly older women who cared for their relative's tombs.
They had parked their bicycles or rollators with the valuables nearby.
The last theft of cash in the amount of several hundred euros occurred last Friday.
Six graffiti sprayers arrested
Six graffiti sprayers have gone online.
The six teenagers were arrested in Marzahn on Sunday, police said.
The 15 to 25-year-old men had sprayed a railway wagon at Hohenschönhausen-Grenzgraben.
They had climbed onto the car with a ladder and smashed a window.
One witness alerted the police.
When plainclothes officers appeared, the perpetrators initially fled.
A little later, however, they were arrested at a stop in Landsberger Allee.
Wheelchairs captured by car and seriously injured
A wheelchair user was seriously injured while crossing a road in Schwedt.
The man wanted to cross a street at a pedestrian crossing on Saturday and was overlooked by a female motorist, the police said.
The vehicle captured the wheelchair user, who fell onto the road and injured his head.
He had to be taken to a hospital for inpatient treatment.
Seven-year-old hit and injured at Wedding
A seven-year-old boy was hit by a car and seriously injured on Sunday evening in Tegeler Straße in Berlin-Wedding.
He came to a hospital with facial and leg injuries, the police announced on Monday.
The child suddenly ran into the street in front of the car of a 53-year-old woman.
She could no longer brake her vehicle in time and captured him with the car.
The boy wasn't accompanied by adults.
Cyclist seriously injured in Kreuzberg
A 46-year-old cyclist was hit by a car and seriously injured late on Sunday evening in Gneisenaustraße in Berlin-Kreuzberg.
He came to a hospital with head injuries and was unresponsive, police said on Monday.
How it came to the collision with the vehicle of a 22-year-old man is still unexplained.
Car burnt out in Köpenick
A car burned out early Monday morning in Dregerhoffstraße in Berlin-Köpenick.
A 34-year-old man was still trying to extinguish, a police spokeswoman said.
But he did not succeed, so that the fire brigade had to bring the fire under control.
The vehicle is supposed to be an older model.
Arson is suspected, but without a political motive.
Driver's escape after accident in Lichtenberg
After a serious accident in Lichtenberg, the driver fled in the night to Sunday without taking care of his injured companion.
According to the police, the man with his car in the Möllendorfstraße, crashed against a mast of the tram and landed in the track bed.
A tram driver was still able to brake in time and saw the driver flee.
The alarmed fire department had to free a seriously injured 23-year-old from the car wreck and take him to a hospital.
The owner of the vehicle later stated that her car had been stolen shortly before.
Fire in Tempelhofer Keller
In a house in Tempelhof, a cellar cradle burned in the night to Sunday.
Tenants of the house in Marienfelder Allee had noticed smoke in the stairwell and alerted the fire brigade, the police said.
At first, an 18-year-old who was conspicuously interested in the extinguishing work had been arrested, it said.
However, the suspicion could not be confirmed, so he was released again.
Injured in brawl in fast food restaurant
In a brawl in Pankow, two guests of a fast food restaurant were injured in the night to Sunday.
According to the police, four men between the ages of 18 and 21 were initially in dispute with two vendors of the restaurant in the Prenzlauer Promenade at around 1.20 a.m.
Two 19-year-olds wanted to help, whereupon they were beaten and kicked abruptly by the quartet.
Both suffered head injuries.
The four attackers could still be arrested at the restaurant.
Courageous helper attacked on subway station - perpetrators arrested
A courageous helper jumped aside beleaguered girls at Gesundbrunnen subway station and conceded beatings.
Three rowdys attacked the 41-year-old man on Saturday and also spray-pained pepper spray in the face after blows with fists.
Then the trio fled.
According to the police, a 19-year-old could be arrested a little later on suspicion.
A passer-by had alerted the police by mobile phone.
Officers found the pepper spray in a recycle bin near the subway station.
The aide came to a hospital for outpatient treatment.
Excavation pit in embassy district caught on fire
A fire broke out in an excavation pit in the embassy district in Tiergarten on Saturday.
According to the police, building material ignited around 12.00 a.m. during welding work in Tiergartenstraße.
As a result, a large plume of smoke was created, which could also be seen well from a distance.
The fire brigade approached with four squadrons and was able to bring the blaze under control quickly.
The fire also affected a district heating and a power line.
In addition, the statics of the excavation pit must now be checked, said a fire department spokesman.
People were not injured in the fire.
During the extinguishing work, Tiergartenstraße had to be closed to car and bus traffic.
40-year-old seriously injured in robbery
Unknown persons attacked a 40-year-old on Friday evening in Mitte and seriously injured.
According to the police on Saturday, the man was on his way to Martin-Opitz-Strasse at about 23.30, when he was initially approached by the three perpetrators and asked about the time.
Subsequently, the men followed him, beat and then suddenly stepped on her victim and fled with his purse and mobile phone.
Police officers found the seriously injured man lying in the street and alerted the emergency room doctor.
The man had to be taken to a hospital.
Father attacks tram driver - child had to watch
In front of his four-year-old son, a drunk father insulted a female tram driver on Friday night and subsequently beaten.
According to the police on Saturday, the 49-year-old BVG employee had to stop in the Seestraße in Wedding, when she was suddenly attacked by the 30-year-old.
The man punched her in the face while the child had to watch.
Police officers briefly detained the drunk, the boy had to be picked up by the grandfather.
The tram driver was slightly injured.
Garbage container set on fire at Wedding
Unknown persons set fire to dumpsters in a backyard in Wedding during the night of Saturday.
According to the police, a resident noticed the fire around midnight and at the same time observed how two dark-clothed men fled over the adjacent cemetery wall in Steegerstraße.
The alarmed fire brigade was able to extinguish the
Hungriger Labrador turns on stovetop - Apartment fire
A hungry dog is believed to have sparked an apartment fire in Brandenburg/Havel.
The Labrador probably jumped on the stove to get food, the police announced on Friday.
He probably moved the switch and turned on a plate.
Paper on the stove caught fire.
The apartment on the fourth floor of an apartment building was on fire on Thursday afternoon.
The dog died of flue gas poisoning.
The 18-year-old tenant, who was not in the apartment during the blaze, is being investigated for negligent arson.
"Twilight" world premiere in Hollywood with a big star line-up
Flashlight thunderstorms, screeching fans, radiant "Twilight" stars: At the world premiere of "Breaking Dawn - Bis (s) at the end of night 1," thousands of onlookers had gathered on the black-colored rug in front of the Nokia Theatre in Los Angeles.
For several hours, the march of the stars lasted.
The vampire saga fans weren't disappointed: vampire performer Robert Pattinson, his werewolf rival Taylor Lautner and "Bella" beauty Kristen Stewart gave autographs and posed with fans.
In the fourth part Bella is finally allowed to marry her great love.
SAP aims to double number of employees in China
Europe's largest software maker SAP is investing billions in China over the next few years and wants to almost double the number of its employees in the country.
By 2015, about two billion dollars (around 1.5 billion euros) are to flow, the Dax Group said on Tuesday.
"We will create additional research and development facilities and hire employees accordingly," co-chief executive Bill McDermott announced at a customer fair in Beijing.
The workforce is expected to grow from currently about 2500 to 4500 employees.
The number of branches of the world market leader for enterprise software will double from five to ten or eleven.
So far, SAP is present in Beijing, Shanghai, Guanzhou, Chengdu and Dalian.
Approximately 4000 of the world's more than 176 000 SAP customers are based in Boomland.
The growth plans in China are also an important component of the Dax Group’s 2015 strategy.
SAP intends to increase sales to 20 billion euros by 2015.
Last year, Walldorfer had redeemed around 12.5 billion euros.
Currently, the stores of the software provider, which employs 55 000 people worldwide, are doing as well as rarely before.
SAP has been active in China for 20 years.
"Now we'd like to expand our activities (...)," McDermott explained.
"Our goal is to promote sustainable growth in China through comprehensive education."
Mass Slaughtering on a Personal Level
Sensitive and magnanimous begins Peter Englund's new book "Beauty and Scarry. A Story of the First World War, told in nineteen fates" with a long list of main characters who goes to your heart.
Here’s a tip on how to proceed: Carefully cut out this side and make it your bookmark.
You’ll get to know these people very well in Englund’s novelous narrative. And these main individuals will function as your GPS, as a lighthouse in those few moments when, like Englund’s, you’re lost men and women in the fog of war and deprived of your illusions.
Peter Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also the new permanent secretary of the Swedish academy, which determines the Nobel Prize for literature.
He has written an unusual book that he does not incorrectly describe as "a work of anti-history."
It contains few big names, important treaties, or famous battles. There are hardly any ambassadors, frenzied reporters or discussions about tactics and equipment.
It's less a book about what happened, he explains, but rather "a book about how it was." It's about "feelings, impressions, experiences and moods."
"Beauty and horror" almost the war experiences of 20 more or less inconspicuous men and women together on both sides of the war, from schoolgirls and botanists to mountaineers, doctors, ambulancers and officers.
Some of these people become heroes.
Some become prisoners of war, lose limbs, go crazy or die.
Each of these lives flickers like the votive candles in a church, some are added, while others extinguish.
Englund's book deviates from the usual history books.
It also corrects the idea that the First World War was only the devastating war of position on the Western Front.
"Beauty and horror" wanders with an expert's view over other theatres of war: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa.
On the soldiers in this book fall beehives, one celebrates Christmas under the pyramids in Egypt, tsette flies are an unsolvable problem.
This is almost from the beginning a moving book.
The war floods the lives of these people like a natural disaster, like a hurricane Katrina, who smells like Kordit.
Imagine you’re a woman who’s at home with her kids and hears cannon fires in the distance. Do you stay, or flee?
Who's coming anyway?
Hardly anyone understands what's happening or why this war is being waged in the first place.
"Missing facts," Englund notes, "were refurbished with conjectures, assumptions, hopes, fears, fixed ideas, conspiracy theories, dreams, nightmares and rumours."
"Beauty and horror" is followed by individual persons such as Florence Farmborough, an English nurse in the Russian army, and Richard Stumpf, a young German ship sailor.
Her stories were mainly taken from memoirs, letters and other material that had already been published.
Their accounts of life can be frightening or poignant, but they are extremely lively in England’s collection of small moments losing in detail.
Many of them are dealing with deficiency andrift
In the story of a German schoolgirl, he recounts: "Replacement, replacements everywhere."
"Replacement coffee, false aluminum, false eraser, paper dressings, wooden knobs."
He tells more about the replacement dinner in Germany.
He describes "meat made from pressed rice and baked in mutton fat (and most recently served with a false bone of wood), tobacco made from dried roots and dried potato peels, shoes sooled with wood."
There were, as he notes, "837 registered food substitutes that were allowed in the production of sausages, 511 registered coffee substitutes."
Some stories are about honor and bravery.
One American recognizes his own urge for saviousness and says of the war: "You feel that despite everything that was intended for men rather than to sit in the appendix, with the evening sheet or the bestseller and a cigarette and a whisky in his hand, and to pretend that such a façade means civilization and as if there were no barbarism behind one's own strengthened and fogged shirt chest."
Conversely, a British soldier realizes that he is likely to die and that no one will take notice or take care of it.
"When one refrains from the thought of sacrificing oneself, one wants to think that this happens in front of an audience," he writes.
Instead, it's as terrible as "a convicted man who has been hanged in secret."
Other observations are about the old Europe falling apart, and about new types of terror.
"The conflict increasingly became an economic competition," writes Englund, "a war between factories."
He captures the arrival of what he calls "a new species in the bestiary of the still-young century": "the word-wise and ideally convinced mass-suspector who slaughters as he sits behind a desk."
People behave in an unforeseen manner and there is just as frivolous behavior as heroism.
Englund discusses the soldiers who were actively trying to get infected in a prostitute with a STD to escape the frontline service.
"The grotesque expression of this can be discovered in trade with gonococcal pus, because soldiers bought and smeared on their genitals, hoping to get to the hospital like this," he writes.
“Those who were really desperate rubbed him into his eyes, causing them to be blind for the rest of their lives.”
In this translation from the Swedish by Peter Graves, Englund’s poetry becomes supple yet unobtrusive and adapts perfectly to his human task.
In dozens of small scenes, he captures the way the wary "unleashed uncontrollable forces in a unique way: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred."
People are starting to wonder why their leaders get them fighting.
The best books about the First World War were mostly rather distorted, such as Paul Fussell's "Great War and Modern Memory" or novels such as Erich Maria Remarque's "In the West nothing new" than extensive history books.
Englund's band joins an unconventional hall of fame.
His book has the most devastating ending I've ever read in a non-fiction book.
I don't give it here anymore.
But it's as if he snatched this page away from us with his book with the main characters in front of his nose and lit it with a match.
After an early misstep a meticulously planned assault
Hundreds of police officers were involved, some of whom wore operational helmets.
One had decided for the night from Monday to Tuesday, because at that time there are hardly any people in Zucotti Park.
The operation was kept secret and few senior officials were informed. All the others initially believed to participate in an exercise.
Police Commissioner Raymond W. Kelly was in the midst of them and his presence underlined the fact that the operation was full of challenges.
The incidents of the past few weeks, such as the onset of pepper spray against protesters, which violates the rules of the department and gave the squatters a huge increase in public sympathies, were no longer allowed to repeat themselves.
And so the police action to remove the protesters from Zuccotti Park was carried out after two weeks of planning and training.
Officials had prepared these by observing how occupations were handled in other cities.
A disaster exercise had taken place with a view of Zuccotti on Randalls Island.
Officials stepped up so-called chaos training - counter-terrorism measures, which also includes the rapid relocation of a large number of police officers - to focus on Lower Manhattan.
The last training session took place Monday night on the Manhattan side of the East River.
The order to move into the park came "at the last minute," said someone familiar with the commands what a declaration as an "exercise."
"The few policemen who, to my knowledge, didn't knew it was about Zuccotti Park," said someone who wishes to remain anonymous.
"The only ones who knew it was about Zuccotti Park were the department's senior officials."
One reason for secrecy was a lesson the city had learned.
On October 14, officials wanted to vacate the park, but then swerved back when hundreds of protesters came together after they learned of these plans.
Officers from various police units were involved in Tuesday's operation, including operational squads from across the city - hundreds of mobile officers normally deployed in highly criminal neighborhoods.
Mr Kelly said that over the course of the day, many people go in and out of the park, almost like commuters, so 1 o'clock at night is a good time to go inside.
"It was appropriate to do this when the least number of people are staying in the park," he said.
Ambulances with Jupiter lights and loudspeakers stood ready before departure at Pike Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive near Manhattan Bridge.
The lamps and the pre-recorded messages roaring from the loudspeakers seemed to intimidate many protesters.
As public officials entered the park in her light-blue windbreakers, many protesters simply swept up their belongings and left the place.
Police said no tents would be touched by 1:45 p.m. to give protesters time to pack their belongings together.
Other teams of officials were seen gathering in the area to intervene should arrests in the park become necessary.
Reporters were forced to leave the park.
Paul J. Browne, spokesman for the police commissioner, said this had happened for their own safety.
But many journalists claim that they wanted to stop them from observing the police actions in the park. And they said that the handling of the officers had been very rough.
Mr Browne said television cars on Church Street on the western side of the park could have taken photos.
As the police moved to the west through the great clutter of personal belongings of the protesters, including suitcases, artificial turf and bags with clothes, she was followed by employees of city cleaning, who picked up what had been left behind.
Some protesters, who refused to leave, were towed outside. Pictures of this appeared on the Internet a short time later.
A main core of protesters moved near the kitchen area of the camp in the center of the park position.
Some erected barriers and the police continued their methodological arrests.
Approximately 10 people in the epicenter of the camp tied together by their arms.
And two people chained themselves to trees, Mr Kelly said.
Civil protection officials were summoned to cut them loose.
As Mr Kelly said, no arrests were carried out in the park until about 3:30 a.m.
Eviction was completed after 75 minutes, according to the police.
Mr Browne said 142 people were arrested in the park.
Most of the arrests occurred, he said, due to "undue behavior and resistance to arrest."
The most prominent official on the ground was Joseph J. Esposito, the department's chief, the department's tallest uniformed official.
Phil T. Pulaski, head of department of the detectives, was also there.
Despite his presence, Mr Kelly "didn't direct the operation," as Mr Browne said.
In the event that they were supposed to access, a second group of officials waited on the eastern side of Broadway between Liberty and Cedar Street.
Barricades have been erected on Cortlandt Street, a street block north of the park, and in Pine Street, a block further south.
It was possible to observe how, from these locations, the police continued to expand the period around Zuccotti Park.
In one place, it was observed how several officers, many of them with protective shields, drove people outside.
Approximately 28 people were in the northern area.
Some of the night's most violent actions took place in the south of the park.
Around 5 p.m., a protester jumped onto the roof of a police vehicle south of Pine Street and other demonstrators let the air out of the tire of a police bus.
In one place, the crowd threw a piece of plywood.
In the end, an officer and a protester came to the hospital.
Caregivers' union will file lawsuit against the city over dismissals
The union, which represents hundreds of school caregivers who lost their jobs last month, plans to sue the city on Wednesday, as the layoffs are not necessary and discriminatory because of their undue influence on the schools teaching poor students.
The lawsuit is probably the last open to the union, district council 37, in its efforts to undo the layoffs.
Six-and-a-hundred seventy-two school helpers, care coordinators and other collaborators who were among the city's worst-paid employees and had lost their jobs in a move on October 7 have described the union leader as a political revenge for denying the city access to a health fund maintained by the working groups last year.
This allegation has been categorically denied by the authorities.
At a city council hearing last month, Dennis M. Walcottot, director of schools, said the layoffs had been part of the budget that had been passed in June.
A member of the city council shot back and explained to him that in the budget there was nothing specified that employees of the school aid would be dismissed.
Officials from City Council 37 said they had and warned the Ministry of Education against a lawsuit. On Wednesday, there will be a press conference at which the filing of the lawsuit before the Supreme Court in Manhattan will be formally announced.
The lawsuit will list Mr. Walcott and the city's Department of Education as defendants, counting eight laid-off workers as plaintiffs, all of whom are colored or Latin American women, like most of the aides who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's Department of Justice, said the city would not face the lawsuit and thus their individual claims could not be commented on.
According to a lawsuit submitted to the New York Times, the union has focused on the number of layoffs at schools in the city's poor neighborhoods to support a lawsuit for unequality. It claims that poor schools have lost more aid workers as they are chronically underfunded.
She lays out that while there have been no redundancies on Staten Island and few layoffs in Upper East Side or TriBeCa schools, 17 school helpers have lost in District 23 in Brownsville, Brooklyn, and 46 aides in Districts 8 and 9 have been discharged in the South Bronx.
Determining the loss of five out of eight school helpers employed in public school 36 in Harlem, where 68 percent of students live in poverty, the lawsuit states: "This is not a neighborhood of sheltered parents who can organize donations to absorb budget cuts."
P.S: The budget of the 36th district was cut by 3.26 per cent this year. In addition, funds were losted when the city changed its calculation method for extra funding for any poor school enrollment.
The union also complains that the heads of high schools were misleaded by language in a memo that authorized the dismissal of care coordinators.
The memo used the English word "excess" to describe the plot, even when "excess" in the U.S
"Sixty-six care coordinators have been dismissed, not transferred," the bill said.
mission ended, Congress ready to agree on Libya
In the end, it looks as if Republicans and Democrats in Washington are already agreeing on the uprisings in Libya.
All that was necessary for this was the success of the revolt, the arrest and assassination of longtime dictator Muammar Gaddafi and the cessation of American interference.
On Thursday, the Senate International Relations Committee resolves a resolution underloying Chairman John Kerry (Massachussetts) along with Senator John McCain (Arizona) and Joseph I. Lieberman (Connecticut), praising Libya for successful rebellion and U.S. troops for their "courage."
"This resolution honors the courageous people of Libya who have begun the reconstruction of their country," Kerry said.
"It is an affirmation of the support of its democratic efforts by two parties."
The language may sound innocent, but so far nothing has been easy in the Libya debate.
This summer, lawmakers from both parties and the White House argued over the choice of a series of resolutions to endorse or disapprove of the U.S. military's role in NATO's Libya mission.
In June, Kerry and McCain tabled a resolution that authorized the restricted use of U.S. military forces in Libya.
The bill was dismissed in the Senate by a broad fringed group of both parties and there was no vote.
But on the same day, this law fell through in the Senate. In addition, the board overruled a decision to cut funds for American operations in Libya.
As a result, the U.S. mission got into some kind of legislative purgatory - with a Congestion that, while upset enough to criticize Obama's course of action as part of the mission, didn't get angry enough to definitivelyly pull the ground away from under his feet.
Then the indecision of Congress was overtaken by the events.
Tripoli fell fell among the Libyan rebels in August and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
The NATO mission officially ended on October 31.
Now perhaps the Senate is ready for action.
"Congratulates the resolution of the Committee on International Relations to the people of Libya for their enormous courage and perseverance in their self-liberation" and "praises the men and women of the U.S. military and their coalition partners who support military operations to protect the people of Libya for their extraordinary courage and professionalism."
The resolution also reaffirms "the national interests of the United States in a successful and irrevocable transition from Libya to democracy."
Will the new measures receive full support from the panel on foreign relations, or will they divide lawmakers, as June's law did?
The last compose is the resistance of Senator Richard G. Lugar (Indianapolis), the committee's top republican.
Lugar's spokesman said on Monday that he had not yet seen the final version of the new law.
According to Kapitol, it's up to the Senate to vote this week on a constitutional amendment calling for a balanced budget, a top priority in nearly every congressional legislature period by Republicans and some Democrats.
The measures, led by Republican Bob Goodlatte (Virginia), will be the first constitutional change in the budget or any other area that is currently coming to vote in the Senate or Congress.
Other legislators have so far unsuccessfully tried to put their changes to the vote, even though they've tried it
Goodlatte's amendment is just one of 68 constitutional amendments that have been submitted to this Congression, covering a whole range of issues.
Some are double - the same law has been submitted to both Congress and the Senate - others only deviate slightly from each other.
There are at least 15 versions of the compensatory budget change in the Senate alone and another dozen that would "control federal spending."
Some of the laws are also identical, but they give their principals - especially beginners to the Republicans who want to polish up their CV - the possibility of bragging that they have "committed" such a measure.
The budget control bill, passed in August, requires both Senate and Congress to vote on an amendment to the budget settlement.
In March, 58 senators voted in favour of a non-binding resolution supporting the idea of such an amendment.
Beyond the budget, some members want to limit the number of provisions that members of Congress can submit, while Republican José E. Serrano (New York) seeks to lift the limit to two terms of the president.
Republican Jesse L. Jackson (Illinois) has proposed a series of changes that guarantee, among other things, the right to good education and health care.
A change that prohibits marriage among same-segred people, as well as a change that prohibits the burning of flags, is a recurring template.
Some legislators also want to allow the repeal of federal laws if two-thirds of states reject them.
As diligent as the members suggest changes, they are as much faster as in previous years.
In the 111th Congress, lawmakers submitted 77 of such amendments. in the 110th Congress 66.
The pace fades compared to that of the early 1990s, in which members regularly proposed more than 150 changes every two years, according to the figures provided by the Senate library.
However, they weren’t much more successful than today.
The last amendment to be added - the 27th - was ratified in 1992, but has actually been proposed to Congress as early as 1789.
"Hit but not defeated," Giffords said in the first television interview following the assassination
Ten months after an armed man shot at her from a distance during an electoral event in Tucson, Republican Gabrielle Gifffords (Arizona) stepped up Monday night on national television. She sang alone to the Broadway play "The Sun Will Come Out Tomorrow" and said the desire to recover was greater than the one to return to Congress.
When she, along with Diane Sawyer, appeared in the ABC news "20/20," she wrestled for words and needed help walking.
The broadcast showed the severe slow recovery from the damage caused by the bullet that divided its skull, pierced the left side of its brain and traversed its head from front to back.
Three months ago, she surprisingly returned to Congress to triumphantly cast her vote on the debt brake.
In her first television interview after the January assassination in which six people were killed and 13 injured, Gifford smiled, laughing and singing - describing her recovery as "difficult."
When Sawyer asked her how she felt, Gifford (41) replied: "Quite well."
The interview with Gifford and her husband, astronaut Mark Kelly, was part of an hour-long "20/20" special recorded shortly before the release of the couple's new book: "Gabby: A story about courage and hope."
The special haunted Gifford's recovery since the January 8 assassination.
In videos Kelly had recorded for the first few weeks, she can be seen in the hospital bed, shaving a bare shaved head and a long scarf over her forehead.
She can only lift one or two fingers if her husband encourages her.
Months later, Giffords sits in a wheelchair, while therapists show her performing simple movements.
A nod with his head.
A twick with your lips.
And then, her first words: "What."
After a few days, Giffords whispers another word: "Toast." She wants her breakfast changed.
Videos played on the ABC show show that music played an important role in Gifford's recovery: you can see the congresswoman, along with the theatrists, sing along to Cyndi Lauper's "Girls Just Wanna Have Fun" and to Tom Petty's "Free Fallin."
In an interview with Sawyer, Giffords, who spends two hours a daily with therapy, said she can't remember the day of the assassination.
Her husband said that on March 12, he read Giffords an article by a daily newspaper about the assassination when she interrupted him and asked for the first time about the six people who had been killed during the assassination.
Alleged shooter Jared Lee Loughner sits in a Missouri prison and is forcibly receiving medical treatment while awaiting his trial.
"Many people have died," Gifford's Sawyer recounted.
"Hard, hard, hard."
In the course of his wife's recovery, according to Kelly Sawyer, she said, "I've been hit."
"So, I'd say, "Gabby, you didn't get defeated?"" Kelly said.
You just got hit.
And you'll get through that and you'll recover and you'll come back stronger than ever.
Troops are more likely to feel pity than respect
The event was a Wall Street gala that raised millions of dollars for homeless veterans in New York City.
Kid Rock sang a ballad about helplessness, frustration and loss.
A hundred soldiers, sailors, pilots and marines gathered around him on a keyword.
The crowd in black tuxedos stood up and applauded.
"The soldiers were seen as heroes," said David Saltzman, who organized the fundraiser in the spring.
A high-ranked military officer at the gala, who was witnessed by the chief of staff and then-chairman, Admiral Mike Mullen, among others, saw the role of the troops differently.
"They were introduced like orphans," the officer wrote in an e-mail.
I'm sure the organizers meant well.
I've known that.
But it wasn't respect, it really wasn't.
The blatant contradictory impressions illustrate the difficult relationship between the military and the mostly distant, sometimes admiring American public.
Trooples are showered with praise for their casualties.
But praise has its award, service members say.
The public is increasingly behaving as if the people in uniform were suffering.
"We were by no means casualties," said Brigade General Sean B. MacFarland, who commanded troops in Iraq and will soon go to Afghanistan.
"But seemingly the only way to support is to press us into the role of unhappy souls."
The issue is extremely sensitive to military leaders who don't want to appear ungratefulous or in dispute with the public they serve.
They have also realized that the rage that returning troops have faced in recent years of the Vietnam War has been far worse.
As a result, conversations about silencedness and private conversations take place between war veterans.
After his two sons have returned from battles with the Marines, colonel Mark Cancian warned them that people outside the military world could see their service from two different perspectives.
Some would look at them with a sense of reverencement as they defeated rebels and traveled to exotic places.
Others would be wondering if an "angry, violent veneter hides below the surface," said Cancian, who had fought in Iraq and took over an upscale state post in Washington after his return.
During his quest for work, he said he sensed that some of the interrogators subtly questioned whether he was able to withstand the pressure of a demanding post in Washington right after his return from battle.
"If you talk about your service, you have to hold the negative impressions against it," Cancian explained to his sons.
The lack of easility of the military is partly rooted in the lack of American interest in the war.
Achievements on the battlefield are rarely singled out as praise by a country that has little experience with the military and sees little benefit in the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
"We, as a nation, no longer appreciate military heroism as it was absolutely common in World War II," said Lieutenant General David Barno a.D., who commanded U.S. troops in Afghanistan.
Instead, the praise of the politicians and the public largely focuses on the suffering of a service member.
Troops gain recognition for the number of attacks they have endured, the number of friends they have lost or for the severity of their injuries.
Wireld faces growing pressure for tough crackdown on rape
This week, an important hearing decides whether 28 women and men are indicted for non-intervenement in rape in the military court.
If it doesn't come to that, hundreds of plaintiffs will be ready for the next hearing.
Private Elizabeth Lyman was 25 years old and 11 weeks pregnant when she was raped at night by a fellow soldier.
It was in October 2008 and she was just returning to her barracks in Miramar, north of San Diego, from dinner with a friend when she met a male colleague carrying a 12pm pack of beer.
He asked her if he could accompany her into her room for a drink.
"My alarm bells went on," Lyman said.
But the Texanist swiped aside her concerns.
I was like, "Oh what, he's 19.
He's from Texas," she said.
"I wish I'd listened to my gully."
A few minutes after going to Lyman's room, the man stepped behind her, pushing her down and raped her, she claims.
This week, on November 18, Lyman and 27 other current and former military employees are anxiously awaitinging a trial with a historic hearing in Arlington, Virginia deciding whether a lawsuit they filed against former Defense Secretary Robert Gates and his predecessor, Donald Rumsfeld, will be admitted in court.
The lawsuit alleges that Gates and Rumsfeld did not crack down on the widespread rape within the military and violating the constitutional rights of the soldiers.
Filed in February by Susan Burke, a lawyer from Washington, D.C., the lawsuit initially included 17 plaintiffs, but who rose to 28 in the following months: 25 women and three men who all claim they were raped or sexually abused by fellow soldiers, and that the military failed to investigate, convicte, or provide appropriate jurisdiction following the alleged attacks.
Burke says she're pursuing the top military lead as the problem begins there.
"The military is hierarchically constructed," she says.
“Who are the individuals who are in a position to eradicate the retribution of the culture?”
Burke also says she has contacted nearly 400 other survivors since February, many of whom may be part of future lawsuits.
Their strategy: Instead of all plaintiffs putting their eggs in a basket, so to speak, they will, if necessary, file many more lawsuits in order to maintain the pressure.
The defense defense team, led by federal prosecutor Neil MacBride, has filed a motion to dismiss lawsuits.
The court's files, available to Newsweek, detail the defense strategy, first and foremost that the military cannot be indicted by current or former soldiers for injuries that have occurred in the armed forces.
Due to a 1950 Supreme Court, the so-called Feres Doctrine, the government is not liable for injuries inflicted on active-duty soldiers.
"The alleged damage occurred during plaintiffs' military service," the document said.
The Ministry of Defence denies any comment on this story.
Things have changed since the 1950s.
If women are still not admitted to service in combat, the distinction lies only in the name: modern wars rarely have clear fronts.
In both Iraq and Afghanistan, teams of female soldiers are playing increasingly important roles, especially in contact with local women in the war zones.
The result: between these two wars, 150 female soldiers died, two-thirds of them in combat operations.
It's time for the military to be modernized, Burke says.
And she's not alone in her fight: three more lawsuits other than hers are pending.
At Yale Law School, Veterans' Legal Services Clinic prepares a lawsuit against the top four military academies for allegedly promoting a misogynistic atmosphere.
Independently of this, the non-proficient Vietnam Veterans of America collects a lawsuit against the military, which focuses on the diagnosis of "personality disorder," who received rape victims to release them from service - a common course of action, according to activists such as Anu Bhagwati of the Service Women's Action Network, a human rights group.
Her group filed a lawsuit against the Veterans Administration in December, accusing it of discriminatory practices in its handling of benefit claims for individuals who claimed to have been sexually abused on duty.
Nine laws have been submitted to Congress independently by a mixture of senators and representatives of both parties offering a number of clues.
In 2010, according to the Sexual Assault Prevention and Response Office, 3,158 sexual abuses were reported.
However, most abuses are not reported at all, and according to the Pentagon’s own estimate, this figure takes into account about 13.5 percent of the estimated 19,000 cases that occur per year.
The report, published in March, also examines law enforcement rates: in 2010, 20 per cent of recorded cases in the military came to court, only half as many as in civil law.
The plaintiffs in Burke's case describe their attacks as just the first of a series of traumas.
In Lyman's case, she reported the alleged rape of the military police less than an hour after the robbery
That night, she says, she was asked 11 times to recount the details of her rape by a whole number of cops, doctors and commanders.
She had a rape set and the blood of her attacker, which came from an incision on his arm, was found in her bed.
Six months later, in April 2009, Lyman was listened to by a judge. During that hearing, she was asked about the sex she had before assault with her boyfriend.
Six people testified as character witnesses on behalf of the alleged perpetrator who was ultimately acquitted.
"I still remember the day when the verdict was read out," Lyman says.
"I thought the contractions were going to set in. I ran screaming out of the courtroom."
"They took me to the psychiatric department and I remember that when I came out again, I said to my commander: This was not his procedure. That was my procedure."
Lyman has been urged to visit a military psychiatrist who ultimately diagned a personality disorder and declared it incapacitated.
In January 2010, she was dishonorably discharged, so she receives no support whatsoever.
Whether the lawsuit Lyman and the other plaintiffs filed is progressing is pure speculation, says John Turley, a professor of jurisprudence at George Washington University Law School.
Nevertheless, he notes: "In my view, they should move forward."
What's between them and a verdict is this doctrine, which has been criticised since it was questioned for the first time.
I've been a critic of the Feres Doctrine throughout my life.
The military is lagging behind for a decade because they don't have the same incentive and deterrence that liability entails.
He adds: "The odds are against them... but it's important for them to try."
These things will only change when good people are willing to fight.
If the hearing doesn't follow their path, Burke, the plaintiff's lawyer, will begin with her hundreds of other plaintiffs for future cases.
"We will continue to fight the military in this regard until it comes to reforms," she says.
Or until we die, whatever's first.
Walls have eyes: How researchers are watching you on Facebook
Before becoming the new face of right-wing extremism in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was just one of many guys broadcasting their anti-immigration opinion online.
On Monday, Breivik, who confessed to having perpetrated during a rampage in Norway in July that killed 77 people, had his first public hearing in court.
Although Breivik may have acted alone, he is far from alone in cyberspace: he has spent plenty of time in front of his computer and chatted with some of the millions of nationalists who support far-right groups on sides of social networks.
After this summer's tragedy, researchers wanted to find out more about these people.
But how do you find them?
That's easy - you only have to log in to Facebook.
"We found it wasn't hard to get to them," says Jamie Bartlett, the lead author of a recently published report by the British ideas factory DEMOS on digital populism in Europe.
Facebook's hideout of personal data is so encyclopedic, Bartlett says, that researchers simply have to use the site's advertising tool to determine their desired demographic data exactly with scientific accuracy - just as marketers have been doing for years.
Bartlett's team found half a million fans of far-right groups across Europe and then targeted them with adds, but instead of linking to a new band or a new diet product, the Adds invited users to a survey asking about their level of education, their stance towards violence and the assessment of their own future.
Thanks to Facebook, Bartlett and his colleagues were able to interview more than 10,000 supporters from 14 far-right parties in 11 European countries - without even having to leave their office once.
"It's quite a new method of research," Bartlett says.
Some of the results are hardly surprising. Online promoters of far-right groups are mostly young, masculine and annoyed about immigration .
But in a kind of surprising distortion, "those who combine their online activism with offline activism are more sensible, democratic and less violent than those sitting behind their computer screens."
DEMO's work is just one example of how Facebook is becoming a new hot tool in the hands of scientists.
Idea factories, medical researchers and political scientists use the site to study everything, from health issues to social trends such as "likes," text messages and status updates.
With more than 800 million active users adding an average of three content per day, Facebook's "data supernova" generates a research boom that increased the number of scientific papers with the name of this page in the title by nearly 800% over the past five years.
For some researchers, the beauty of Facebook is the fact that through this page you can study people that you don't usually reach with your clipboard on the street.
"If you used to try to question people from the British National Party, it was very hard to identify them."
"They first had to inform the party and that would never have granted them access," Bartlett says.
"On Facebook, all of this falls away - you're going straight to them."
Others say that the site provides a way to recognize and solve social health problems.
A recent study by Dr Megan Moreno of the University of Wisconsin, Madison, and her colleagues, has found that students discussing their frenzy on Facebook are significantly more prone to problem-drinking than students who were silent on this subject.
Moreno suggests that equal students, such as residential counselors, monitor the site and help a student if it posts too many status updates.
"You can't solve a problem if you didn't recognize it," Dimitri Christakis, Moreno's co-author and head of Child Heal, Behavior and Development at the Children's Research Institute in Seattle told the Washington Post.
"We have found a way to identify vulnerable children who would not have been found otherwise."
In addition, Facebook is constantly tapping the data of its own population.
The site's data team has compiled statistics on relationship status and Valentine's Day, turnout in the 2010 midle elections, and a national happiness index (although, in the first place, information has been used to which only Facebook has access - masters of digital freedom have long known that the site should make its large data treasure trove accessible to others for research purposes).
But the users of Facebook know that the page observes them, whether they want it or not. In order to be able to play Scrabble with a friend thousands of miles away, you allow the page to spend your own personal data.
But what if Facebook users become part of a survey they don’t even know anything about?
While researchers have strict guidelines on how to collect their data offline, everything is allowed online.
"I don't think many users even thought about the fact that a researcher could study their profiles," Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Lab, says of a 2006 study in which he looked at the public pages of students on Facebook.
"As far as she's concerned, it's just about her and her friends."
Then there's the question of the methodology.
Even offline there is no guarantee that a research participant is absolutely honest.
On Facebook it is impossible to knew how many of the information is true in a user profile and in posts.
"What you're saying on Facebook and what you're doing outside of Facebook is two completely different things," Selwyn says.
That’s where Selwyn thinks, despite his years as a technology scientist, that a clipboard and a pen are still the best research tools anyone can use.
"There's no substitute for going out into the real world and talking to real people," he says.
"Social research has to take care of the social - and a lot of social life still takes place offline."
'Cold and inhuman': Anders Behring Breivik appears before a public court for the first time
Anders Behring Breivik, the man who confessed to two rampages that killed 77 people in Norway this summer, has described himself as a "resistance" fighter in his first appearance before a public court in Oslo.
"I'm a military commander in the Norwegian resistance movement and a Templar Knight of Norway," he said outside a courtroom with more than 500 people.
"I object to the court as you have received your mandate from organizations that support hasside ologies and because it supports multiculturalism."
"I acknowledge the laws, but I plead not guilty."
Judge Torkjel Nesheim interrupted Breivik during this monologue as he "didn't want to give Breivik the opportunity to use this hearing as a platform where he can express his views."
For the same reason, he denied Breivik, who brought a pre-written speech, the permission to contact the relatives of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also ordered that police keep Breivik in custody for 12 more weeks, he doesn't have access to the media for four weeks. He said that the authorities will closely control all visits and entire correspondence over eight weeks.
Approximately 30 survivors and relatives of the victims attended the hearing.
Some came in the hope of being able to see Breivik, others wanted the confirmation that he remains behind lock and bolt.
Everyone took part with the goal that everything boils down to closing away.
"I thought he seemed cold and inhumane," Utoya survivor told Norwegian broadcaster NRK.
"It was unpleasant, but for me it goes a bit further after I've seen and heard the suspect."
Breivik wore a dark suit and a blue tie and appeared calm and professional throughout the hearing. He looked journalists and survivors in the eye as he entered the building and left again.
Despite Breivik's bizarre hint that he was part of a larger "resistance" movement, the judge concluded that he was not mentally ill and said that there was no evidence that he had had accomplices.
It may please some that the July 22 attacks were seemingly the work of a single extremist and not that of a group of radicals.
But does this automatically ease the grief and confusion that an entire nation still senses?
"I wished he looked like a monster, but he doesn't do that," a victim's relative said.
"It would be so much easier if he did."
7 Lessons from the interview with the late Steve Jobs, which was shown in a theater this week
After the death of Steve Jobs on October 5, some compared him to Henry Ford, highlighting the technology genius as a great modern inventor.
But what Apple fans might find most insightful in the recently rediscovered job interview, which was dusted off this week before special film screenings in the important markets, is how much this creative mind has understood business processes and workflows.
He was certainly a designer, a dreamer and self-proclaimed hippie, but he was also a meticulous organizer eagerly tinkering with diagrams - a man who believed that many managing directors suffer from "a disease of thinking," "that a really great idea makes up 90 per cent of the work... [but] there's an enormous amount of craftsmanship between a great idea and a great product."
In 1995 Bob Cringely developed the TV series "Triumph of Professional Idiots" about the departure of the personal computer. And he sattled for more than an hour with jobs in which they had one of the rare, in-depth conversations.
By that point, it had been 10 years since Jobs had been forced out of Apple. And he was already working hard on his new computer company NeXT, looking forward to widespread adoption of the internet.
While a small portion of Cringley's interview was used in specialist idiots, he says the interview's copy templates were missing from delivery.
It wasn't until after Job's death that a full VHS copy was discovered in the director's garage.
This slightly reworked footage forms the basis for the 68-minute film "Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview," coming to the Landmark Theatre this Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone known for loathing extensive interviews, Jobs appears surprisingly eager to explain his technological philosophies and business strategies here.
And incredibly thoughtful.
In the course of the interview, there are four times longer pauses between question and answer - 10 to 15 seconds of ponderation, in which Jobs clearly balances his thoughts and strives for accuracy.
There are also moments when Jobs′ perfectionism comes to light when he reacts seemingly impatiently to Cringely’s questions and pushes him to address topics or incidents of greater importance more quickly.
Recorded before the era of Pixar, iPod or iPhone, the Lost Interview somehow seems eerily farsighted when Jobs assesses what other companies are doing right or false in his opinion and represents his own vision of the computer's future.
It’s one thing to read posthumous appreciations of career Jobs. But seeing jobs here as the eager dreamer who’s on the bring is something very different, original and inspiring.
He has a vision of how things could and should be.
Shortly after he had carried out all this, he left and did it.
A quick look at seven notable sequences of Lost Interview:
As he learned to run a company:
"During the whole years in business, I've always ask, "Why are you doing things?". And the answer you always get is, "Oh, that's how it's made easy."
No one knows why he's doing what he're doing. No one in business really thinks about things in-depth.
I've figured that out.
Jobs continues its efforts to rationalize accounting at Apple.
Amazed by how costs were recorded - mostly starting with "standard costs", which were quantified for mere conjectures and which were then adjusted with "deviation" - he developed an automated factory that ensured that the business costs could be determined down to the smallest.
As the pope was fooled:
Jobs tells of the "blue box" he built with Steve Wozniak, a device with which you could sneak into the phone company and make long-distance calls for free.
Much has already been written about the device, but Job's enthusiasm when he describes the Blue Box as an act of self-empowerment is contagious.
He describes how Wozniak and he would test the box by using a paid phone, making a call, and then connecting from one AT&T network to another while looping through as many satellites as possible.
"We sent things half a dozen times around the globe and if young into the phone, it came out again a minute later on the phone next door," Jobs kichernd recounts.
"We were young and we learned that we ourselves can build something that can control trillions of dollars in infrastructure around the world."
Both of us, we didn't know much, but we were able to build this little thing that can control a gigantic thing, that was an incredible lesson and I don't think there would be Apple without it.
Jobs continues to tell of a glorious praise that Wozniak and he almost pulled off when they shouted in the middle of the night via blue box in the Vatican and wanted to talk to the pope, while they were able to imitate Henry Kissinger as best they could.
Since several members of the Catholic hierarchy were summoned in the middle of the night to talk to the American diplomat, the two had to laugh before the pope himself came on the phone.
How he fell in love with the technique (and unpreparedly called Bill Hewlett):
At the age of 12, when he was looking for spare parts to build a frequency counter, he searched for Bill Hewlett in the phone book and called him.
Shortly after her 20-minute phone call, Jobs received a part-time job at Hewlett-Packard for the summer.
"That had a remarkable impact on me. It was the only company I'd seen at that age and it shaped my views about what a company was and how well it treated its employees."
Later, Jobs became one of the HP employees who visited the company's research labs in Palo Alto, where he saw "the first desktop computer ever made."
He was as big as a suitcase, had a small screen with a brown tube and I fell in love with him.
I'd be climbing up at HP as a teenager and hanging on to this machine and writing programs for them.
How To Improve Innovations:
Several times in an interview, Jobs talks about how to spontaneously invent new products.
Early in his career, when he installed some boards, he was asked by a client to assemble the whole computer.
He worked with a payment deadline of only 30 days and had to think through both the assembly and the delivery of the finished devices.
Later at Apple, he remembers the problems he had in developing a computer mouse:
"I remember resounding arguments... You yelled at me that it would take five years [to build a mouse that would cost $300]] and I stood up, walked out and found a manufacturer."
Ninety days later, we had a mouse that we were able to build for $15. That was unbelievable.
About large companies slacking:
Long before he revived Apple, Jobs foresawed the ways in which so many industrial giants stumbled with their visions and lost control of their shares.
“Let’s say you’re working at IBM or Xerox, so you’re doing a better copier or printer, so what?”
They own the share monopoly, so the company is no longer successful.
Sales and marketing make it more successful, so [it's such people] who ultimately run the companies and the product with which people are pushed out of decision-making.
The product genius that has led to this monopoly position is eradicated by the people who have no concept regarding good products vs. bad products - craftsmanship necessary.
That's what happened at Xerox...
Xerox could have owned the entire computer industry.
One could have been ten times taller if it could have been Microsoft of the 1990s...
They've gotten defeat from victory.
On innovation as an art form:
“There’s a lot of craftsmanship between a great idea and a great product when you develop this great idea, it’s changing and growing.”
You learn a lot about your subtleties.
You have to make votes, certain things you can't, but electrons, glass, robots, factories.
You have to remember 5000 things, these concepts, and bring them all together...
Ultimately, it boils down to the taste, it boils down that you're trying to deal with the best stuff that people have created, and then trying to bring those stuff together into what you're doing.
Picasso said that good artists are copying, great artists are stealing.
We are always shameless in stealing great ideas.
Part of what Macintosh made great was that the people who work there were musicians, poets, artists, zoologists, historians who happened to be the world's best computer scientists.
If it hadn't been for the computer industry, they'd create fantastic things in other areas.
Scalia and Thomas dine with opponents of health care laws, accrue as court
On the date the Supreme Court gathers behind closed doors to discuss the politically sharing question of whether one will listen to a challenge to President Obama's health laws, two of his judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, took part in a dinner sponsored by the law firm that the case will take place in court.
The incident took place last Thursday, when all nine judges met for a meeting to deliberate on the petitions for consideration.
One of the cases covered was a lawsuit raised by 26 states against far-reaching health care reforms passed by Congress last year, a buzzword among Conservative activists all over the U.S.
The judges agreed to the hearing. In fact, a 5 1/2-hour basic discussion is expected in March, presumably the outcome of which will presumably boost the 2012 presidential battle, which will be in full swing at the time the court decision is announced.
The lawyer, who will stand trial and claim that the law is to be overturned, will presumably be Paul Clement, once attorney-in-law under George W. Bush's administration.
Clement's law firm, Bancroft PLLC, was one of the nearly two dozen companies that supported the federalist society's annual dinner, a long-established group dedicated to defending conservative legal principles.
Another company that sponsored the dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the business associations, the National Federation of Independent Business, which contest the law.
Another sponsor was the pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc., which is financially enormously involved in the outcome of the process.
The dinner took place a few hours after the court deliberated on the case at a Washington hotel.
Mitch McConnell, the Senate's faction leader and a declared opponent of health care overhaut, among others, had service
Guests of dinner? Scalia and Thomas.
This is nothing new: The two judges have been attending events of the Federalist Society for years.
And that doesn't violate moral rules.
In fact, judges free themselves from the code of conduct that regulates the actions of the lower federal judges.
Were they, they would probably fall under principle 4c of the Code, which states: "A judge can participate in charity events law closer and other organizations, whereby the judge may not be a speaker, guest of honor or, according to the program, a guest of such events."
Nonetheless, Scalia's and Thomas's extreme proximity to two of the law firms related to the case, as well as to a company with a massive financial interest in it, were enough to alarm morale-and-government activists.
"This baffling rupture with morality and the contrast to the co-defeat punishes the lawsuits of some judges' lies that the court adheres to the same rules that apply to all other federal judges," said Bob Edgar, president of the Common Cause, a civil rights movement in the U.S.
"The judges won and dined during a benefit event with lawyers with pending proceeding at the court."
Their appearance and support of a fundraiser for this event undermine any claim to impartiality and are not acceptable.
Scalia and Thomas show little consideration for critics who say they mix court matter too easily with groups derived from the agenda, such as federalist society.
And Thomas' wife Ginni is a well-known conservative activist.
In contrast, the Conservatives claim that it is Judge Elena Kagan who has a mortal problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan was attorney general under the Obama administration when the first law challenges came to court in the first instance.
Her critics urged Kagan to withdraw herself from hearing the case for bias, as she was too involved in defending the law to be unbiased.
Kagan doesn't want to do that.
Boeing pulls record order of 18 trillion dollars ashore
Airline Emirates orders 50 two-speed Boeing 777 aircraft with an option on 20 more aircraft.
In addition, Boeing is selling six Boeing Dreamliners to Oman Air, a business worth more than 1 trillion dollars.
It's really rare news that a company gets an order over 50 pieces of its products.
But it's a big deal if the company's plane maker Boeing Co. is and the list price for those 50 planes overall stands at a record sum of 18 trillion dollars.
Just as uncommon is Boeing's client: the Middle East, a growing gold mine of future aircraft orders.
In a forecast released on Monday, the Chicago-based company estimates airlines from the Middle East will need 2,520 planes worth 450 trillion dollars over the next 20 years.
Boeing announced over the weekend its biggest order ever for commercial aircraft, as measured by the value of the order: 50 two-speed Boeing 777 jets.
The hammer order came from Emirates from Dubai.
In addition, Boeing announced in 2011 at the Dubai aviation show, the airline has an option to buy 20 more aircraft, which raises the total list price to 26 trillion dollars.
On Monday, according to Boeing Oman Air ordered six Boeing 787-8, the so-called Dreamliner, which enormously improves gasoline consumption and passenger comfort.
The list price for six of these planes is more than 1 trillion dollars.
"That's absolutely positive for Boeing," said Neal Dihora, Morningstar's store expert.
"The quality of these special customers is very high."
Many airlines in the Middle East are owned by oil-producing countries.
So they don’t just have the money to pay the planes. Rather, an increase in oil prices, which usually harms airlines, as this is one of their biggest cost points, isn’t as damaging, since the country earns on a higher price of oil.
That's important as they tend less than other airlines to cancel or postpone aircraft orders when oil prices rise, Dihora said.
"They've had a natural hedge," he said.
Emirates's appointment reaffirms Boeing's leadership position in the broad-bodied aircraft market and helps keep Airbus competitor SAS at bay, which attacks supremacy with the A350.
Boeing's victory at the aviation show comes four months after Airbus has announced that it would postpone the introduction of the largest A350, which competes directly with the 777-300ER.
One postponed the introduction to gain more confidence after customers demanded a higher payload and reach.
Boeing shares rose 1.5% to $67.94.
Sister of Mexican president seemingly loses election in Michoacan
Luisa Maria Calderon claims drug trafficker opponent Fausto Vallejo of the PRI has backed in the race for the governor's post.
President Felipe Calderon's sister has apparently lost her bid for the office of Muchoacan's governor during the violent state elections. She claimed on Monday that drug traffickers had influenced the race in favor of one of her opponents.
According to the preliminary results, Fausto Vallejo of the Institutional Party of the Revolution, the PRI, has won the race for the governorship post in the western state.
"The intervention of organized crime throughout the electoral processes, and especially yesterday, is alarming, not only for Michoacan, but for the whole country," Luisa Maria Calderon said in a radio interview one day after Sunday's election.
"You threatened our candidates, ous employees in the polls ... You've staffed voting booths, set up road blockades ... and instructed people to vote [for the PRI]."
The PRI reigned in Mexico for seven decades until it lost the presidency in 2000.
But they aspire to a return and victory in Michoacan is an important step in these efforts.
The PRI is hoping for victory in July's presidential election.
Vallejo appears to have narrowly beaten Claderon, who had still led in the polls before Election Day.
Calderon, a nominee of her brother's conservative Partido Acción Nacional PAN, refused to recognize Vallejo as victor.
Silvano Aureoles, the candidate of the left-wing Democratic Revolutionary Party PRD, the currently incumbent governor, came in third place, a heavy defeat for the divided and hapless left-wing leadership for the 2012 presidential election.
Aureoles also refused to acknowledge the preliminary results.
Vallejo, former mayor of Morelia's capital, denies links to drug traffickers and urges the other candidates to accept the results.
Michoacan has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in marijuana, heroin and amphetamines.
It is President Calderon's home country and he has chosen Michoacan to launch a military-led offensive against drug traffickers in December 2006.
Violence is still ongoing.
A PAN mayor was killed a week before the election during an electoral event for Luisa Maria Calderon and numerous candidates have dropped out of local elections out of fear.
For the PRI, a victory strengthens the party, no matter how disgusting the elections in Michoacan also appear.
The man presumably representing the PRI in the race of presidential office is Enrique Pena Nieto who congratulated Vallejo from Washington and said: "I think this victory will make us look bravely into next year."
ADB urges Asia for aid in eurozone bailout
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) is calling on India and China to support the rescue of the eurozone in its severe debt crisis to prevent a long-term downward trend that will curb Asia's growth.
Rajat Nag, administrative director of ADB, based in Manila, said the world's two fastest-growing economic nations must do "everything in your power" to speed up the recovery of the currency bloc either through the International Monetary Fund or through direct mutual agreements.
He warned of the BRIC countries (Brazilia, Russia, India, China), which "sufferingly" look at the difficulties in Europe, and said that financial supply of European leadership and European resources through Asia helps prevent a long-term collapse of the global economy.
“We’re all in the same boat.”
"So everyone who can help Europe out of this crisis is helpful," he told the Financial Times in an interview on the sidelines of the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
“Asia may be protected to some extent, but it’s not immune.”
"So if China and India can help, it's by all means."
Europe's monetary union has fallen into a serious debt crisis in recent months.
In the past few days, Italy's interest costs have risen dangerously and the prime ministers of both Italy and Greece have resigned.
Europe's emerging severe debt crisis fuels fears that the global economy could plunge into recession and led to shouts that the leading emerging economies would have to come together to support the eurozone.
Mr Nag predicted that any support of Asia will be channelled by the IMF, but said that mutual agreements - such as the purchase of bonds of the EU rescue fund, the European Financial Stabilisation Facility (EFSF) - would provide greater bargaining power to European partners in Asia.
Anand Sharma, India's trading minister, said that "India will do everything in its power" to help the eurboro, as one's own economy is now suffering from declining exports and drying up foreign capital flow.
"Nobody wants the eurozone to remain unstable and turbulent," he said.
"We are facing major challenges and we need to maintain a high level of growth."
"It's not an option, it's an assignment, because where else do we find work for our millions of young men and women."
Others argue that developing economies like India have no instigation to help the rich Europeans if they themselves face major economic challenges.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University, said it would be very hard politically to select aid to the 1.2 trillion people in India, of whom more than 800 million out of 2 dollars, or even less in the day.
"At some point, they're going to find out that the Greeks will retire at 50 and spend holidays on the beach. Then you have a problem," he said.
Lee Howell, administrative director of the World Economic Forum, asked similarly why India’s reserves should be used to retain the numerous and well-paided public servants in Greece, with jobs in poorly managed facilities such as the railway.
Mr Nag said crises in the eurozone will have "significant domino effects" on the whole of Asia.
ADB's forecast with economic growth of 7.5 percent in Asia for the years 2011/now threatening "the risk of gradation" due to imminent danger from Europe.
He said vulnerable emerging markets would have to create "crisis plans" to protect themselves for a downward trend and a significant churn of their votes.
Urgent warnings from the Pentagon about possible cuts in the defense system
Defense Secretary Leon Panetta heated up Congress on Monday as he warned that automatic budget cuts would unnoticedly weaken national security, triggering a financial chain reaction from the Pentagon's halls to the theatres of war in Afghanistan and civilian assembly lines.
The Pentagon is already thinking through cuts of 450 trillion dollars in the course of the coming decade, but now fears that further cuts by 600 trillion dollars or more will become necessary if Congress fails to finalize in spending.
"The implications of these cuts would be devastating for the ministry," Panetta wrote in a letter to Republican Senators John McCain, Arizona, and Lindsey Graham, South Carolina.
He said a failure of the Congress in achieving a budget agreement, which will lead to so-called confiscation, would lead to 23% general cuts as well as a halt to numerous new projects.
"Such a high cut, carried out in such a careless manner, would make most of our ship and construction projects unworkable - you can't buy three-quarters of a ship or building - and seriously harm other modernization efforts," Panetta wrote to the senators.
"We would also be forced to involuntarily separate ourselves from much of our civilian personnel and, since the reduction would be implemented so quickly, we would almost certainly have to furlough civilians in order to be able to achieve the goal."
"These changes would destroy the beliefs of those who sustain our military and seriously damage our alert."
The cuts were ultimately going to hit the combat troops," Panetta said.
"While the wartime contingents in anti-terrorism are not directly affected by confiscation, the serious encroachment on the basic budget would adversely affect war efforts," Panetta warned.
"Personnel settings have been shortened, leading to delays in attitudes and hiring oversight that support the war."
"The salaries of the staff would be shortened, which leads to late payments to war-time traffickers and the legal and political support would be disturbed."
The two senators answered Panetta 10 days ago and asked for more details regarding the potential influence of the automatic cuts on the Department of Defense.
"The consequences of confiscation by the Department of Defensiburity would initiate a rapid decline of the United States as the world's leading military power."
"We're vehemently against this draconian act," the senators said in a joint statement Monday afternoon as they released Panetta's letter.
"This is a result that we can't live with, and it's certainly not one to impose us."
"Confiscation is a threat to the national security interests of the United States and it should not be allowed."
Whether Panetta's letter and senators' new warnings will increase the pressure on a compromise in the budget or intensify demands to exempt the Pentagon from cuts remains to be seen.
Panetta has increasingly bluntly pronounced the possible cuts, even though he was able to gain many years of experience with the budget in Congress and the White House before he came to the helm of the Pentagon, and knows that he is facing heavy decisions.
At a press conference last week, the defence secretary painted a bleak picture of what might be imminent - a military with a shell, but no nucleus.
"It's a ship without sailors."
“It’s a brigade without ammunition.”
"It's an air force squadron without adequately trained pilots."
"It's a paper tiger, an army of barracks, buildings and bombs without adequately trained soldiers capable of carrying out the mission," Panetta said in his opening address at the Pentagon.
"It is a force that lacks morality and alert and that is unablevable to absorb it with potential opponents."
"She's actually making you angry."
In an appendix to his letters to McCain and Graham, Panetta cited new details such as "significant operational risks: delays in times of crimines, conflicts and disasters, serious restrictions on our worldwide operational and intervention capability, resulting in unacceptable risk in future war operations."
And Panetta said that some of the biggest defense projects could fall victim to deletion, including those who have already been tested and are already in the early planning stages.
This list also includes the F35 Joint Strife Fighter, a planned new bomber aircraft, the next generation of ballistic submarines, the new Littoral Combat Ship, and the new ground combat vehicle, the Army and Marine need to replace the Humvee.
Were to stop further development and testing of the F35, it would save up to 80 trillion dollars over the next 10 years, but proponents say it's an important next step to armament, so potential dangers from China and other advocates can be eliminated.
How to unmask a lie
A look at the current headlines shows how dubious and widespread deception and lies are in everyday life.
The Republican presidential nominee, Herman Cain, is preoccupied at the allegations of sexual harassment on the part of several women. Both sides accuse each other of lying.
Penn State's leadership is accused of perjury as one allegedly withholded reports that a retired football coach sexually abused boys.
Last week, an open microphon was listened to when French President Nikolas Sarkozy told American President Barack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu was a liar.
Lies are destroying careers and shaking countries.
New York congresswoman and internet exhibitionist Anthony Weidner made a fool when he circulated denial that was very quickly refuted by irrefutable evidence.
Former presidential nominee John Edwards is accused of violations in campaign funding related to covering up an extramarital affair.
And then, of course, no one who experienced it forgets the media circus that President Bill Clinton sparked when he lied to Monica Lewinsky during his secondance in the Oval Office.
Stories about cheating at school and at college exams are widespread.
There were incidents in which teachers gave their students the exam responses so that they were good even in their performance reviews.
Mentors who should teach the opposite send a message that lies and cheating are okay.
How much deception do we experience?
According to studies, we lie between 10 and 200 times every day.
However, many of these lies are regarded as necessary lies.
Another study shows that strangers lie to each other three times during the first 10 minutes of a meeting.
Recognising or "exhibiting lies" is one that is important for everyone to learn, both for personal and professional reasons.
Far from a social game such as charades, in which the goal is to shout "Have you!", unmasking a deception is a reputable field of knowledge based on scientific data collected over the course of the past six decades at prestigious universities carrying out far-reaching research projects, especially in the fields of psychology and physiology.
One result of the research is that most ancient myths have been unmasked about lying.
Lies look you in the eyes.
They don't always stutter, stammer, blush and fidget.
From this, however, one must not conclude that these liars are difficult to uncover and expose.
A practised untarrator of lies can penetrate to the truth by learning about the structure of the assertion, the fine facial expressions, about questioning and timing.
I’ve spent several years examining the scientific findings of the comprehensive and ever-growing realm of unmasking. And it’s clear that unmasking a deception is a modern skill that is easy to learn and helps us to navigate in our complex world, especially if hiring or questioning individuals or negotiations or administration are among one’s own professional responsibilities.
Good liars are very good at assessing other people, arousing their interest and mastering one’s own emotions. And they intuitively feel how they are perceived by others.
Thanks to the research, we know that extroverted people lie more often than introverted people, that men are more likely to expose "self-centered" lies than women whose lies are more "orientated to others" - usually they want to protect the feelings of others - that married people lie to their partners less frequently than unmarried people (but the lies they recount are mostly "fat lies").
We also know that if one person has been unmasked as an culprit, others feel less obsolete to lying to that person.
How do you know if someone's lying?
First of all, by observing this person’s normal behavior.
That's called "baselining," the starting position.
It provides a point of reference for later evaluation of changes.
Observe the attitude, the laughter, the tuning quality of your subject.
You're better to know when someone's usually tripping with their foot all the time, so they don't wrongly accuse him when he's tripling his foot during a meeting.
Then pay attention to accumulations of conspicuous verbal and non-verbal behaviors.
Think of these accumulations as alarm bells shrill, not proof of unmasking.
People who are deceiving may freeze when they try to remember their story. Perhaps their feet point towards the door or they lean towards the exit, change their position in a significant way, or show a "relaxation after-at-attitude conversation," this deep, relaxing exhalation and the change in posture when all the hard questions are over.
Inquirers often incorrectly signal that a hiring interview is over, only to look for this relaxation after the hiring interview.
Also pay attention to your subject’s language.
Scott Peterson's slip-up is famous when he used the past form when he claimed his slain wife was still alive and sparked a national quest for her.
Deceptives can also use distancing language: "I didn't have a sexual relationship with this woman... Miss Lewinsky" or if one repeats a tricky question as a whole.
The most common verbal signs are subtle.
Some people use "qualifying language" if they answer a tricky question: "Well... to tell you the truth ... as far as I've known ... to the best of my knowledge and conscience."
As a result, the answer becomes perceiving rather than factual, which often causes the alarm bells to shrill.
There is no magic tool for unmasking lies, but you can learn skills thanks to which unmasking is possible.
Thanks to these skills, anyone is less likely to become a victim of scammers, both privately and professionally.
Controversial plan of an oil pipeline is being revised after being jeopardized by delayed U.S
A few days after the Obama administration threatened to delay the approval of a planned oil pipeline from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico, which angered the unions while reassuring environmentalists, the pipeline-building company is scrambling to re-plan the project so you can get back to normal.
TransCanada said on Monday evening that the planned pipeline will be diverted from the ecologically sensitive area of the Sandhills in Nebraska.
The company announced the decision at a press conference at Nebraska's parliament building.
Alex Pourbax of TransCanada said the company remains confident that the pipeline will ultimately be approved, albeit with a different course.
This came after an announcement by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs last week, according to which one would postpone a decision regarding a federal approval for the project, until the new possible course was checked, which avoids the area of Sandhills in Nebraska as well as the Ogallala aquifer, a large underwater supply.
"This is a real step forward," Republican Lee Terry, a Nebraska Republican, told Fox News.
The new environmental review ordered by the State Department may adjourn a decision until 2013, after next year's elections, even as officials deny that politicians were involved in the decision.
Obama stated in a written statement last week that the decision was a State Department assignment and pledged his support.
The 1700-mile pipeline has been linked to the Gulf of Mexico.
She would pump 700,000 barrels of oil a day from Alberta province to the refineries in Texas.
To do this, the pipeline would have to traverse six states.
Environmentalists hate them, but labor markets love them, because they cherish that the pipeline creates 20,000 jobs.
Yet Nebraska's legislatures, which are opposed to the plan, are weighing down the laws to force a departure from the Sandhills region and the Ogallala aquifer, a major source of drinking water and irrigation.
The current study on influence on the environment of the State Department has found that the project would have little impact on the environment, but the source of the energy industry states that the ministry's principal examiner has requested a separate probe of the review procedure focused on two questions.
One is whether a lobbyist hired by TransCanada, Paul Elliott, who was an adviser to Hillary Clinton in 2008, is in a conflict of interest.
The other is whether a company tasked with carrying out the original study was an inappropriate choice as it is related to TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle indicted as she allegedly forced a toddler to vomite out of fear
A New York family claims the disco diva Patti LaBelle would have received a raging attack in the lobby of her Manhattan building that would have so scared her little daughter that she vomited in fear.
Tirade of the "Lady Marmalade" singer scared 18-month-old Genevieve Monk that she's suffering from "personality changes, sleep disorder" and "increased fear of strangers," her family said outside the Supreme Court in Manhattan.
It was on November 10 last year, when stage worker Kevin Monk, his wife Roseanna, a kindergarten teacher, and Genevieve wanted to embark on a family outing.
While Kevin Monk retrieved the car, Roseanna Monk walked with his luggage and Genevieve on his arm into the lobby in her building on Riverside Boulevard.
Roseanna Monk said she stopped near the door and deposed her daughter for a moment.
"Someone appeared behind me and said, "Do you know what your daughter is doing right now?" I said, "Yeah," and went to take her back on the arm,"" she said.
LaBelle admonished the pregnant woman furiously.
"She said in an aggressive tone, "You shouldn't drop off your daughter near the door," said Roseanna Monk.
"I told her, "I don't care what you're saying or thinking."
She became angry and started scolding - she used the F-word and A-word and became very loud...
She had a bottle of water in her hand and began tipping water out of the bottle at me.
She said Genevieve had become hysterical.
"I said to this woman, "See what you did with my daughter," the child's mother said.
LaBelle threw herself at the mother, who was still carrying Genevieve on her arm, and had to be restrained by her accompaniment and moved to a waiting car, Roseanna Monk said.
The baby screamed so much it had to vomit, she said.
The LaBelle family later requested an apology in writing, but received no response.
LaBelle's lawyer and representative did not respond to calls asking for comment.
The lawsuit amounts to unspecified damages, but the family's lawyer, Sam Davis, said he does not charge a fee and plans the family to donate the money they will donate to children's cancer assistance.
"We want Patti LaBelle to take responsibility for their behaviour," Davis said.
"This kind of behavior is absolutely unacceptable, especially when it's displayed opposite to a pediatrician carrying an 18-month-old baby on her arm."
Climatic adversities put the brakes on the economy
In the first year when Mario L�ópez Valdez is in government, unemployment and undeclared work rose in Sinaloa, while foreign investment declined and the economy stagnated.
In 2011, Sinaloa will be remembered in economic history. It is the first year at which Mario López Valdez, who describes himself as the initiator of change and stoked great hopes regarding economic growth, is in government. However, as a result of climatic adversity, there may not be talk of growth, but on the contrary, the main economic indicators deteriorated.
The low temperatures in February not only caused losses in the millions in agriculture, but also limited the growth of the state economy and in the first quarter caused a general decline in economic activity by 3.6 percent. This is mainly due to the historical degradation of agricultural development by 31.16 percent, which had a braking effect on other economic sectors.
According to a report on the effects of frost, 582 000 hectares of agricultural lands were damaged by Consejo Mexicano para el Desarrollo Rural Sustentable and the remaining 21.07 percent were partially destroyed, mainly the vegetable, maize and chickpea cultivation.
Unemployment has risen continuously in the Mexican state until November. The Instituto Nacional de Estadística y Geografía observed a rapid increase in unemployment in the first quarter, because from January to March 55 053 inhabitants of Sinalo had no employment, which corresponds to 4.53 percent of the working population. In the second quarter, the unemployment rate rose to 5.28 percent and from July to September
Reduced liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa in the year goes back to several factors. The economist Gerardo López Cervantes, dean of the Faculty of economics and social sciences at the University of Autónoma (UAS), comments that the politics of the public institutions indirectly explains the growing unemployment.
"What we're seeing right now is an increase in unemployment dealing with several factors. Among other things, with liquidity losses in the market as a result of fund cuts by the legislation passed to combat money laundering in Congress, because for those who illegally accumulate dollars, it's not easy to let these work for themselves, invest them or use them for otherwise," he explained.
Migration stopped
López Cervantes pointed out that growing unemployment is also a demographic factor.
"The number of people leaving the country looking for work abroad, especially in the United States, has declined. On the one hand, this is due to the entry problems and, on the other hand, because it's also difficult to find work there. These people are staying here looking for work here, which raises unemployment," he says.
Undeclared work
The analysis of the labour market in Sinaloa is even more serious considering the high proportion of undeclared work. According to INEGI, this comprises 60.38 percent of those employed in the country who work without paying into social security coffers.
Unemployment and undeclared work come the low wages, the lowest in the whole of Mexico, according to the statistics of the Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Socially (Mexican social welfare institution) and the Secretaría del Trabajo y Previsión Social (government authority for labor and social care in Mexico).
The average monthly income of workers in Sinaloa was 5352 pesos by September; this is much less than the general government average, which stands at 7375 pesos.
Decrease in ADI
In addition to the reduction of economic activity and growing unemployment, the strongest decline in foreign direct investment (ADI) of the last ten years is recorded in 2011. According to Secretaría de Economía (state economic authority), the ADI amounted to 630 597 dollars from January to June, which corresponds to a shrinkage of 87.74 percent compared to the 5 143 312 dollars in the same period in the previous year.
The Secretaría de Desarrollo Económico del Gobierno (Government Authority for Economic Development) in turn has a total investment of 17 280 000 pesos in 2011, which mainly comes from companies in the tertiary sector.
Debts
In view of the economic crisis, the state government applied to the National Congress in July for a loan amounting to 3 000 000 000 pesos, which were to be invested in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities to reactivate the economy. The National Congress approved an amount of 200 600 000 pesos, however, no positive effects of the debt on any of the economic sectors in the country could be detected until November.
Reactions
For the representatives of entrepreneurship, the first year of reign Mario López made Valdé's incapacitancy apparent.
Miguel Loredo López, chairman of the Cámara Nacional de la Industria de la Transformación (State Chamber of the manufacturing industry in Mexico) argued that no efforts were made in 2011 to harmonise the views of the different sectors, including the state government.
"The economic activity in Sinaloa was really very tenuous this year. It has to work hard to regain the terrain lost this year and in 2012 we have to make a double effort. The efforts of everyone, and in particular the progress of the already well-attuned government team, have not been sufficiently coordinated," he explained.
Slow economic development
Loredo López expressed that events such as the frost at the beginning of the year complicated and slowed down the country's economic development.
"The low economic flow is the result of several events, including frost. One of the most important impacts is a slowed-down economy, which is not sufficiently moving to evoke an economic dynamic that could produce an acceptable flow of processes, products and services. That's why we're going to conclude the year well below our expectations," he said.
Redefine vocation
Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, chairman of the Confederación Patronal de la República Mexicana (Entrepreneurial Association of Mexico) in Sinaloa, stated that it was necessary to persuade the state to engage in more dynamic productive activities.
"In general, we have observed that work has been carried out. We believe it is necessary to develop a long-term programme to redetermine the country's economic vocation and line, because it is important to activate Sinaloa's material and humane resources," he said.
There's a lot to do
Coparmex's chairman emphasized the importance of developing a perspective for the future that puts the government's efforts in better shape.
"In general, I would say that the government has achieved good results in various areas. Economic development has progressed, but it is important to develop a long-term programme," he explained.
He stressed that the current state power had made progress on the government.
"There are areas where you can say that the government has acted more comprehensively. What I can say is that work has been done and that the government is aware that there is still a lot of work ahead of it," he said.
Arrest for 8 kg Marijuana
Five years in prison and 100 daily sentences were sentenceded by a resident of Navolato for possession of eight kilos of marijuana.
Prosecutors of the republic announced that the drug trafficker was Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval, who was punished for an offense against public health - here the possession of Marijuana intended for trading.
Pursuant to the AP/SIN/CLN/687/2010/M-I, Valenzuela Sandoval was arrested by Mexican militarians on June 7, 2010, in surveillance of the Cipriano Valdez road opposite guard post No. 80 in El Castillo, Navolato.
The drug trafficker drove in a white delivery truck, in which he stored a black bag in a box in the cart and in the passenger's seat a small backpack in which the military found eight kilos and 23 grams of marijuana.
