Debt for construction work
The social buildings for migrants planned in the H�ábitat, Rescate de Espacios Públicos and 3x1 programmes are in danger of not being completed due to a lack of funds or only with a great delay, Miguel Ángel López Miranda said.
The head of the municipal authority for social development explained that although the construction programs have already progressed, they are now in jeopardy, since the state government has not paid the funds it is entitled to exceed 11 million pesos for the three programmes.
He said that the total investment in these three programs amounted to almost 50 million pesos, of which 25 percent fell on the state. Until now, only Federación and the municipality had contributed funds to the construction work, which is why a delay in the construction termination was to be expected.
"12 million they have to contribute, and even though the year is already coming to an end, they didn't give us a penny, even though they would have had to pay us as early as July," he said. "In order not to be late, we have already started the construction work, and they are already well advanced, but we need the money."
He emphasized that under the government of Jesús Aguilar Padilla, the state had not fulfilled its obligations, which is why the municipality had to take out a loan of 7 million at the beginning of its reign. This would correspond to the amount that the central government would have had to raise.
"This had already happened to us in the last legislative period, because the state did not have sufficient economic resources. The programs continued and this year we had to procure the funds that the state had not raised to participate in these programs," he said.
Even if the state authorities were obliged to disburse these funds, he commented that the Minister for Administration and Finance was requested to speed up the administrative work and disburse the outstanding share as soon as possible.
"Almost 12 million they have to contribute, and even though the year is already coming to an end, they didn't give us a penny."
The Court authorizes the transition to digital television in Mexico for 2015
In recent years, the conversion of analogue to digital signals has been one of the most important changes worldwide.
The Mexican Supreme Court (SCJN) dismissed the constitutional lawsuits filed by the Senate and the House of Representatives against presidial decree, and through which the switchover from analog to digital signals must be brought forward from 2021 to 2015, due to a lack of eight votes required to contest the law.
A minority of four ministers, including the president of the Supreme Court, Juan Silva Meza, were of the opinion that President Felipe Calderón had not exceeded his responsibilities when he issued the Legislative Decree with the details of the switchover to terrestrial digital television.
With this decision, the constitutional dispute initiated by the Senate was rejected, since it did not receive the required qualified majority of eight votes, so that the court could decide on the constitutionality or non-constitutionality. Thus, the law remains in force.
In the lawsuit project drafted by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero, it was proposed to declare the Legislative Decree unconstitutional, as the Comisión Federal de Telecomunicaciones (Cofetel) (Federal Commission for Telecommunications in Mexico) is responsible for the regulations relating to radio and television, which is why the Legislative Decree would be contrary to the hierarchical principles
In favour of the project, only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero themselves agreed.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies filed constitutional complaints against the Legislative Decree. After accepting the lawsuits, Minister Sánchez Cordero no longer applied the Legislative Decree and discontinued all activities for the establishment of digital transmission.
The Council of Ministers also rejected the action filed by the Chamber of Deputies without entering the debate on the project, for which Minister Sánchez Cordero was also responsible.
In his contribution, the Prime Minister stated that he did not agree with the arguments of the project, in the sense that the state government is not allowed to regulate the measures that the public administration is supposed to take with general regulations.
"I also don't think that, if you were to do, the independence of the regulatory body would be compromised," she also reminded that for constitutional reasons the central government is responsible for the planning of strategic policy areas, such as the telecommunications sector, because it directs state affairs.
Replacing the analog signals with digital is one of the most important changes that has taken place around the world in recent years, and it brings about a better use of the radioelectric spectrum, an increased quality of the signal and more channels.
The postponement of the transition, she said, was a deferation of the benefiction of digitization, which was a decision in the public interest, which not only had technical aspects, "because it encompasses economic, social, political and security aspects."
These matters, she emphasized, "necessared the action of the whole system, according to the principle of economic governance by the state, they included a group of characteristics that put the constitution in the hands of the central government."
She recalled that many countries worldwide had already carried out the digital transition, even though they had highly specialized facilities.
One cannot conclude that the government intervenes in the competences of Congress with the release of the contested legislative decree, because the laws passed by the legislature would be complied with," she assured.
The possibility of counting the ballot papers in Michoacán individually is being considered
According to PREP, the difference between the first and the second place in the governor's election is just under 27 percent, with 879 votes of a total of 6074 have not yet been counted.
The possibility of individual counting of ballots is a reality this year in the Michoacán elections, because according to the data issued by Programma de Resultados Electorales Preliminares (PREP), the difference between first and second place in the gubernatorial elections is just under 27 percent, with 879 votes of a total of 6074 have not yet been counted.
In this sense, the President of Consejo General del Instituto Electoral de Michoacán (IEM), Mar�ía de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, has reminded us that, just last 9 November, the electoral office had adopted a series of regulations on the harmonization of the local regulations with the reforms of the states in electoral affairs.
It should be remembered that Michoacán had neither adapted his political constitution nor his electoral law nor his law on electoral justice to the reforms passed by Congress, so that these had not come into force in this round of elections. The Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel avoided their publication in the State Gazette within the deadlines provided for this with the argument of still having objections against it.
On November 9, Mexico's (SCJN) Supreme Court ruled on the constitutional lawsuit filed by the local legislature and judged the vote of the state commissioner on the constitutional reforms as "invalid and unlawful," as he did not have the right to "decide on it or veto it."
María de los Ángeles Llanderal explained in an interview about the possibility of individual counting of the votes that, thanks to the adoption of the directives by the Consejo General del IEM, there would now be the possibility that the local electoral authority could partially or completely count the ballot papers.
Thus, under the following conditions, the votes contained in one or more electoral packages cannot be partially counted The results in the protocols do not agree; the minutes of counting and calculation of votes are not available in file or it is not in the possession of the chairman of the electoral committee or there are obvious errors or modifications in the minutes.
In addition, the total counting of the fully completed voting slips can take place if there is a difference between the presumed winner and he who is in second place, which is equal to or less than one percent, provided that at the beginning of the meeting there is an explicit request from the party or coalition chairman, which is the second of the mentioned candidates.
Vote counting can also take place in its entirety if, after completion of the ordinary calculation, it is determined that the difference between the presumed winner and the nominee in second place is equal or less than one percent, provided that there is an explicit request from a representative of the party whose candidate has finished second. In this case, the electoral packages that have been suspended a partial count would be excluded.
It is worth mentioning that the elections in Morelia are in the same situation, where, according to PREP, the difference between the first and second places is almost 0.16 percent, although the votes of 129 out of a total of 923 electoral protocols have not yet been counted.
His Last Will
His ashes will be scattered over the Zapalinamé mountains, the family confirms.
The remains of pilot Felipe Bacio Cortés trickled down this Sunday evening on Saltillo, his place of birth. On Monday, the death show and a funeral Mass took place.
According to family members, the lieutenant colonel's last will was that his ashes would be scattered across the Zapalinamé mountains.
After an honor, the family of Bacio Cortés, along with the others killed in the accident, returned to the capital Coahuila in an aircraft of the Mexican Air Force. Those closest to the colonel took part at the ceremony in Campo Marte.
At twelve o'clock at noon, the ashes of the lieutenant colonel landed on the funeral hall, which is located on Boulevard Nazario Ortiz Garza, and where the family members and friends came.
At 5:30 p.m., his remains were examined at Saltillo's Technological Institute. This morgue was initiated by his former classmates from the technical high school diploma and his American football team, to which Felipe Bacio belonged during his preparation for high school.
"He wanted to return to Saltillo and that his ashes would be scattered over the Zapalinamé mountains. That's what he had always said to his family and his friends like that," Sandra Bacio Cortés, sister of the pilot, announced.
From her point of view, the lieutenant colonel had fulfilled all his dreams and she is of the opinion that he had stood in the abundance of his life. "He was a successful man, extraordinary as a father and as a son, a loving brother and he always stood up for his family."
Felipe Bacio Cortés left the town of Saltillo very young to enter the school of the Air Force of Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some couldn’t believe it because they thought it was dizzy even on a truck. Nevertheless, he always aspired to realize his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembers him as a good person. "He had hardly arrived in Saltillo, and the whole family wanted to welcome him, as on the occasion he brought Martha Sahagún to Saltillo."
The young man remembers that even at the age of seven, he had driven to Zapopán to graduate his family member. That's why he knows that his uncle died when he did what he liked best: flying a plane.
One last farewell
Lieutenant colonel of the Mexican Air Force, Felipe Bacio Cortés, was passed with all honors under the direction of Governor Jorge Torres López. Their honor granted him the people and the government, the Mexican army of the sixth military area, the Mexican air force, headmasters and pupils of Saltillo's technological educational institution, where he graduated in 1985 and stood out as outstanding in both studies and sports.
Bacio Cortés died a few days ago, when the helicopter he flown, in which the secretary of state José Francisco Blake Mora and six other people from the same authority also sat, crashed.
At 6:05 p.m., the cremated remains of Bacio Cortés, citizens of Saltillo, arrived to be handed over in the forecourt of the barracks from the funeral home to members of the Mexican Air Force, led by Major Jaime Martinez, where his family members, including his daughter Amanda and his wife Cristina, his mother, siblings and other relatives, pupils and teachers, were already expecting him.
The abandoned body was found on a property on the Cadereyta NL
Several hours before the find, military personnel presented eleven people who had been arrested in the same locality in connection with organized crime.
Three men were founded by gunshots into different parts of the bodies and a shot of grace in an abandoned finca in the village of Cadereyta Jiménez, Nuevo León.
The event had taken place at mileage 1.5 of the country road from Cadereyta to Santiago at the height of the municipality of El Castillo.
Several temporaries saw that the door of the country house surrounded by a beige-brown wall stood up wide.
When they looked into the house, they found the corpses of three people about three meters further inside, which they immediately told the relevant authorities.
One wore a yellow t-shirt and dark pants, without shoes; next to him was another dressed in a white top.
Almost a meter away, another person's body lay with red T-shirt and blue trousers.
All three were on their stomachs.
According to the information obtained on site, the three people must have been murdered in another location because no bullet casings were found.
On the wall of the country house you can read inscriptions, which an organized crime gang writes to another, in addition, several bullets were found in the wall and door.
11 people arrested in Cadereyta
Secretaría de la Defensa Nacional (State Secretariat for National Defense) introduced 11 people, including a minor who were part of a criminal gang operating mainly in Cadereyta and associated with kidnappings, murder, halconeo (human chasing) and clandestine funerals.
According to the information, eight people were initially arrested on November 11 in the settlement of Los Alveros and a prisoner was freed.
Three properties and a mobile phone were secured. Those arrested confirmed to be members of the "Los Zetas" criminal group.
On November 12 at 06.00 a.m., military officers found a car without license plates with two inmates on their guard round in the village of Rancho Viejo in Cadereyta.
During the inspection of the car, the military found a long gun, a charger and 18 cartridges.
Later, on November 13 at 2 p.m., one of her accomplices was arrested in the municipality of La Frauda, who tried to flee at the sight of the militaries.
They are blamed on secret burials in the towns of General Terán, China and Cadereyta.
On Wednesday Formation of the New Italian Government
The Prime Minister-designate, Mario Monti, will meet with the President of Italy tomorrow to present to him the new government, which the crisis, which has led Italy to the brink of the economic collapse.
The Prime Minister-designate, Mario Monti, will meet with the President of Italy tomorrow to present to him the new government, which the crisis, which has led Italy to the brink of the economic collapse.
A message from the presidential palace announced that Monti, appointed on Sunday, would meet with the president, Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday, to confirm the formation of the government.
It is assumed that he will present a cabinet composed of technocrats, although it is not yet clear when the new prime minister will take office.
Monti said he would present to the president the results of the talks with the political parties about the formation of the government.
"I would like to reaffirm my absolute confidence in our country's ability to cope with these difficult times," the former EU commissioner told journalists.
Monti did not explicitly confirm the formation of the government, but his comments show that the obstacles have been overcome.
The "frame is well staked out now," he added.
Monti has managed to form a government in less than three days, which is much less time than normal. Meanwhile, Italy is battling a political and financial crisis that has driven the cost of the debt to an unbearable amount.
The new government, led by former EU commissioner Monti, must pass a tough austerity package demanded by euro-politicians to regain ailing confidence in Italy.
The pressure to hassle, which weighs on Monti, intensified by the fluctuating market situation, as a result of which Italian government bonds defaulted 7 per centures on interest for 10 years, equivalent to the state at which Greece and Ireland were forced to apply for the European bailout.
Emma Marcegaglia, president of Italy's Confindustria industry association, told the press after a meeting with Monti: "We'd say we're going to support this government to the best of our ability."
We believe that this government is the last chance for Italy to come out of this emergency.
Monti's chances of success were significantly magnified by Silvio Berlusconi's PDL's support, which had to resign on Saturday as a result of the crisis.
"We believe Professor Monti's efforts are destined to produce good results," Angelino Alfano, secretary of the centre-right party, said.
The support of the PDL, Italy's largest party, is essential, because so far many of its members have spoken out against a multi-the-majority technocrat-made government that Monti is currently forming.
Support from Parliament
Monti's new government needs strong parliamentary support to implement the likely unpopular austerity measures.
Resistance to his efforts or delaying the reforms could once again bring about a devastating attack by the financial markets.
The Italian Association of Foreign Banks also exerts pressure with its hint that a failure of Monti was a disaster.
Monti began on Monday with his debates with political parties, unions and business associations, as well as with youth and women's organisations.
On Tuesday evening, the session round is to be completed.
Monti was appointed this Sunday by Napolitano, who, in response to the crisis, set in motion an extremely rapid change of government.
After a short sigh of relief towards the end of last week, when it was announced that Berlusconi would resign, as a result of uncertainty about the success of the new prime minister, the cost of Italian government bonds has once again reached critical limits.
Rescuing Italy with a government debt of 1.8 trillion euros would be too much for the current EU bailout.
Monti said his government would have to last until the next elections, which are expected to be held in 2013, despite predictions that politicians will only give him time to implement the reforms instead of preferring the elections.
Monti said that he would like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the major parties insist that this should consist only of technicians, an indication of their concerns in view of the procedure imposed by the pressure of the financial markets.
Political sources point out that mutual mistrust and disagreements between the parties complicate the involvement of individuals from political life.
Spaniards have more playing opportunities than the Ticos
