From then on only five came to over 30 games: Keilor Navas with 31, Randall Azofeifa with 32, Michael Barrantes with 31, Carlos Hernández with 36 and José Luis López with 35.
The ones with the fewest games.
Fewer than 30 matches are played by Gerard Piqué (37), Sergio Busquets (36), Santiago Santi Cazorla (32), Álvaro Arbeloa (31) and Raúl Albiol (31).
From the side of the national team come to less than 30 games Roy Miller with 26, Winston Parks with 26, Gabriel Badilla with 25 and Roy Myrie with 23.
The rest isn't even approaching the limit of 20s.
While Nacho Moreal has only four games and Jordi Alba two, in Costa Rica Víctor Bolívar records only one and Ólman Vargas and Edder Nelson haven't had their debut yet.
Spain's storied goalkeeper is David Villa with 50 hits, followed by Fernando Torres with 27.
For Costa Rica, Bryan Ruiz has scored the most goals with nine, two more than Carlos Hernández and three more than Roy Myrie and Parks.
The quintet included Costa Rica in its tour in celebration of its twenty-year anniversary
"Playing in Costa Rica is a dream come true"
In the exclusive conversation with Viva, bassist Jeff Ament of Pearl Jam says the band is prepared to give one of their best concerts next Sunday at National Stadium.
They were expected for more than 20 years, but the band assures that a dream comes true for them when they are playing in Costa Rica.
That's what the group's bassist and co-founder Jeff Ament, who gave an exclusive interview to Viva last week from Brail.
The band is located in the South American part of their tour in celebration of their twenty-year anniversary and on Sunday, the 20th, they make their only stop in Central America.
On this day, they will perform from 19.30 at the Nationalstadion together with the band The X from Los Angeles and Las Robertas from Costa Rica.
They read an excerpt from the conversation, the full transcript of which can be found in La Nación's online edition.
For your Costa Rican fans, a dream comes true when Pearl Jam is set in Costa Rica: Are you aware of what this means for your fans?
Playing in Costa Rica is a dream come true for us.
That's just the second time we've played in South and Central America, and so far it's been great.
I've only been on holiday in Costa Rica once. It's such a beautiful country that we'll probably stay there for a few more days.
It's exciting for us to bring our music there.
I ask you this because the fans of the band are completely devoted. They experience the days before the concert intensely.
What do you tell your most avid fans?
Thank you for standing by our side all these years.
It took us almost 21 years to get to Costa Rica. Thank you to have waited so long and we’re sorry to take so long until we came here.
Some people even think that your concert will be the biggest show of the year.
Doesn't that put some pressure out of your visit?
Some of the greatest shows we've put down over the last 21 years took place in the last two weeks.
We've been playing on a few giant shows in Sao Paulo and we're hoping they made us fit for this concert in Costa Rica.
Really, we're keen to play there.
We love this country, we like to surf and are thrilled by the jungle. Costa Rica is one of the best countries in the world.
How did you get to know Costa Rica and its waves for surfing?
Many of our friends are surfers and I have a great friend who lives in Tamarindo, where the waves are simply unimaginable.
My friends always went surfing Costa Rica and Nicaragua. Together with Mexico, these are excellent areas for surfing. In this way, about people who love surfing, we learned about it.
With a look at the tour, you have offered huge shows with many songs.
What plans do you have for the event in Costa Rica?
Since we've never played there before, we don't set boundaries either.
We’ll try to give the best possible show with a lot of different songs. We want to play a few songs from every album and offer our fans a good and versatile repertoire.
You will never repeat your repertoire.
Why? Why?
I think that’s partly because we have nine records and we can play over 120 songs. Everyone likes a different song, so the decision, which one we will play, isn’t easy.
If we record two or three songs that we haven’t played for a long time, we can better maintain our concentration and they surprise us because they remind us of the reasons why we liked these songs. Otherwise, they could be forgotten.
This makes the show more interesting for us and I think that’s why it’s for the audience.
With this tour you will celebrate your twentieth history.
If you look back, do you find it difficult to believe in your success?
Yes, totally.
First of all, I believe that none of us has imagined playing in a great rock band for more than more than 20 years.
We continue to rely on this blessing and think it’s great to be able to make music together.
We like each other and are thrilled to produce music.
We’re lucky to be able to go to the places in the world we’ve always wanted to visit.
Things couldn't stand better.
What did it mean for you to actively participate in the birth of the grunge?
It has always been an honor to belong to this group of big bands.
We're good friends with the people of Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice in Chains.
It's surprising and plentiful when a movement emerges from a group of friends.
From time to time, a band emerges from a city, but it's already unusual when five or ten groups come from the same city at the same time.
We're proud and honored to be part of the Seattle group.
We still play together sometimes.
Last year we were at a festival with Alice in Chains and in Canada with Mudhoney and we saw Soundgarden on her tour. We’re excited to still still be friends with all of them and it honors us to be part of this emerging Seattle sound.
It’s also very uncommon for a band like yours with the same musicians to stay together for so long.
Yes, four of us have been playing together for 21 years and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago. That alone is much more time than many bands survive.
First of all, I believe that we are all lucky to still be alive and we also take care of each other.
We are all close friends, which is why musical paths have opened up that give each individual the freedom to experiment.
One trusts the other and his music. We couldn’t do better.
Your relationship with fame has not always been easy. On the other hand, thanks to your success, you have always been able to do what you wanted.
How do you find balance?
The way we've grown has made it easier for us to find a balance.
We're sure we don't tour too often, because it's too easy to lapse into this game with huge crowds who love your music.
As long as you think that people have come because of the music, it reminds you that you have to pay attention to the music and what it has created without being obsessive about being a famous rock star.
What memories do you have of your other Mother Love Bone project?
I think we wrote very good songs and at the beginning it was great.
We've been a band for three years that had never edited a record until the death of Andy (Andrew Woods).
We’ve been disappointed to have taken so long. We’re still good with Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore), who built up the band with us. When I listen to a song or see photos, I’m thinking of what an unbelievable person Andy was and how he made me laugh. It’s always been a joy to go to work, to laugh together, and to talk about music and a lot
He was an extraordinary person and we miss him.
Domestic band Las Robertas are responsible for opening the show in Costa Rica.
Is it true that you know their work, especially Eddie Vedder, and that's why the band picked for the opening?
Yes, the production sent us videos from several groups and we liked this band best. That’s why we’re curious to see what Las Robertas can offer live. That’s going to be exciting.
Why do you always change Yellow Ledbetter's lyrics when you're playing live?
That's a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), I don't know.
He sings him, and I think he sings the first thing he can think of.
By whom have you been influenced as a bassist?
Of so many+
The first to come to mind are Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski...
It's so many that I could talk about the bassists I like all afternoon.
How did Pearl Jam manage to keep the grunge alive, even though it's no longer a fad?
I don't think all of us feel very connected to the word grunge.
When I think about it, I can think of the bands I’ve mentioned before, such as Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana and everyone else who came up at the same time. If they’re still important today, I think that’s great, because it’s worth listening to these music and bands like Mudhoney are making better music now than ever before.
At first her music was darker, now she is more positive.
Why? Why?
That’s interesting, because I think there’s a few songs on the new album that might seem a bit frivolous. But there are also other songs like The End or Just Breathe, which are classified as very dark songs, even though they also have something positive about them.
Tears came to both of me when I heard Ed she was playing. They're very dark songs.
I believe that as you get older and something traumatic happened in your life - e.g. if you lose a family member or a friend - that affects your artistic work and your music and these two songs clearly embody this for me.
But I also believe that you can be a happy person and still make dark music.
Peru:
Fujimori came back to the hospital for medical examinations
Former President Alberto Fujimori was admitted to a public hospital on Tuesday to investigate the muscle loss of his legs, his GP informed.
72-year-old Fujimori, who serves 25 years imprisonment for violating human rights during his reign (1990-2000), has several conditions, including tongue cancer, which operates four times and is under control.
Alejandro Aguinaga, a member of Congress and his personal physician, told television that Fujimori would be examined for three days at the Peruvian hospital Instituto Nacional de Enfermedades Neoplásicas to determine the reason for his pain and muscle loss in the lower bodies.
Aguinaga commented that, in his opinion, Fujimori could be pardoned for humanitarian reasons. Nevertheless, he did not wise this because he considered him innocent.
He has been operated on tongue cancer four times and he has lost 18 kilos of weight. In addition, there are other diseases such as severe erosive gastritis, a cyst in the pancreas, kidney stones, high blood pressure and blood circulation problems in the legs, the doctor enumerated.
Fujimori has been at the Dirección Nacional de Operaciones Especiales de la policía prison since September 2007.
In April 2009, he was sentenced to 25 years in prison for the murder of 25 people by the death squadron of the Peruvian army, who secretly worked during his reign.
Mujica travels to Mexico to strengthen political relations
An official delegation led by Uruguayan President José Mujica and consisting of several ministers and entrepreneurs will start their visit from Mexico on Tuesday to expand trade between the two countries and discuss political issues of the region
Mujica will meet with his Mexican counterpart Felipe Calderón next Wednesday. That, according to the Uruguayan president, will be the culmination of his fully booked schedule with politicians, businessmen and a meeting with the Uruguayans living in Mexico.
The meeting of both presidents in Guadalajara "is suitable for exchanging information about all policy areas between the two countries," the South American country's presidential office announced on its website.
According to local media, Mujica is seeking Calderón's support regarding the incidence with France, when French Prime Minister Nicolas Sarkozy put the South American country on a list of tax havens at the G20 summit in the French city of Cannes.
In his discourse, Sarkozy threatened to exclude those countries that still act as tax havens from the international community. This declaration was rejected by Uruguay, prompting a request from his ambassador to the European country.
Mexico has chaired the G20 since November, a group of the world’s most powerful emerging and industrialized countries. Since January this year, there has been an agreement on the exchange of tax information to avoid double assessment with the South American country.
In recent years, the Government of Uruguay has tried to speed up the concretization of agreements for the exchange of tax information in order to be removed from the "grey list" of the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD). This includes countries that have not introduced all international standards for cooperation in tax matters.
At the business level, there has been a free trade agreement between Mexico and Uruguay since 2004, which has contributed to a growing exchange of goods between the two countries.
The mustared installer enters the third dimension with "Super Mario 3D Land"
For 25 years, Super Mario has tirelessly struggled to save Princess Peach out of the claws of the evil Bowser, but until now never in three dimensions: in "Super Mario 3D Land," he fights for the girl's love in stereo technology.
The game, which comes to the counters all over the world this month, has been tailored from the outset to the features of the Nintende 3DS wearable console, which can be played three-dimensionally without special glasses.
"Mario is the figure who has to say how stereoscopy works without glasses," assured Omar Álvarez, the person in charge of the press specializing in Nintendo in Spain at the presentation of the video game in Madrid.
Álvarez explained that "Super Mario 3D Land" is "the first video game from Nintendo that has been developed for this console from the outset", because until now successful articles such as "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of 3D" were adjustments of already existing titles.
Nintendo's spokesperson underlined that although it's three-dimensional, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "affordable" video game that allows for a fast, fluid game.
Álvarez confirmed that "Super Mario 3D Land" contained two games in one: the first part is "easy" to capture the less-seized users.
After that, the experts about the different levels of the games, which are becoming increasingly difficult, can take a "second round."
In addition, users can choose in real-time between an "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of the action. However, to overcome the obstacles without becoming a victim of the "optical effects" must always be played three-dimensionally.
This outingression Super Marios into the three-dimensional world also means the figure's return to a portable console, which has not been the case since 2005.
With this video gamble, Nintendo winks at the fans of the saga and repeats the recipe for success of the last editions: The mustached installer has to overcome platforms peppered with danger in order to free Princess Peach.
However, the player encounters news about the movements, the environment, the disguises and the enemies - and a "playable" Luigi.
Colom vouches for Portillo's extradition to the United States
The president of Guatemala, Álvaro Colom, announced this Tuesday that he would grant the extradition of former governor Alfonso Portillo so that he is responsible in the United States for the conspiracy to launder money of US$70 million.
Thus, the trial on the request of the USA for extradition of Portillo ends, so that it can be brought to justice there.
In a press conference, Colom confirmed that his decision was based on the independence of judicial authority and the government, taking into account that a decision-making court had already ordered the extradition.
He stressed that he had not interfered in court decisions during his administration and had upholded all the extradition requests of which he was informed.
In addition to Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín is extradited for murder and aggravated assault, Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes, both for drug trafficking.
Colom’s action is an act of government that takes place when all judicial and constitutional instances are exhausted for the conviction of the former state president (2000 -2004).
Pursuant to the decisions of the Fifth Criminal Court and the Guatemalan Constitutional Court, Portillo must be extradited if his legal situation in Guatemala has been clarified and his human rights are guaranteed.
Portillo was acquitted for embezzling 120 million Quetzales in the Ministry of Defense during his reign. This trial preceded extradition, but the minister objected and revived the case.
Trinijove has helped 6000 young people threatened by social exclusion
The organisation has been launched to avoid the marginalisation of young people without vocational training.
The first 25 years of life of the Trinijove social welfare organization have led to important results: in this quarter-century, 6000 young people threatened by social exclusion have been supported in order to find a way out of their difficult situation.
In addition, there are more than a hundred jobs created for this social group.
On the occasion of the anniversary, an event was taken yesterday at the former factory buildings of Fabra i Coats, which was attended by more than 300 people.
Trinijove was launched in 19686 in the Trinitat Vella district with the aim of helping young people with a low level of training, without work, with drug problems that were threatened by the risk of marginality.
At yesterday's celebration in Sant Andreu were attended by numerous officials of institutions and institutions who worked with the association during these 25 years.
The participation of political representatives of different directions during election campaign times proved the impartiality of the work carried out by Trinijove.
In addition to the president of the Generalitat (Parliament of the Autonomous Region of Catalonia), Artur Mas, and his predecessor Jordi Pujol took part in the festivities of the former socialist education minister Ernest Maragall and the former mayor and currently left-green city councillor Ricard Goma.
The large hall of Fabra i Coats was also visited by the current Catalan Minister of Social Affairs and Family Affairs, Josep Lluís Cleries, and the director-general of the social work of the savings band La Caixa, Jaume Lanaspa.
Mas emphasized that Trinijove was launched in very difficult times and that today's times are no less complicated. But he added that the work done so far is "a good foundation."
For the president of Generalitat, Trinijove is a role model who knows how to fight against difficult circumstances and gives hope to people in trouble.
"The economic crisis is nothing new for the club and the work it does," he explained.
Occupied house, where giant parties were organized, was vacated
Homeowners had occupied 11 of the 13 apartments in the building on Balmes No. 51 (Barcelona).
The twelve squatters left the building yesterday afternoon without resisting.
Everything points out that the nightmares of the neighbors of the house no. 51 on the street Balmes have come to an end.
Twelve people voluntarily left the building yesterday at noon, which they illegally occupied and in which they had organized huge festivals that had driven the neighborhood to despair. The eviction took place by judicial order without any police intervention.
After eviction, the door lock was replaced to avoid a similar situation.
"From now on, entry will be controlled," the house trustee confirmed.
The squatters, who had occupied 11 of the building's 13 apartments - the rest were inhabited - took the entire electric system and part of the water supply. Also, they had painted the walls and left behind all kinds of rubbish, especially cans of alcoholic drinks.
Huge parties were celebrated in the three lower floors, which lasted up to two days and drove tenants to despair.
Next to the building is a discotheque, Balmes 51, which also suffered damage.
Her owner, Maria Pantinat, explained to Europa Press that her business had declined by 80% since the start of the squat.
The owner of the discotheque pointed out that the squatters made their unfair competition, because they sold the drinks to three euros, while these cost 10 euros in the discotheque.
In addition, the owner of the leisure restaurant had been reported by the neighbors because of disturbance of peace, while according to her statement the noise did not come from the visitors of the disco, but from the squatters and in particular from the guests of the festivals, which were organized there, and which, according to statements of the angry neighbors in recent weeks, became more and longer.
Those in charge of the discotheque explained that the harassment had been so great that it agreed with the owner of the building not having to pay rent for the last nine months.
Guardia Urbana (urban police) also confirmed that it has received more complaints from its neighbours in recent weeks.
The chairman of the PP (Partido Popular - People's Party) in Barcelona's city councie, Alberto Fernández Díaz, had demanded last week to clear the building immediately and tackle the problems of civic coexistence as well as coming down the district.
He explained that before the summer only one floor of the building had been occupied, but that the squatters had penetrated into all floors of the building in recent months and almost all apartments. In some there had even been thefts.
In this sense, he added that "it is necessary to put an end to impunity and to crack down on the asocial acts in the building."
Yesterday, another building in the Nou Barris district was cleared by the Catalan police.
At 3:30 p.m., police approached Montral No. 35 street after receiving a call from a neighbor who witnessed four people trying to enter the building.
The four were arrested immediately.
Park or mountain?
The architects agree with the tender, apart from the disputes over the fees.
The municipal tender for the 16 gates opens the debate about the importance of Collserola in Barcelona.
In the mid-1980s, at the culmination of the meanwhile disappeared Corporación Metropolitana de Barcelona (CMB), this administration published its insult on the promotion of Collserola.
The brochure described the mountain as a huge park in Barcelona city.
He has even been compared in numbers with New York Central Park.
With the demise of CMB in 1987, the park in the eyes of many became the upper limit of Barcelona, because the capital of Catalonia is one of the few that does not spread north-south, but (mountains) and below (sea).
Collserola's classification as a nature park plays with this idea.
As a role model no more now served Central Park, but Yellowstone with wild boars in the role of yogi bear.
That’s how far, when the city issued a huge tender in September, consisting of 16 competitions, for more access gates to Collserola.
Undoubtedly, one gate refers to the concept of a park.
It's either like this, or someone intends to put goals in the countryside.
The question arises of itself: Collserola, Park or Berg?
The tender is not only from an organisational point of view, but also with regard to a reflection on the citizen, the topic of conversation in the mouth of all architects from Barcelona.
It's almost a recurrent issue that two members of this old-established caste, who meet in the elevator, can draw on.
And it is precisely this fact that provides the material for one of the rare correspondences between what three architects from Barcelona think about it and some university professors who have applied for the project.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Modol and Marta Bayona: The tender is appropriate.
For Roldán "it is necessary" to talk about the "front on the mountains" after the attack on the coast, as far as the discourse of Ildefons Cerda, who has not been carried out about the green apples.
"One should start from the principle that Barcelona's largest park is the beach.
Among others, because it is easily accessible.
Collserola is more complex," judges the architect, for whom the title "Gates of Collserola" contains a "lexical trap": it must not be "envisioned as entrances to a park, but as a confinement of areas in which action must be taken."
Modol, like Roldán and Bayona with urban planning experience, regards the proposal as an "intellectual challenge" that carries "some dangers."
Dangers due to the "uncertainty that the designation of a public space entails today" according to the experiences from the 1980s, which had their "swan song" in the area of the forum.
The first fear is one’s own limitation to what can be developed.
"To limit something always means to determine the next step," i.e. to cross it.
"You have to think about new urban planning tools, because the current [receiving the plan General Metropolitano (PGM) ] can lead to a real disaster."
Modol, who sticks more closely to the question, simplifies the side of Collserola, which is heading towards Vallesana, "can be understood as a park that does not in the direction of Barcelona," for purely topographical reasons.
Modol recalls that there is already a study from the patronage of Collserola that should not "fall on deaf ears," even though he is grateful to the city government for giving his panel the opportunity to participate in this project "from the very first moment."
In addition, he notes that the proximity of the mountain to the city is the escort letter, which allows the architects to participate in the debate, even though he expresses his "doubts" about whether this technical profile should guide the multi-disciplinary teams launched ("exactly the right thing," he says) "We architects should only be a tool."
Bayona hints that the answer to the question in the headline of this article is complex, as the tendering conditions lack a global view of Collserola: "There have been 16 competitions advertised in cross-section," he says, "on the mountain-sea axis. But there is no longitudinal perspective looking at the area in its entirety."
That, at least in his case, has led him to participate in the 16 competitions.
Otherwise, the program and the definition of a project for a gate would collide with that of the architect of the gate next to it.
In his opinion, the city should definitely guarantee that the strip, directly above the Ronda de Dalt, is equipped in its entirety in such a way as to create a soft transition between the city and green zones.
Casino awards Jonás Larrazábal who comes free
After 72 days of silence, Casino Red Plenipotentiary freed Monterrey's mayor's brother from the trial against him for alleged extortion.
After 72 days of imprisonment, Manuel Jonás Larrazábal is released after Casino Red's plenipotentiary has forgiven him before the District's Second Criminal Court, which leads the trial of the mayor's mayor of Monterrey for extortion.
After the assassination of Casino Royale, Sergio Gil García, the owner of Casino Red, published a series of photos and videos showing Manuel Jonás allegedly forcing him to pay a sum of money so that the city government approves the secret operation of the betting house.
According to the criminal complaint, the public prosecutor's office opened criminal proceedings against the panistic mayor's brother, who was arrested for 30 days on September 2.
After four hours with Ministerio Público 3 en Delitos Patrimoniales, he was taken to Casa de Arraigo 1 at Barrio Antiguo of the capital.
At this location, he remained 49 days after a second arrest warrant after Ministerio Público's offense had discarded the offences for bribery and organised crime.
On October 19, he was transferred to Cadereyta's Detention Centre for allegedly bribing the owners of Casino Red with one and a half-million pesos. On the other hand, he remained only a few hours in the medium-security wing, as there were death threats against him.
Authorities allowed him to take part in legal proceedings as an inmate of San Nicolás de los Garza's municipal prison.
On October 25, he was served a detention ordinance by the Secretary of the Second Criminal Court of the District, Jorge Yánez, who found that there was sufficient evidence after the nearly two-month-long investigations to blame Jonás for the payment of 1.5 million pesos to the representatives of the betting house in order to be able to open it in return.
This Monday, Casino Red's plenipotentiary, Víctor Aldo García Gómez asked Judge José Luis Pecina, the mayor's brother, took the penalty, which is why he will be released in the next few hours.
During the judicial hearing, Ramiro Arias, the official of Ministerio Público assigned to the tribunal, García Gómez, asked whether he had repaired the damage and those of the owner of the betting house had expressed his forgiveness. However, the lawyer withstood the name.
According to Case 197/2011, Jonás Larrazábal is acquitted. There are also no objections to the triality below, and no damages will be demanded.
After the attendance of the representative of the betting house at about 15.50, Larrazabal Bretón was immediately released.
Court secretary Jorge Y�ánez drove to San Nicolás de los Garza municipal prison to tell Jonás that he had been acquitted and that his case would be filed.
He also informed him of the judicial order for immediate release.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome announces details of the plane accident in which government secretary Francisco Blake and another seven people were killed.
Secretaría de Comunicaciones y Transportes (SCT) informed that the investigation results into the accident, in which government secretary Francisco Blake Mora and another seven people died, indicated that the helicopter, with its entire unharmed structure, bounced directly against the ground without the pilot being able to initiate an emergency landing.
At a press conference, Dionisio Pérez J�ácome, Secretary of Communications and Transport, mentioned that the pilot had confirmed before take-off that the weather conditions were suitable for the flight.
"The fact that the propeller blades had contact with trees at a height of 25 meters, with the helicopter losing the hull, seems to indicate that the helicopter had had several ground contacts."
He also explained that the helicopter was built for 19 occupants, which is why he met all the requirements for transporting the personnel.
In addition, he said no helicopter parts were found during investigations in the area where the plane had come down.
"No aircraft parts were found elsewhere during the search, the surcharge affected the entire structure.
The helicopter was built for 19 occupants, which is why it met all the requirements for passenger transport.
The area has an abrasion of 7 to 30 degrees.
At present, it is a priority to get the test results as soon as possible, but this could take several months, perhaps even a year.
For his part, Gilberto López Meyer, director-general of Aeropuertos y Servicios Auxiliares (Secretariat for Aircraft and Relief Services in Mexico), assured that the helicopter had flown by the Estado Mayor Presidencial on the surcharge with cruising speed and the plane had fallen onto a soft slope.
"This information strengthens the assumption that it was a normal flight operation at the time of the impact."
At a press conference, the head of the SCT, Pérez Jácome, listed six relevant points that should be taken into account during the investigation:
visual flight:
In line with flight standards, it was a flight according to the rules of visual flight. This means that the crew should have had sufficient visibility during the flight and on the ground.
weather conditions:
The AICM recorded suitable weather conditions at take-off. During the onward flight towards the border area between Mexico City and the Mexico State, some low-hanging clouds drew up.
Called flight routes:
It is possible that because of the clouds, the crew had selected a lower area with better visibility towards the Cuernavaca Valley.
Impingement Site:
The collision coordinates and the last radar recording of the helicopter are consistent. Possibly there was neither a loss of control over the aircraft nor a deviation from the flight route before the impact.
Scattering the remains of the plane in a limited area:
The information available up to now indicates that the helicopter had impacted from a straight and lateral direction.
The pattern of the scattered aircraft remains suggests that the helicopter, with its unscathed structure, had bounced onto the ground.
No evidence of blaze or explosion:
The remains of the aircraft offer at the scene of the accident do not inflict fire or explosion.
The latest assembly results point to several ground contacts.
During the on-site investigations, aircraft parts were found at an altitude of 9200 feet above sea level. The site is sloping and has a slope of seven to thirty degrees.
The photos show the sequence in which the helicopter has probably broken.
Some information suggests that the helicopter may have had several ground contacts.
After a meticulous search in an area that went beyond the cordoned-off area at the scene of the accident, no scattered aircraft parts could be found, which reinforces the assumption that the intact aircraft structure had bounced against the ground.
MSS Announces a Research Lecture on Finding the Gene for Obesity
She social security institute of Mexico leads a research project to find the gene for obesity in children and adolescents, which is supported by IPN specialists.
The Instituto Mexicano del Seguro Social Social - IMSS (Social Insurance Institution of Mexico) announced that it is leading a research project on the discovery of the gene for childhood and adolescencies under the title "Genetics of obesity in childhood and adolescency," with which the genetic markers are to be found in conjunction with overweight.
Miguel Cruz López, Head of Unidad de Investigación Médica en Bioquímica (Department of Medical Research, Biochemistry) at the Hospital de Especialidades del Centro Médico Nacional Siglo XXI explained that an interdisciplinary team consisting of doctors, nurses, nutrition specialists and social workers has been established.
This team is supported by specialists and students of the post-graduate degree program at Instituto Nacional de Salud Pública (INSP) and at the Centro de Investigación y de Estudios Avanzados (Cinvestav) del Instituto Politécnico Nacional (IPN).
The population is invited to participate in the study. Interested parties can come to the sports fields of the IMSS in the valley of Mexico, where tests with children and adolescents are carried out every Saturday from 8.00 a.m.
He emphasized that the investigation began on the Cuauhtémoc sports field (in the west of México state) and has been continuing since October 8 on the Nezahualcóyolt sports field (in the east of México state).
If the target of 500 to 600 investigators is reached, the study will continue on Independencia's sports field (in the south of the capital district) and be completed on that of Morelos (in the north of the capital's district).
He explained that the aim of the study is to get to know the factors and the role of genetics in connection with the development of obesity and the identification of risk factors of Mexican children and adolescents for the prevention of chronic degenerative diseases.
On the occasion of the worldwide Diabetes Day, he explained that it was sought that three thousand children and adolescents between the ages of six to 14 should fill out nutrition-related questionnaires with the consent and under the supervision of their parents.
They are also asked questions about their physical activity and family history in order to control risk factors that could result in diabetes or renal insufficiency.
He also stated that on these Saturdays the investigators were measured (weight, size, waist circumference), their blood pressure controlled and blood values such as glucose levels, triglycerides, cholesterol, insulin and genetic factors (attempting the identification of genes associated with childhood obesity).
Cruz López stressed that this investigation would be carried out on the occasion of health workers' concerns about the alarming weight gain of young people.
"The health surveys from 1999 and 2006 showed that in just under six years the proportion of obese boys had increased by 77 percent and that of girls with obesity by 47 percent. Overweight was also increasing dramatically," the IMSS specialist stressed.
He stated that the search for genetic markers was justified because the genetic history of the population of Mexico differed from that of other peoples.
He stated that an (e) Mexican (in) is on average 65 percent Native American, 30 percent European and 5 percent of African ancestry, which is reflected in the genes associated with diabetes and overweight.
In general, he explained that the genetics of obesity are expressed in the inability to burn the excess of the energy (calories) taken from us, so that it is stored in the form of fat.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit to the study participants consisted in a comprehensive examination of the children, which would determine whether they had a degree of overweight or obesity.
It would even examine the occurrence of dark skin wrinkles in the neck and inner arm (acanthosis), which indicate the likelihood of metabolic disorders and diabetes in the pre-stage.
All information would be treated confidentially and communicated to the parents in writing by the doctors. The doctors explain to the parents all measurement results and suggest changes in the lifestyle that are appropriate for the prevention of diseases.
Cruz López explained that there are three fundamental factors responsible for overweight, obesity and other chronic diseases: lack of exercise, i.e
The high calorie intake, i.e. an increased consumption of sugared beverages and carbohydrates (tamales, bread, pizza, hamburgers, etc.) together with a high fat and protein intake as well as the genetics to be analyzed in this research project.
Tarmaspole from UNAM prevents potholes
Rafael Herrera, a researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry at UNAM University of Mexico, is using polymers to make the mixture more resistant to weather conditions and temperature fluctuations
Were to pay attention to the signs emanating from the road surface, it would not be necessary to repair almost 200 000 potholes if the rainy season in the Zona Metropolitana del Valle de México (city area of the valley of Mexico) breaks out, said Rafael Herrera Nájera, a researcher at the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) at the University of UNAM, warned and assured that if the laying out and the maintenance of the pavement
In his presentation "Potholes from a technical point of view", which he gave on the occasion of the International Year on Chemistry, he announced that the laboratory for which he is responsible is developing asphalts modified with polymers in order to produce a mixture similar to the asphalt, which, however, has greater mechanical resistance at both high and low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera felt that this type of modified asphalt should be used in areas with an extremely hot climate, such as in some cities in the north of the country, in order to improve the behaviour of the road surface and thus avoid the occurrence of potholes.
He explained that the tarmac - the heaviest part of petroleum - is a hydrocarbon compound, part of which has a high molecular weight - the so-called alphenes - and the other part is a low - the so-called malthenes. Together, they give the road surface its characteristic tough-elastic behavior.
Tough-elastic behavior of the asphalt means that it behaves like a liquid at high temperatures of almost 180°C, at 120° C. it is very viscous and almost solid at 50° C., while it becomes brittle at very low temperatures.
"This means that the resistance of the asphalt on the roads is not uniform, but changes in one day depending on weather conditions and temperature fluctuations," the chemical engineer explained.
FQ's researcher noted that Mexico City's streets mostly had a flexible road surface composed of six layers of material, of which the last three contained asphalt.
Between the top layers there was a wear layer of asphalt, which consisted of small stones bathed in asphalt and then solidified.
Every single stone of this wear layer was bathed in asphalt.
If the vehicles move towards it and forces are generated, this layer enables a good distribution of forces in the road surface.
"If it were modified asphalt, the forces would spread even better," the chemical engineer said.
"Above the wear layer," he explained, "a final layer is laid out, the so-called support layer, which consists of tiny stones, i.e. of sand, which was also bathed in asphalt.
Since this layer is in contact with the vehicle tires, they require a greater mechanical force than the wear layer and it must produce friction.
If this road surface receives and distributes most of the forces, the support layer also requires an appropriate thickness and the material of which it consists must be maintained.
The weather conditions, especially humidity and temperature fluctuations, are further, important factors to consider, especially the water," he said, "because it significantly impairs the behavior of the wear layer.
When the terrain becomes damp, water penetrates into the layer and emerges again with difficulty. This causes softening of all layers of the road surface and, as a result, the appearance of potholes.
If a pothole occurs," he said, "the top layer is first removed, while the wear layer is only affected afterwards. But the damage does not occur immediately, but it is a process that would have to be maintained at the first signs caused by appropriate maintenance programs."
The member of Sistema Nacional de Investigadores (National Research System of Mexico) explained that the vehicle load was another important cause for the occurrence of potholes. Therefore, the thickness of each layer of the road surface must always be in relation to the vehicle load and it must be ensured that this regulation is adhered to in order to avoid frequent potholes.
Rafael Herrera pointed out that there was still a lot of research, for example about asphalt emulsions, which are already on the market in other countries, and with which the potholes could be repaired simply and without the application of high temperatures.
What lackeded the candidate del Valle to be the new Miss Colombia?
Analysis of the factors that had an impact that Melina Ramírez Serna didn't get the crown of Miss Colombia.
El Valle receives its 14th mistitle.
Once again, Valle del Cauca went out empty-handed in awarding the crown to Miss Colombia.
This time, Melina Ramírez Serna had to be content to stand on the winning podium, flaunting a silver crown and the band she identifies as the new Miss Colombia.
With the result preserved this night in Cartagena, where Miss Atlántico took the crown and the cedar, the departamento already comes to 14 mistreades and Melina joins the legion of women from Valle del Cauca, who had held the crown almost in their hands. Among the proclaimed Misses are: Miriam Ospina Benoit (1947), Clara Domínguez
The big question that many are asking today is: What was missing?
Because the result corresponds to the decline of a great favorite, who had shown at every performance that she had everything to be the new Miss Colombia. It was not for nothing that she had been chosen to the Queen of the police and Mejor Rostro Jolie of Vogue, titles who had also received the beauty queens Taliana Vargas and Natalia Navarro, and who took home the crown of Miss Colombia.
The first factor, which is considered crucial to the jury’s decision to appoint her first miss, is that she had not convinced the answer to the question “Which book influenced you the most and why?”, which had been asked by Martín Murillo Gómez of Carreta Literaria Leamos.
The young woman from Valle del Cauca replied:
"I read a lot, I pretty much believe in literature, I think it's a way to civilize, in the end I believe that the book, which for my childhood shaped the most, is `The Little Prince', is a rather profound book.
Right now, I'm reading `guilt and atonement'. It's a book of universal culture that we should all read. I'm reading a book by Irene Nemirovski, a Russian, a beautiful book calling `passions'.
A response that encountered little counter-loving, firstly because of the term `civilize itself' and secondly, because it had not concretely explained how each book she mentioned had influenced it.
But after completing the ceremony, Melina declared to feel calm and happy with the result: "The universe is wise, it happens what needs to happen.
And for all the people, please read a bit `The Little Prince'. It's a great book.
Among other things, it is said that the darling of El Valle is burned out.
A Bogota journalist hinted that the fact that she was the preference who received all the applauses and who had received all the prizes in the run-up (gastronomy competition Easter, audience vote on the best hand-tailed costume and Reina Madre), "had made the other contestants jealous, which the queen stressed, who therefore couldn't shine on stage."
And you noticed this at their first performance in a swimsuit on the beach of the Hilton hotel and in their two elevators at the coronation celebration.
Even though she certainly appeared in both, she lacked the strength and momentum that the representatives of Magdalena and Atlántico flaunted.
Ariel Osorio, a journalist of the "Como en casa" program at Channel RCN, said that the fact that Melina was crowned Miss "shows that the contestant, who wins the most prizes during the competition, will not become queen after all."
In addition, at the end of the press, Valle went out of the way - a mistake.
Another hypothesis is that Valle was unable to convince Jurie regarding her interest in social work, which is ultimately the essence of the National Beauty pageant.
There will already be a reason that two days prior to compiling international jury Lucy Doughty declared they were looking for a woman who was committed to their country's social affairs.
"Beauty alone is not enough for Miss Colombia. You have to look beyond the physical and focus on the social feeling that she had worked on projects even before participating in the competition," she explained.
It is also speculated that Valle had sinned with her defects of derriere and fibrosis, which was noted to her in the gastric region. But this is contradictory, because the jury chose her as a candidate for `natural beauty' with a healthy body, good eating habits and responsible physical work.
The coach of the queens, Jorge Hernán Orozco, admits that Melina didn't have the best bodies, that there were better, "but her problem with the fibrosis on the belly, which for me honestly was hardly noticeable, squeezed her score on the last day."
In the meantime, journalist Guido Hoyos of the magazine Cromos is of the opinion that the error of the woman from Valle del Cauca was the surgical procedures she had undergone.
"The surgeries are ok, but Valle didn't know how to get her done right.
Her body has never been her strong side, and she's had to pay this place.'
Because of this prize, which will be awarded for the first time by the National Beauty Competition, a cruel prediction arose after appearing in the swimsuit, which was to come true at night. It has been whispered that Raymundo Melina had given a consolation prize so as not to let her enjoy the crown.
Whether that's true or not, the fact is Melina's year of rule starting today, not as queen, but as a princess, a position that offers her the opportunity to represent Colombia at one of the international competitions on appointment of the National Beauty Contest.
However, as one of the queens who, despite her overwhelming appearance for Cartagena, she will go down in history.
Such was the work of the doctor who identified the body of Alfonso Cano
A lophoscopist from Cali went down in history as the man who identified "Alfonso Cano."
The work of these officials plays a key role.
Last November 4, a lophoscopist of the investigative unit of the Prosecutor's Office (CTI) from Cali as the man confirming the identity of FARC (Fuerzas Armadas Revolucionarias de Colombula ′′′): "Alfonso Cano" by comparing the fingerprints.
Of the seven lophoscopists in the CTI's department, this week Eliazar González had volunteered on duty, a man from Puerto Tejada who has been involved in criminal investigative proceedings for 21 years.
At the beginning of his career González worked in communities of Chocó, where he frequently had to venture into the jungle for five days to reach the place where the corpses he was supposed to identify.
In 2000 he moved to Valle and after completing the required courses he specialized in lophoscopy, which is the examination of the skin exposed to friction, i.e
In the week of use against the so-called Alfonso Cano, Eliazar was advised to pack clothes for at least three days.
Something big awaited him.
Friday night he flew from Palmira to Cauca in a helicopter.
He knew that he should identify two bodies, and that there were suspicions that one of the man who had directed the world's oldest guerrilla, which is why he was supposed to take Guillermo León Sáenz (his actual name).
However, when he reached the place where the corpse was located, he could not identify it due to his physical appearance, because this did not resemble the illustrations he had seen in the mass media.
He didn't have a beard, was dressed like a farmer of the area, didn't wear glasses and was much thinner.
But fingerprints don't lie.
Armed with a magnifying glass, ink, planting trowel and a flashlight, Eliazar compared the corpse's fingerprints to those on the imprint sheet.
He had seen correctly: it was "Alfonso Cano."
Even the same night, this man from Puerto Tejada was responsible for the Minister of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, to announce that it was actually the head of the FARC.
In search of traces
For a dactyloscope from the Sijín in Cali, the appraisal given to President Juan Manuel Santos Eliazar González at a press conference on the use against "Cano" was very important.
A lot of people don't know our work.
Daktyloscopy is an exact science without an error rate, which can be a key factor in dissolving a crime.
Lophoscopists not only identify bodies, they also support investigations into murder and robbery and confirm the identities of those arrested.
In their arrest, many offenders present false identification papers or they have none.
We'll take their fingerprints and find them in the database.
The explorer told us that he had to tear a piece of wall out of a house in a theft, because the thief had supported himself with his hand at exactly this point, when he pulled out a safe full of jewelry and money.
"Walls are difficult because their surfaces are unsuitable for fingerprinting. But in this case, we were able to take the imprint of the whole hand and identify the offender who had participated in multiple thefts," he recalled.
With the help of the fingerprints, murder suspects can also be exonerated or burdened.
Recently, a dead was found surrounded by liquor bottles.
During the investigation, it was suspected that a family member could be suspected, and in fact a fingerprint on the bottle matched.
Although this does not guarantee that it is the murderer, he is nevertheless related to the place where the offence was committed.
A Dactyloscopist of the CTI explained that "the fingerprints with chemical or physical reagents would be taken from the object."
When they appear, a planting trowel or tape is used to take.
After that, the imprints are sent to Asif, which is the criminal database, or to the civil registry office.
"The problem is that there are still many Colombians without identification papers," explained the expert from the CTI.
He said that not everything is the same as it is portrayed in the TV series SCI.
Just as not all gold, which shines, isn’t everything you touch is a fingerprint.
However, it can still be used if a corresponding comparison is made.
The "outragements" challenge authorities in New York
Hundreds of protesters crowded into tents in Zuccotti Square.
A majority complied with the eviction order and changed its location to a nearby park.
Members of the Ocupy Wall Street movement challenged New York's authorities this Tuesday as they again tried to enter Manhatten Square with a judicial order after being cleared off the square at dawn, trying to occupy another place at the Big Apple.
Hundreds of demonstrators crowded together outside Zuccotti Square, to which they wanted to gain access with a judicial order that allowed the "indignants" to camp again at this point. Meanwhile, another part of the movement adhered to the eviction order, occupying a park further north in the city where there were nine arrests.
"Our idea is that if the court doesn't allow us to camp in the square, we're getting together in another place and continuing the battle by legal means to return to Zuccotti, with sit-ins and peaceful demonstrations," said Mark Bray, one spokeswoman for the "Ocupy Wall Street" movement.
Bray stated that the "outraged" response to the eviction of her tent camp, which had been ordered directly by Michael Bloomberg, the mayor of New York, came all the way to tribunal, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order banning the forcible eviction of the square and authorised the protesters to camp.
In addition, the "outrages" occupied a new park located at the intersection of centrally located Canal Street and Sixth Avenue very close to the driveway into the Holland tunnel connecting New York and New Jersey, which is the property of Trinity Church.
They were able to occupy the place for at least an hour. But after that, the police arrived and arrested some of the protesters, although Bray cannot give exact numbers. He assured the Efe news agency that he also saw several journalists with handcuffs underneath.
The organization assures that hundreds of people had gathered at this point, where a secondment of religious leaders had also arrived as a sign of their support of the members of the movement and for mediation with the police.
In the meantime, Zuccotti Square (private property area) remains closed in anticipation of a judicial hearing scheduled for this Tuesday, during which the legality of the eviction that took place at dawn is to be analyzed.
Some of the protesters driven off the square carried photocopies of this judicial order with them and distributed it. They even gave it to some police officers guarding the square, which resulted in "some small clashes," as Bray reported, who had indicated that some police officers had beaten protesters.
The court order prohibits authorities from clearing members of Occupy Wall Street from the square as well as the application of "laws that were passed after the occupation and forbidding that the protesters return to the park with their tents and other belongings they had used before."
Mayor Bloomberg clarifies to the press that due to this judicial order, the reopening of the square for public traffic and the protesters had been lifted. He pointed out that the city laws did not allow them to set up again with tents and sleeping bags in this place.
The mayor personally defended the eviction of Zuccotti Square carried out at dawn, during which nearly 200 people were arrested, and which was decided because the protesters "violated the right."
Bloomberg explained that he had given the order to evict the square because it had "morphed into a place where people didn't come to demonstrate, but for breaking the laws and, in some cases, to inflict harm on other individuals," because "some businesses would have received threats" and local residents feared "for their quality of life."
"Eviction at dawn was disgusting and shows Bloomberg is basically interested in the protection of financial interests rather than the protection of the interests of North American workers," the movement's spokesperson added, who accused the mayor "not to respect the freedom of the press."
Occupy Wall Street officials said they were thinking about organising "great actions" for Thursday, along with community organizations and trade unions to commemorate the two-months the protests have been lasting since September 17.
Pamela Anderson is the Virgin Mary on TV Christmas show
Together with Michael Bublé and other Canadian celebrities, she will appear in the TV program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Canadian Pamela Anderson was Tim Allen's assistant in "Home Improvement," lifeguard in "Baywatch," rabbite on "Playboy," the wife of rock musician Tommy Lee and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Maria on a Christmas show of Canadian television.
The private television channel CTV announced today that Anderson, along with the "crooner" (ballad singer) Michael Bublé and other Canadian celebrities, will appear in the TV program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1 and breathe life into a very special Virgin Maria.
CTV circulated today pictures of the television program, in which Anderson - who also appears for distribution on the internet of a self-made porn, in which she performs together with her former husband, the drummer of "Mötley Crüe," Tommy Lee - as Virgo Mary with a puppet in her lap.
In the scene, Peters, a well-known Canadian comedian of Native American origin who recently worked as a presenter on Charlie Sheen's "My Violent Torpedo of Truth," appears as a baby Jesus.
CTV's Christmas program not only relies on Anderson and Peters, but also the Canadian singer Bublé, who married the Argentine model Luisana Lopilato at the end of March in Buenos Aires, and who is not far away from this controversy.
Bublé recently referred to North American Kim Kardashian as a "whore" at one of his concerts in New York.
Japan reborn after earthquake
GDP grows by 1.5% thanks to domestical consumption, ending the spiral of free fall from the beginning of March
Since last March, the Japanese economy has been paralyzed, still reflected in the recovery after the destructive earthquake that culminated in a nuclear crisis, and the worst disaster Japan had experienced in World War 2.
The first estimates of the government in Tokyo assessed the property damage to 300 000 dollars, a blow that seems to be weakening despite the difficult world economic situation.
After three-quarters of permanent cases, data on gross domestic product from July to September served to finally conclude this chapter: GDP grew by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and by 6% to the rate projected for the year.
The value measured in the second quarter - in Japan begins the financial year in March 2012 - also represents the fastest growth since the period from January to March 2010.
The recovery can be partly explained by the machine sale for the reconstruction of the areas devastated by the earthquake and the subsequent tsunami.
In fact, companies increased their investments by 1.1% compared to the 0.9% decline for investments recorded from January to March, the first decline after six months of unstainable rises.
Even so, the actual turnaround of the Japanese economy isn’t based solely on Keynsian impetus that use the crisis to build or rebuild infrastructure.
Japan has woken up in particular thanks to the development of domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60% of Japan's GDP.
This variable increased by 1%, sparked by growing spending on vehicles, travel and recreational pursuits.
Stigma of the yen
Tokyo has confided in the domestic market after the yen - the Japanese currency - flees into one of the "refuge currencies" of investors fleeing the eurozone's debt crisis and the murky forecasts for the U.S.A., a privilege that, like a tombstone, weighs on the export companies' accounts.
With a tougher currency, the products abroad are more expensive, a serious problem for a country whose economy relies on the health of major technology producers.
Toyota and Sony, for example, attributed their weak business results from July to September to the yen's listing.
While markets continue to besiege peripheral EU countries, Yoshihiko Noda government remains vigilant.
"You have to pay attention to the ongoing risks, for example the deterioration of some foreign economies, the effects of the floods in Thailand and the rapid appreciation of the yen," Motohisa Furukawa, the secretary of state for economic policy, said yesterday.
Prospects aren't really very sneering.
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) stated that most member states showed clearer indications of a slowdown in economic development in September.
The indicator that precedes the turning points of the global economy brought to light that, at the greatest economic potencies, the brakes would have been pulled.
Because he sank by four-tenths to loose 100.4 points compared to the 100.8 in August.
The eurozone is even below this average, which is at 100, after falling by eight-tenths to 99.13 points.
Athen raises doubts again
Papadimos says the austerity measures would be adopted, but the Conservatives will not sign a written deal
With the skyrocketing risk premiums of the peripheral countries and widespread insecurity, Greece continues to get lost in the labyrinth that leads out of the great European debt crisis.
The impression that the "consensus" of the new Greek interim government is more of a reaction to the pressure from Brussels than a genuine conviction of the political forces is slowly evidenced by facts.
While the new prime minister, Lukas Papadimos, pointed out yesterday that there were no alternatives to the structural reforms and that the hundred days agreed on his appointment were possibly too short for their implementation, the leader of the Conservative party Nueva Democracia, Antonis Samaras, assured that he would support the new austerity measures and will by no means withdraw his demand for new elections on the planned date, February 19.
"The most important task of this government is the implementation of the decisions taken at the summit of 26 October," Papadimos assured yesterday at the opening of a parliamentary debate, which will peak tomorrow with the vote of confidence for the new government.
A package of 24 programmes to promote employment, to reform disciplinary law for civil servants and new measures against tax evasion are the first initiatives.
Even so, Samara's communication does not clear the doubts about his stance: "We have pledged in support of the interim government, but for nothing else."
The Conservative leader went a step too far with his challenge from the EU and the IMF, the organism that desperately needs the 8000 million that Athens desperately needs. He clarified that he would not endorse a commitment on the fulfillment of austerity measures.
But the European Commission, still irritated because of the effects of playing poker with Papandreu referendum, reiterated yesterday that it was impossible to shakle its demand: economic aid would not be signed if the new government and the leaders of the main Greek parties signed "an unequivocal written agreement as soon as possible."
Economy Ministry spokesman Amadeu Altafaj explained that troika's inspectors came back "very soon" to Athens to discuss with the new government "what was "necessary" for the payment of the sixth section in the amount of 8000 million euros, even though there is still no definitive date.
The Greek skein still has threads for a while.
Queen Sofía: "These are instruments for progress and hope"
The queen stressed Spain's "predominant" position in relation to this type of funding
Spain "has achieved a predominant position worldwide in the role of micro-financing."
Queen Sofía of Spain yesterday remindeded that Spain had become the world's second donor for this means of international cooperation over the past two decades. Its "essence" was based on being an "instrument for social progress, justice and the hope for a better future for all of humanity based on trust in people."
The Queen inaugurated the fifth World Summit on Micro-Loans in Valladolid yesterday. She was convinced that there were "no doubts" that this type of business would be "absolutely essential" to meet the millennium targets set by the United Nations by 2015 and "in particular the goal, the number of people who have to live with incomes of less than one dollar a day, i.e
Spain "continues to work" on the expansion of the beneficiary network "to the last corner of the world and it performs this task responsibly," noted Queen Sofia, who hopes that the initiatives and contributions that will run from the summit, which lasts until Thursday, are "guaranteed the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest and in particular for women" as main beneficiaries of these loans.
"We've seen a lot of successes" and "face a lot of challenges."
The summit on microloans is not only a forum for debates, but also a platform to initiate "two fundamental goals."
The Queen said that by the end of 2015, 175 million poor families should have access to these financial services by the end of 2015 and that tens of millions of these households will cross the poverty line.
The conception of microloans in Spain and the rest of the world goes back to the path "initiated years ago" by Muhammad Yunus, whom Queen Sofia judges as an "act of justice and recognition."
She described the father of this financing instrument as an "admirable idealist who was committed to the fight against poverty" and whose work she knew for 15 years, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to get to get to get to get to get to get to get to
This experience was followed a year later by the first World Summit in Washington and further meetings on Ivory Coast in 1999 and Canada in 2006. Among all, "we have been able to observe a continuous further development," explained the Queen, whose commitment to this project yesterday earned her the assessment as "family head" in Valladolid and as an "advocacy of the poor."
Finally, Queen Sofia ended her lecture by animating those present to participate in the debate and seeking solutions in the fight against poverty.
'Ethical commandment'
Spanish Foreign Minister Trinidad Jiménez pointed out that solidarity is "an ethical imperative" for the entire democratic state.
In this sense, she emphasized that it was a mistake to bind development aid at times of economic booms, because it was a "permanent obligation."
Jiménez was of the opinion that micro-loans were a "serious and rigorous policy to eradicate poverty," and that the governments of the industrialized countries would now have to work "more than ever" according to criteria of solidarity, and should not allow the solidarity of the crisis to fall victim.
For Spain, micro-loans are one of the "most important forms of development aid."
Secretary-of-State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodríguez, applauded "the success," "marginalized" people "who are always in front of closed doors" to "hope," and the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, welcomed those present in a city that "opes their gates, infected by the spirit of solidarity."
The CDU is proposing to elect the EU president on the basis of general voting
The first day of the Congress of the Christian Democrats (CDU on Monday, November 14 in Leipzig went without real difficulties for Angela Merkel, chancellor and leader of the Christian-Democratic party.
The future of Europe was not originally envisioned in the programme, but prevailed in the autumn, in particular in order to satisfy those activists who assess the government’s policy with regard to the Greeks today and possibly Italy tomorrow as too lax.
The presidential motion proposed by the Bureau from the European Commission was adopted almost unanimously.
Among 1001 delegates, only nine had voted against it, ten had abstained.
The several-minute standing ovation, which was initially valid due to Angela Merkel due to their intervention decrees and Wolfgang Schäuble a few hours later, was not open to any doubt on the part of the three finance ministers entitled to Europe.
The Chancellor's main idea - as well as the requests - bears the following slogan: the answer to the crisis is "not less, but more Europe".
Not only because Europe means peace, but also there "nine million jobs are directly dependent on the euro".
For the chancellor, "the time has come for a breakthrough in Europe."
This could be reflected by a modification of the Lisbon Treaty with regard to the budget policy of the countries in the eurozone, which strengthens the control of the European Union; however, this could also result in the election of the President of the European Commission on the basis of general balloting in order to give Europe a face."
However, this is definitely one of the motion proposals adopted by the Congress, to which Wolfgang Schäuble clearly stood.
Surely it is little likely that the 47 will quickly agree on this revolution: Great Britain is opposed to the matter, and the leaders of the CDU believe that Nicolas Sarkozy is of the same opinion.
However, the milestone is laid down.
Likewise, in an overwhelming majority, the delegates have rejected the proposal of the weighting of each country in the ECB board according to the weighting of the individual economies, which obviously entails an increase in Germany's position.
The chancellor’s call for reassurance of the climate within her party has therefore reinforced reference points to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl. However, she has no longer tried to win her congress at the expense of “small” or European countries in difficulty, who would immediately criticize arrogant Germany.
As far as the European request dedicates a paragraph to the significance of the "German-French friendship", Angela Merkel does not invoke France on the plan.
At least not directly.
Because this reminiscent of the fact that two centuries ago Leipzig was at the center of a bloody battle.
In fact, one of the highly humbling defeats of the Napoleonic army.
What does sick reports represent fraud?
According to the family allowances, UMP and the government are now tackling the sick reports.
Nicolas Sarkozy will arrive in Bordeaux on Tuesday, November 15, in order to visit a family health insurance fund and to present a speech about the fight against social fraud and economic dependence on the state, which has been recurring since 2007.
In order to accompany this attack, which proceeds from the quest to subject the economy in a test of hardship, but on the other hand is no less harbinger of an axis of the right-wing presidential campaign, the majority wants to focus on workers' sick reports.
First with budgetary objectives: the government is targeting the introduction of a fourth waiting day before taking into account a sick report on workers in the private sector.
In good German, this means that a diseased worker would lose the wages of four days without the approval of the specific company, i.e. at least 15% of his monthly income.
A measure that, according to the daily "Les Echos", threatens to conjure up anger, but would help to contribute 280 million euros.
And which was accompanied by a different measure of rather symbolic character: to give the state officials an unremunerated waiting day in the case of illness (so far there is for them, in contrast to the three waiting days in the private sector, no waiting period).
Meanwhile, these questions are under discussion, the majority are also rekindling the topic of fraud, this time targeting the abuse associated with absence of work.
"Controls will be significantly more important, and beyond that, these will be recompensations if required," Xavier Betrand warned of RTL's microphone on Sunday, November 13.
As in other cases, the effects of this report on the reality of fraudulent acts in the field of sick reports seem a little disproportionated.
In fact, this has been the subject of permanent observation since 2002, as well as political offensives, which have borne fruit most often.
The French are no more likely to be sick than people elsewhere in Europe.
However, let’s linger first with the nationwide digits.
According to the Landeskrankenkasse CNAM, more than 237 million euros of daily severances were paid out in 2006.
According to the Economic Research and Documentation Institute of the health care, the daily severancements in 2008 amounted to 11.3 billion euros 5% of healthcare expenses.
This amount was 46% for maternity leave as well as occupational accidents and 54% for sick leave, i.e. 6.2 billion euros.
2.5% health expenditures.
In 2010, according to a study by the Alma Consulting Group, the French were granted an average of only 14.5 sick days compared to the 17.8 days in 2009.
A mean that covers up certain differences: another, published by Monster.com in 2007 by Monster.com website, showed that 75% of the 40, 000 interviewees surveyed told French workers that they had not taken a single sick day.
Another study recorded this time by health insurance clarifies that 37% of sick reports in 2010 were shorter than 8 days; 22% between 8 and 14 days, 15% between 15 days and one month, 15% between one and three months and 11% above.
Compared with their European neighbors, French workers tend no more than elsewhere to incapacitate.
A long-term study conducted in 2010 by two researchers from the French State Centre for Science Research (CNRS) showed that between 1994 and 2001 the global absence rate (sized or otherwise justified) oscillated, namely in France between 10% and 11% contrary to 20% and 28% in Denmark, 15% in the United Kingdom or 16% and 18% in the Netherlands.
The fraud in sick reports represents a vanishingly small proportion with regard to undeclared work.
With regard to fraud, only a weak extent is achieved here.
Enhancing the legislation since 2020 entails the existence of systmatic checks on sick reports exceeding 45 days.
In 2008, CNAM (public college of education and training of technicians and engineers) found under 1.5 million checks that 13% of the 285,000 realized short-term sick reports, i.e. in 37,050 cases, were unwarranted or too long.
In the systematically controlled sick reporting cases of more than 45 days duration, one counted less than 1.2 million 11% of "unadjusted or unwarranted" cases, that is 13,000.
In total, 169,000 "unjustified" cases affect several million workers who have claimed a sick report.
A digit that represents only a weak extent compared to, for example, that of undeclared work.
According to UMP MEP Dominique Tian, author of a factual report published in July with regard to this question, this represents between 9 and 15 billion euros of tax loss per year, i.e. more than the total cost of daily allowances, and thus substantially more than the defraudation at the latter.
Papademos: Greece should introduce a new adjustment plan
Greece needs a new adaptation program to capture its economy.
This was announced on Monday, 14 November by Prime Minister Lucas Papademos in front of the Parliament, in addition to the fact that the one hundred days granted to his government on the part of the Union was not enough to complete this task.
"In order to continue the efforts to strengthen the economy, we need the support of our European partners as well as a new budget adjustment programme," the head of government said in his first publical speech at the opening of the debate on Parliament's vote of confidence.
Implementing the decisions taken at the euro summit on 27 October was the "main task" of the new government, as the participation of the country within the eurozone was "at stake", as he added.
Correspondingly, M. Papademos has planned to reduce the country's government deficit "around the 9%" of gross domestic product from today until the end of the year, after it was 10.6% in 2010 and at 15.7% in 2009.
Rights reject the new austerity measures
Antonis Samaras, head of the right wing, on Monday had given his approval of the already approved measures regarding an attempt serving to escape from the debt crisis, but sent ahead that his party would not agree to an additional austerity.
"We will not vote in favour of new measures," he said on the occasion of a parliamentary meeting of his New Democracy party.
He had agreed with the requirements of the reduction of budget deficits and debts, as well as to the fight against waste, but was hostile to any policy that would prevent the economic recovery.
Olli Rehn, EU commissioner entrusted with economic and monetary tasks, who had prevented the IMF and the EU from releasing the necessary loan of 8 billion euros without a written guarantee from all parties via their support of the planned measures, Antonio Samaras had replied that his word would suffice and he would sign nothing under external pressure.
Euthanasia and accompanied suicide must be legalized, according to a panel of experts
The Canadians live in denial of death, according to an account of the Royal Society of Canada
The panel stated that governments, health care institutions and physicians worked together for pain-relieving treatment to provide the best pain-relieving remedies, and beyond cancer cases.
The Canadian society lives in a denial attitude towards death.
Only 9% of Canadians actually agree to talk to their doctor about conditions in which they want to die and make dispositions for that purpose.
This is one of the conclusions of an imposing report from the Royal Society of Canada, in which six experts have been involved who are interlinked in various fields, including ethics, law and medicine.
This body recommends that the government amend the criminal law in such a way as to allow accompanied suicide and euthanasia where this choice arises, for patients who are considered able to make such a decision.
As a prelude, the experts quote a recently published survey by the independent company EIU, which had compared the quality of death in 40 countries of the globe.
Even thince Canada has placed in 10th place, the study notes that "medical death care in Canada has spawned a culture where people are afraid of crunching the issue of death."
Medical care or not: 77% of Canadians do not have access to pain-killing agents, the experts appointed by the Royal Society of Canada tell us.
And and despite the majority desire of Canadians to die at home, 68.6% of these ultimately die in the hospital.
Viewed from the whole country, the rate is highest at 86% of hospitalized deaths in Quebec.
On the delicate question concerning euthanasia and accompanied suicide, the authors of the report come to conclusions which are contrary to the position of the federal government, which has already argued that there are no changes to the criminal law regarding euthanasia and accompanied suicide.
However, according to the experts who have inspected all those countries who have legalized or decriminalized euthanasia and accompanied suicide, there is no evidence that such decriminalization would result in abusions in its application, i.e. the practice of euthanasia or accompanied suicide by uncompetent or non-consenting persons.
On the contrary, there are considerably more cases of such transgression, especially in countries where euthanasia and accompanied suicide are not legalized, as Jocelyn Downie, co-signatory of the report, confirmed in a teleconference yesterday.
"Euthanasia is practiced in Canada, where it is clearly illegal."
"Accompanied suicide is also practiced in Canada, even though clearly illegal here," Jocelyn Downie explains.
Canadians support euthanasia
Let’s remember that, according to Angus-Reid survey conducted in 2010, 85% of Canadians had already spoken in favour of euthanasia because they think they allow individuals at the end of their lives to alleviate their suffering.
And 66% believe that the facts of legalizing euthanasia do not result in mediating the embassy that the lives of the elderly or disabled have less value.
With regard to the persons who have already practiced an accompanying suicide, the respondences 41% believe that they should not be prosecuted.
As far as the maintenance or cessation of the funds in patients is concerned, the situation is less clear, according to the authors of the report.
Likewise, these suggest in their list of recommendations that it should be stated in the criminal law that the withdrawal of the funds, for which a legally valid rejection has been present, should not be considered criminal.
The panel of experts also calls for healthcare professionals to be trained in the task of reserving end-of-life means and to fear this without criminal prosecution.
The panel stated that governments, health care institutions and physicians worked together for pain-relieving treatment to provide the best pain-relieving remedies, beyond the cancer cases.
Most recently, with regard to the means already widely used in Canadian institutions on health care or life-concluding measures, the panel is at least requesting to consider these, just as they did not serve to reduce physical pain and relate to the same procedures.
Reservations from other experts
Some experts in the field of health services have already raised reservations regarding the conclusions of the expert report of the Royal Society of Canada.
RA Pierre Deschamps, a specialist in health law, has described the attitude of the reporting experts, among other things, as "extremely", since this does nothing set limits for euthanasia in the case of sick people in the final phase.
"This opens the gates for a situation in which anyone who is life-tired and older than 18 years of age ask another person to assist him with his suicide," according to RA Deschamps, who had looked through a summary of the report yesterday.
RA Deschamps also stresses that the authors of the report seemed to place the individual's personal responsibility above all other values involved in the social impact of society.
But now, he says, there would be "guidelines and commitments in a society."
Margaret Somerville, a specialist in health law, has also expressed "strong opposition" to the conclusions of the report, which she described as "pro-euthanasia manifesto."
Margaret Somerville, who is herself a member of the Royal Society of Canada, doubts, in particular, the reports regarding the absence of abuses in certain countries where euthanasia and accompanied suicide are legalized, such as in the Netherlands and Oregon.
King of Jordan calls on President Assad to resign from power
Damascus denounces a "plot"
Syria is increasingly isolated
Bashar al-Assad's partisans demonstrated yesterday in front of foreign ministry authorities.
The diplomatic pressure on Bashar al-Assad's Syria, which, however, counteracts this thanks to Russia's support and dismisses a "complacy directed against the country," intensified yesterday.
Two days before Syria's exit by the Arab League, the European Union has tightened its sanctions on Damascus, even though Turkey and Jordan, Syria's neighboring countries, had taken a position in favor of President Assad's resignation.
King Abdullah II of Jordan yesterday was the incumbent Arab prime minister who had urged President Bashar al-Assad "to resign his official power in Syria," which already killed around forty civilians and members of government power.
"If Bashar were interested in his country, he would step down, but in addition, he should also create the necessary conditions for a new era of political Syrian life," according to the king of Jordan, neighboring Syria, in an interview with the BBC.
For his part, Turkish diplomat Ahmet Davutoglu complained that Turkish mediation attempts since the beginning of the year had not been crowned with success.
"Those in the Middle East who are not at peace with their people and cannot satisfy them must go," he explained, in reference to Syria, the neighbor country, to which Turkey is taking a steadily rougher stance.
The United States, for its part, has greeted "tightening the consensus against Assad as well as the machinations of the Syrian regime" as a result of decisions by the Arab League as well as the European Union.
"International community, United States, EU, Arab League and countries such as Turkey have struck an ever sharper tone in the face of repression in Syria," observes Mark Toner, a spokesman for the state department.
During this period, 16 civilians and at least 19 members of their government power have died in the Dar'a region of southern Syria, according to the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights OSDH.
Two other civilians had died, according to this NGO, during "gunshot changes and serious bombings around Jobar" in the besieged city of Homs.
On the occasion of a press press conference, Syrian Foreign Trade Minister Walid al-Muallim was nevertheless of the view that the country was "taxed towards the end of the crisis".
Incidentally, he reacted emphatically to the decision of the Arab League to suspend Damascus from his duties, which in his estimation represents a "dangerous step".
"Syria will not yield," he added under the assertion that "the plots forged against Syria would fail."
According to the decision to suspend Syria, the Arab League is currently investigating a "protection mechanism for civilians" and wishes to send 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, the media and military observers to the country.
French Foreign Trade Minister Alain Juppé had also spoken out in favor of the deployment of UN observers in order to protect civilians from repressions by the regime that he is encapsulating himself in his "paranoia".
An unscheduled new assembly of the League is scheduled for tomorrow in Rabat to discuss the implementation of the plan decided on November 2, but in particular the controversial expected withdrawal of military troops from the besieged cities as well as the liberation of imprisoned protesters.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov yesterday classified the Arab League's decision as "incorrect" in order to beware of advocacting possible sanctions against Damascus, although China had already admonished Syria to implement the crisis phase-out plan.
Italy
Monti urges markets to be patient and Italy a willingness to sacrifice
Mario Monti, future Italian head of governmen, has demanded a little more time from the markets to form his staff and implement a program that "challenges" to regain creditworthiness lost during the final months of the Berlusconi government.
The former EU commissioner had held a negotiation with the political parties yesterday ahead of today's clash of trade unions and employers.
It was his job to convene a cabinet in parliament, which is relatively strictly composed of external technocrats.
"Monti spoke of an important programme with a number of victims," according to Francesco Nucara, MP of one of the many smaller parliamentary groups participating in the talks, having met the incumbent council president.
Gianfranco Fini, president of the Chamber of Deputies, had in fragrance, for his part, to wait and see that Mario Monti puts a vote of confidence in parliament by Friday in order to ensure that the new government has sufficient support.
The collusion will culminate in the presentation by M. Monti of a deletion list that will undoubtedly range from a dozen ministers to Giorgio Napolitano's head of state.
M. Monti commented that yesterday's nervousness of the stock market and bond markets called for their time.
"There are certain deadlines" to prepare a government as well as a programme of government, he stressed under the remark that it was "certain that the markets will show patience and understanding."
His appointment by the president was greeted by the markets at the beginning, but the unrest had intensified again, especially after an unexpected industrial production decline in the eurozone in September.
The incumbent prime minister should establish a staff consisting of predominantly technocrats, although he had emphasized that "politicians" would like to involve them.
The new government should only exist until 2013, the date of the next general election, he estimated.
According to Gianfranco Fini, the president of the Chamber of Deputies, the new government should receive the vote of confidence of parliament by Friday.
Since taking up its function, M. Monti has pledged to work in the spirit of the goal of making Italy the "renewed protagonist" in Europe.
A spokesperson for EU Commissioner Olli Rehn had stressed that "our diagnosis towards the Italian economy does not change even taking into account the new government."
The EU, which is convinced that Rome will not achieve its target of budget compensation in 2013 despite the austerity plans taken up in these last few months, has in particular applied for new hardship measures.
Emma Marcegaglia, head of Italy's chief executive, who will meet Monti on Tuesday, also insists on the need for buoyancy, since a country that has no growth whatsoever cannot respect deficiency indicators."
M. Monti, who is known for his competence as well as his independence as European Commissioner (1994-2004), stands for an "epoch shift" of Italy after 17 years "Berlusconism"; the "professor" symbolizes the "challenge of trustworthiness" as well as an "other Italy," according to the editorial authors.
The great unknown here is the longevity of his staff.
President Napolitano would like to prevent early elections, as Italy now has until April 2012 for 200 billion euros of state bonds.
Russian Scientist Helps Iran's Nuclear Energy Program
A Russian scientist, Vyacheslav Danilenko, has helped Iran develop an explosive posule usable with a nuclear weapon, an American NGO specializing in nuclear risks confirmed on Monday.
The Institute of Academia and International Focurity ISIS is based on the recently published report of the International Agency for Atomic Security AIEA as well as other documents originating from this UN agency to identify this scientist.
The AIEA had communicated its "serious concern" last week on the occasion of a "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear programme.
According to in-depth study of AIEA documents by ISIS, Danilenko, who was born in 1934, had apparently been working in a Soviet military nuclear center in Chelyabinsk (Ural) for over three decades since the beginning of the 1960s and was involved in the production of synthetic diamonds produced by explosion.
In 19998 or 1991, he is said to have left the center to establish a company in Kiev that produces "nano-diamonds."
His company's economic difficulties in 1995 would have led him to contact the Iranian embassy in Ukraine.
Before returning to Russia, he then participated in an Iranian programme from 1996 to 2002.
In its last report, the AIEA mentioned "strong hints that the Iranian development of a nuclear explosive system" had been supported by the work of a foreign expert who was not only particularly proficient in this technique, but also, as a member of state told the agency that his entire professional career had worked directly on the military nuclear energy program of his country of origin.
Anti-Islamic crimes and offenses have increased by 50%
Crimes and offenses directed against Muslims have risen by 50% from 2009 to 2010, while other racially or relegially motivated violent crimes have either declined slightly in the same period, according to FBI statistics released on Monday, or have increased only slightly.
According to this data, the total number of violent crimes committed against Muslims increased from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, i.e
The total "hate-motivated crimes" had risen to 6,628 cases on a very weak scale, including 47.3% due to racial differences and 20% by religious differences of motivated offenses, according to the FBI.
"Following a mitigation in 2009, it is disturbing to see these crimes and offenses growing again," the human rights organisation Human Rights First stressed. "The rise in anti-Islamic acts of violence is particularly significant," the organization added in a communiqué.
"Human Rights First had emphasized for a long time that violence directed against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crime, should be considered a serious violation of human rights by law," added Paul Legendre, a leader in charge.
"The American government can and should do more to combat these assaults," he added, assessing that this could be carried out with an improvement in police attitudes towards "hate crimes."
"Hate crime" presents itself in American federal law as an heir from the epoch of the struggle for civil rights.
According to Martin Luther King's death, this legislation had been passed in order to punish acts of violence directed against race, color, religion or ancestrity and now also include crimes of sexual affiliation.
Twitter is transforming the platform and more and more Facebook
Microblogging website Twitter on Monday introduced changes in the presentation of its platform to enable its users, in particular, to observe the activities of those who follow their listings, which once again makes the service similar to that of Facebook.
