Austria
Registration of "Golden Household Rule" in the Constitution
On Tuesday, the government of the largest social democratic/Christian Democratic coalition in power in Austria decided to include a "golden budget rule" in the constitution in order to reduce public deficits and also to avoid possible degradation on the part of rating agencies in Austria's top creditworthiness (AAA).
It was the Social Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann who had announced this decision of the outcome of the Council of Ministeries, even though the public Austrian national debt is 74.6%, i.e. far above the criterion set by the Maastricht Treaty of 60%, but well below the debt level of the countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"Had Austria's solvency not fallen from AAA to AA+ in one fell swoop, we would have to pay an additional three billion euros in interest every year," argued Michael Spindelegger, vice-chancellor and Christian Democrat foreign minister.
Austria is also making a demand from the Brussels summit of eurozone member states of 26 October: at that time, heads of state and government had demanded the inclusion of reducing public deficits by the end of 2012, as well as a return to public account equilibrium into the constitution or by adopting an equivalent law.
The measure seized by the Austrian government is similar to the "German model", since Germany had been the first European country that had included such a "rule" in the constitution.
By 2017, the structural budget deficit should be reduced by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP) each year; from 2017, the state’s structural budget deficit should not exceed the 0.35% limit of GDP, whereas the regional states as well as the municipalities should have balanced budgets.
The aim is to eradicate budgetary debt in the 2020/21 horizon under the 60% criterion fixed by the Maastricht Agreement.
Strong yen
Noda threatens new intervention in currency markets
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that Japanese authority would again intervene in the currency markets should the yen continue to rise.
"Should we establish excessive volatility" at the exchange rate of the yen, we will intervene just as we did the last time," M. Noda declared in the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen, in turn, had soared close to the historical levels that the Japanese authorities forced to intervene on October 31.
That day, Tokyo had massively sold yen against dollars to lower Japan's foreign exchange value, which is about to head for a new record high since 1945 against the dollar.
The yen, however, has risen periodically, doped by purchases from investors who see the yen as a "refuge value" in these uncertain times of world economic downturn and indebtedness crisis in Europe.
The dollar has falled below 77 yen on Tuesday, the euro has slipped below the symbolic slat of 105 yen close to the levels that had triggered the last intervention.
Ahead of the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar listed around 120 yen, the euro more than 160 yen.
This straw fire of the Japanese motto harms the exports of the archipelago, as it increases the cost of "Made in Japan" products and reduces the income value drawn by Japanese companies from abroad, as they exchange them in national currency.
"Japan is fighting for its reconstruction after the earthquake, as well as the tsunami on March 11, which has devastated the Tohoku (north-east) region," insists M. Noda, who fears that this inflation of the yen could kill the weak upswing already in the bud.
With growth in the third quarter -1.5% compared to the previous quarter, the third economic world power has once again regained traction for the first time this year thanks to skyrocketing export increases and the consumption of households "plummed" by the disaster.
However, the prime minister estimates that the yen's inflation does not "reflect the economic foundations of Japan."
Finance Minister Jun Azumi, for his part, has urged Bank of Japan (BoJ), which opened a two-day meeting of its monetary policy committee on Tuesday, to take all possible measures to invalidate the yen.
Hirohide Yamaguchi, deputy governor of the institute, however, has informed that the BoJ has already initiated "suitable measures," meaning that no additional currency easing is to be expected on Wednesday.
On the occasion of the last meeting at the end of October, the central bank had increased its state bond purchases by 5,000 billion yen (47 billion euros) in order to repay the 55,000 billion yen (519 billion euros) coverage amounts it had spent on various purchases of financial securities in order to flood the liquidity market.
eurozone:
Interesting feet skyrocket, stock exchanges pull back
The contagion risk of the debt crisis constantly undermines the European key figures.
Despite the taking office of the new heads of government in Greece and Italy, as well as the best growth figures in France and Germany, the rates of the weakened countries continue to rise.
The exchanges, which are still concerned about the stability of the eurozone, are also drifting a little more into negative climes this Tuesday, November 15.
In Paris, the CAC lost 1.4% by 10 o'clock and fell to 3,064 points.
The Dax in Frankfurt declined 0.4%, London by 0.1%.
"Due to the new tensions over government bond yields in the eurozone in difficulty, global stock markets are continuing to spend some of their recent gains.
"Investors are nervous and only too well aware of contagion risks," Terry Pratt, an institutional broker at IG Markets, cited by press agency Reuters, explains.
The stock exchange in Tokyo, which in turn suffers from the renewed fear of market participants in terms of capacity in Europe that the debt crisis will continue, had closed with a basis of 0.7%.
""Japan, like the rest of the world, has its eyes on Europe, and trading is moving towards a limited variation corridor as long as investors have no evidence that the situation has stabilised," estimates Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
This resurgencement of concerns about the future of the eurozone damages the single currency, which continues its baiss on Tuesday.
The euro has been at its lowest level for a month and a half.
Around 10 a.m., the European Valuta was worth 1.3570 dollers, so it declined 0.4%.
According to stockbrokers, it has declined after the CDU, party of Chancellor Angela Merkel, which had gathered in Congress on Monday, granted a motion that in particular provides for the possibility for countries in difficulty to withdraw from the eurozone without leaving the European Union at the same time.
Italian and Spanish rates are well below the 6% limit.
Italy and Spain's loan interest rates continue to be at very disturbing levels.
This Tuesday morning, the return of the Italian bonds reached 6.85% for 10 years, while that of the Spanish titles matured to 6.2%.
These tensions of interest rates in the southern countries, but also in France, continue to feed investor fears.
Future Italian Prime Minister Mario Monti demanded more time from the markets to restore the situation threatened by paralysis caused by the debt burden in Italy.
The new Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos, for his part, had confirmed that the implementation of the decisions taken on the occasion of the eurozone summit on 27 October was "the main task" of his government.
Spain is in the visor of the markets, as the parliamentary elections take place in the country on Sunday.
The interest rate scissors ("spread") between the German and Spanish 10-year bonds has once again reached a new, historic high.
In anticipation of eurozone growth figures
Statistically, France posted economic growth of 0.4% in the third quarter carried by household consumption, ergo a little better than the expected 0.3%.
However, the Federal Statistical Office (INSEE) had revised the basis of the figures for the second quarter with a decrease of 0.1% of gross domestic product (GDP) instead of stagnation.
In truth, it's the fourth quarter that worries economic experts the most.
Germany, for its part, recorded growth of 0.5% over the same period.
An initial projection of the entire eurozone is expected at 11 a.m., with the EU Commission warning of the risk of a renewed recession.
Energy Policy:
Facts should prevail over dogmas
If there is a topic that must not be dealt with on the basis of short-term considerations or on the basis of emotions, this is certainly energy policy.
Availability and cost of electricity directly influence the purchasing power as well as the competitiveness of companies.
It is reasonable to take an electoral meeting as an occasion to reflect on this issue and on the position of nuclear energy.
The debate will bear fruit only if it explores the entirety of economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The French should base their opinion on objective facts, rational data.
Although there are 7 billion people on this planet, demographic evolution is driving a doubling of electricity demand by 2050, not an enormous share of the world’s population should have to forego a vital element.
At the same time, it is necessary to alleviate the scarcity of fossil resources in order to be able to continuously produce electricity without forgetting the imperative need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, an issue that has disappeared from the ecological debate, in order to combat climatic irregularities.
Fukushima's accident does not alter any of these parameters.
Therefore, Germany remains isolated in its decision to forego nuclear power.
This is neither the case in Belgium, which subjects its decision to the need to find a substitution-enabled substitute source, nor in Switzerland, which just intends to build only nuclear power plants of the last generation.
Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India and Brazil, to name just a few countries, continue to pursue their projects with determination.
The United States, for its part, has decided after Fukushima to add another unit to its park by recommercing the Three Mile Island plant, which was shut down after the nuclear accident.
Even if the German model oriented towards renewable energy sources is occasionally presented as a role model, the reality contradicts the discourse.
The election in Berlin will be reflected in the form of an increase in the price of electricity, an intensified by the increased recourse to import gas, in particular on Russia, an increase in CO2 emissions caused by gas and coal-fired power plants.
The totality of these unpleasant effects will become noticeable within a few months.
The case of Denmark gives cause for reflection.
On the one hand, Denmark is the European table leader with regard to wind energy from which it draws 30% of its electricity, on the other hand, the country is one of the strongest users of coal and gas, alternately used with wind windnes; due to this, its CO2 emissions and its electricity price are each 65% and 50% higher than the corresponding European average value.
Thanks to the consistency of our energy policy, our neighbours pay a 40% higher electricity bill in contrast to our French households.
Since the price of energy in the globalised economy is a key role in competitiveness, as well as a stability pledge of the industry on national soil, French companies have an advantage due to Europe's cheapest electricity.
Any significant reduction in nuclear power would therefore lead to a strong increase in the price of energy, which made the noble statements about the reindustrialization of our country and the defense of purchasing power an illusion.
Pollures showed that this was a price that the vast majority of the French are just as unwilling to pay, as it shows resistance to the principle of this energy source.
Let’s remember that our country’s energy policy has made it possible to build an industry with 125,000 direct and 410,000 induced jobs.
Due to its strong export character, it brings a large number of specialized small and medium-sized companies to the international markets and turns them into creators of countless jobs.
If France decides to phase out nuclear power, a large part of these SMEs would fall victim, and the annual 6 billion euros in capital goods and nuclear energy exports would shrink more and more.
Which electrician would commission an EPR designed to work for sixty years if its producer's decline was pre-programmed?
In order to solve the energetic equation, it must be admitted that there is nother than a devil's work a miracle remedy for the generation of electricity.
Developed by Areva’s low-carbon solutions for the use of nuclear power and renewable energy, testify to the complementarity of energy sources.
Maintaining the highest possible level of safety is an absolute imperative for nuclear power.
In France, we have a safety agency that carries out uncompromising and transparent checks in already existing plants, such as construction in Flamanville.
Their working teams, who have made a name for themselves because of their professionalism, are just too obsessed with the safety idea.
Within 50 years, the nuclear industry has experienced three serious accidents.
Only a single one, namely in Three Mile Island, occurred under normal operating conditions; so it went without consequences for people and the environment.
In Chernobyl, the drama was conjured up by reactor construction as well as inend-efficiency, human error.
At Fukushima, some seem to forget that the accident was evoked by two natural disasters of unprecedented proportions that caused the deaths of tens of thousands of people.
The core industry will draw its lessons from this accident as much as it did after the two preceding ones.
As a fruit of this approach as well as a cooperation of the safety authorities of the French and German operators and designers of nuclear power plants, the EPR represents a design that enables it to resulve such phenomena.
Since it is considered desirable among the electrical engineers to build a nuclear power plant, a construction stop in Flamanville would be a fabulous gift to the competition of Areva and EDF by giving them a free way to seize the leadership position in the inescapable development of nuclear power.
It is inevitable and legitimate to look for the best energy bouquet for our country, but it would be irresponsible to allow emotions, dogmas and party manoeuvrers to dominate a debate that determines the equally economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Is Immigration Burden or Opportunity for the Economy?
Nicolas Sarkozy's immigration policy was criticised from a variety of angles - overburdening of police, judicial and administrative bodies on the basis of a policy subject to the figures, compatibility of this policy with the status of a "country of human rights," as France itself had titled.
Recently the harassment directed against foreign students had shaped the newsreaks from the authorities, the interior minister had casually proclaimed (see Libération of 23 May) that France "does not need any talents, bricklayers or waiters from abroad".
But this policy has rarely been analysed from an economic point of view.
There is a far-reaching consensus between the right and the left on this issue.
From the right, the tone of Jacques Chirac, who declared in 1976 that "900,000 unemployed, including over 2 million immigrated workers, should not pose a problem for a country," from the legal side by Michel Rocard, who in 1990 had stated that France "could not absorb the entire poverty of the world."
The only difference is the degree of generosity
Fear of the invasion of national soil by hordes of Polish plumbers was shared in 2005 from both the right and the left.
Immigrants from both camps were perceived as a burden for the economy and French society.
The only difference is in the degree of generosity, which one is willing to admit to the immigrants.
Francois Hollande’s programme is limited to banalities that indicate that partnership development makes it possible to solve our “migration problems”.
This notion of immigration as a burden, as a problem, can potentially have an impact on elections; however, it is economically costly.
As the economic experts Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Comreise to us in a recent work that summarizes the latest findings regarding immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.), there is a broad consensus among specialists about the positive impact of the immigrant flood on economic growth, wages and jobs in the immigrant countries.
Restricting immigration would invalidate growth and damage the labour market situation.
Fears about the influence of migrants are based on the notion that they threaten to substitute the domestic workers, especially the lower-skilled, by putting pressure on their wages.
Experience shows, however, that immigrants are in truth much more complementary than the domestic wage workers, since they focus on occupations in sectors that are unutilised in terms of employment.
The low-skilled immigrants work in sectors that are of little irritation to domestic wage-workers, the more skilled among immigrants work in dynamic sectors where training is not matched to the supply of work.
Positive wage effect
Just as a surgeon would struggle to work in a country where there is a lack of anesthesiosts, this complementarity also has a positive effect of immigrant influence on wages and the domestic labour market.
Giovanni Peri has calculated in this way that a 1% increase in the flood of immigrants brings about a co-involved, long-term increase in real wages between 0.6% and 0.9%.
And this without taking into account the fact that the diversity of the creation of ideas introduced by the immigrants as well as to economic growth is conducive (high proportion of naturalized immigrants among American Nobel laureates; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants also contribute net to the welfare systems, in European countries on average about 1% of the total budget.
The World Labour Organization estimates, for example, that a person immigrating to Germany at the age of 30 contributes an average net contribution (revenues minus expenses) of around 150,000 euros to the public budget via his lifetime.
Nicolas Sarkozy wished to bring about the lack of intersections "with his teeth" at the start of his mandate.
However, these said teeth, which were supposed to deter the immigrants, achieved exactly the opposite effect.
Europe has invented democracy, now it remains to think through European democracy
Democracy is a valuable good; also a sensitive commodity.
The European continent should be able to remember who, according to her invention in Athens more than two thousand years ago, had endured the entire 20th-century tragedies of the First World War, totalitarianism, the Holocaust, the Gulag, but also Francos in Spain, Salazars in Portugal and the colonel in Greece.
Some had probably believed that it was enough to exercise the right to vote, in the most directly possible manner to seize democracy once and for all above their enemies.
One knows since Napoleon "the little one" that it was a feature of the state of mind.
The universal right to vote, the necessary condition of democracy, is not a sufficient condition for this purpose and can just as well become an "instrument of suppression," as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot put it at the time.
No, democracy requires far more: a public space, a public spirit, values, an organization, separation and control of powers, an education, enlightenment, economic and social community spirit, a judiciary.
The crisis in Europe in which we find ourselves is an economic crisis, but it is just as much and, first and foremost, a political and democratic crisis.
In this respect, the change in mind of the Greek truth-wrong referendum would have had a doubly revealing character: once that of the necessity of a powerful return of politics, but on the other hand also that of the disturbing feeling of their impossibility, their inappropriateness.
This ambivalence should be considered obsolete.
Truth is that the front lines of democratic struggle have shifted today, and that Republican soldiers, or at least most of them, are no longer aiming in the right direction.
We have nothing to gain in order to push for the success of the national referendum, which was organized depending on the necessities and in the wrong order.
A release for the European sole ruler or a leap blindfolded into the void of the nationalist withdrawal: A great choice for the citizen!
No, we want Hackebeil, but a collective task, full-right, clarified and steady, an election and democracy, this time on a European level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain and Portugal - by the way you don't know where to end the list anymore - since there's only a single crisis, and this prevails in Europe.
It is the whole of Europe bowing to the dictation of the markets and credit rating agencies, which suffers from its impotence and lack of solidarity.
It is the whole of Europe that is humiliated at international level.
One is troubled about transfers of sovereignty towards Europe - but it is the whole of Europe that is gradually losing its sovereignty, and thus every single one of the nations affiliated to it.
The urgency is to restore the leadership of European project to popular sovereignity.
It is a matter of whether Europeans can reduce the burden of their debts, free themselves from the markets, and initiate a future through investment.
The withdrawal from the crisis has decisions at European level as a condition that cannot be made without total democratic legitimacy, without certainly leading us into perdition.
After this proposal by Jacques Delors and the Socialists has been contested for a long time, today everyone urges European economic leadership to do so.
Agreed.
But to imagine this without political integration and a stronger democracy - as envisaged by the citizens - would be a renewed folly and another impasse.
We need to take on a new stage of European construction.
In order to create the republic in France, it took the fact to "create" Republicans.
The revolution of interests alone could not be enough here.
This instruction remains valid.
We need to form a better-integrated Europe at economic level.
This can only be achieved by creating a more politically democratic Europe, a truly European popular sovereignty.
We need Europeans to create Europe.
"Contactless" chip cards become a common good
The world trade fair for chip cards opens its doors on Tuesday in Villepinte near Paris in an upbeat stance.
According to Eurosmart, 2011 was supposed to end with a 11% increase in worldwide sales of microprocessor chips (EC cards, SIM card phone), with more than 6 billion units sold.
The most powerful segment here is contactless technology, which enables payments on public transport or payment of purchases by simply holding the card to a reading device.
Compared to the 320 million sold a year earlier, 460 million contactless smart cards should be sold worldwide this year!
Their market share was expected to increase even further in 2012 (580 million expected units) and represent over 10% of the global smart card market (6.925 million cards).
Europe gains ground in terms of contactless payment, having lagged behind the Asia-Pacific region for a long time.
A few 26 million Visa cards are already equipped with this functionality, and 75,000 payment terminals accept them.
In France the beginnings are rather modest: only 400,000 contactless visa cards have been circulated.
Consumer markets, however, are vaping.
Carrefour emitted 2.5 million contactless Pass MasterCards this year.
In order to transition to higher speed, it is now necessary for the banks to equip their customers with these cards.
"Two large French financial institutions are committed to systematically handing out bank cards to their customers in the future, which are equipped with contactless chip," announces Gérard Nébouy, director-general of Visa Europe France.
Banks test payment via mobile phone
However, this is only a first stage on the way to the means of payment of the future: the mobile phone.
A vanishingly small percentage of devices has so far been equipped with the NFC (Near Field Communication) technology necessary to carry out these transactions.
“They’re in default.”
"But they're supposed to experience a real uprise development at least next year."
"In 2012, we expect the sale of 80 to 120 million units worldwide," explains Marc Bertin, chairman of Eurosmart.
But banks are taking initiative.
Crédit Agricole had announced yesterday the start of a test run in Caen, which will run from December to next June.
The iPhones of some 200 customers and employees are to be equipped with a capsule that allows contactless payment.
BPCE operates a similar projection in Strasbourg and Marseille.
The Société Générale had also testified yesterday to prepare for the market launch of an offer that allows its customers contactless payment by mobile phone.
Terrorist front, of course, from the Sahara to Nigeria
Paris is concerned about relations between al-Qaeda in Islamic Maghreb and sect Boko Haram.
The long-suspected relations between the al-Qaida terrorist group in Maghreb (Aqmi) and the Nigerian sect Boko Haram appear to be confirmed.
On Sunday, Abdelkader Messahel himself had confirmed that Algiers had now "obtained certainty".
"The way in which both organizations proceed as well as the relations of the intelligence services indicate clear cooperation," he explained without going into further detail.
Paris appears to be doing a similar analysis.
Investigations into the kidnapping of two French in Niger, which took place last January, have revealed interfaces.
Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory, kidnapped in a Niamey restaurant in Niamey, are said to have been killed the following day at the same time as several of their kidnappers on the occasion of an operation carried out by French special forces.
French and Nigerian investigators are said to have found several telephone chips belonging to the terrorists in the rubbles at the scene.
terrorist axis
The analysis of the guided telephone calls leads to several interlocutors stationed in Mali, Niger as well as Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), the investigators are particularly interested in the numbers of two interlocutors: One number leads to a Nigerian who had long stayed in Maiduguri, a town in northern Nigeria, the cradle of Boko Haram; the other leads to a man who is considered close to the Aqmi and the sect.
"This is progress, but you undoubtedly have to wait for clearer evidence before you can speak of operational cooperation between Aqmi and Boko Harem."
"But it's conceivable."
"It has been known for years that the sect draws financial and intellectual support from others and that it is becoming increasingly active," the Nigerian researcher Kunle Amuwo said.
In recent months, these "support services" have become more and more apparent in their course, as well as the speeches as well as Boko Harem's operating modes, which now indicate the involvement of suicide bombers and the use of increasingly complex explosives that bear the same handwriting as those used by Aqmi.
The international community, in order to start with Washington, has put pressure on Abuja since then in order to take into account the problems caused by Boko Haram.
For a long time, the Nigerian authorities did not consider the grouping to be the community of Illuminati, which does not have to pay special attention.
Rapid success
Boko Haram, founded in 2002 in Maiduguri in the vicinity of a mosquitoe, a school as well as the fundamentalist Iman Mohammed Yusuf - which in Haoussa means "western education is a disgrace" - pursues the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this destitute region, the merger is experiencing rapid success.
She politicizes herself and claims strict adherence to Sharia, while the activists affiliated with her campaign against the godless central government.
Activists initially target churches, bars and authorities.
In 2009, Boko Haram’s activists were particularly active with their increased attacks against the commissaries.
However, Abuja’s reaction is, as usually, indifferenced.
The army surrounds the city, one counts 800 deaths.
Mohammed Yusuf, taken into captivity, is killed in prison.
As a result, the sect is becoming increasingly radical and leaving its strongholds in the north.
Last Christmas she confessed to an assassination in Jos that claimed 80 lives, then to another in Abuja.
In June, Boko Haram is assigned the blame for a suicide bombing that took place at the secured police headquarters in Abuja - the first of its kind.
In August, the local headquarters of the United Nations became a priority international target.
The formation of a terrorist axis in Africa, which is to lead from Mauritania to Nigeria to Somalia, has been worrying all security experts ever since.
While the Aqmi has occupied Mali and Niger, the Somalis of al-Shabaab operating in Kenya, Boko Harem appears to be a new threat in the center of the continent.
Last week Alain Juppé warned of Boko Harem on the occasion of a discussion with President Goodluck Jonathan in Nigeria and showed himself ready "to communicate all clues."
The world according to Republican presidential nominees
Mitt Romney, Herman Cain and Rick Perry rival simplification and ignorance of China, Iraq and assistance to Israel.
Can crisis-weakened America lead a complex world with simple ones, not to say simple-minded ideas?
Listening like this to the Republican presidential nominees, voters could almost get that impression.
For several days now, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and their comrade-in-arms have repeatedly announced their thunderous statements, as well as their statements regarding foreign policy, a traditional republican focus.
About China, Iran and Israel aid - often in no way underpined - slogans fall.
Mitt Romney, who is highest in course with regard to the appointment of the "Grand Old Party", threatened to quote the Chinese before the WTO and accuse them of "currency manipulation" - a leading issue in an America that is irritated by the emigration of entire industries to Asia.
Stupid run if this is to implicate the initiation of a trade war, he said.
Jon Huntsman, former ambassador to China under Obama and the only candidate who seems to have a mature vision of foreign policy, has narrowed the air almost in terms of his emotions-flattering behavior.
He had called for a tangible but constructive exchange of talks with Beijing (alsuably Obama’s current stance).
But Huntsman won't be hearned as he stagnates in the depths of opinion polls.
Reagean's image
Romney had also praised military strikes against Iran if the sanctions to halt the nuclear program fail.
"If I'm elected, I'll put a stop to Iran," he said.
"If Obama is re-elected, they'll get the bomb."
In addition, he has promised increased military aid to Israel and indictated Obama has remained guilty of this partner.
Entrepreneur Herman Cain, the second-hielded candidate, in turn is flaunting shameful file ignorance.
He recently called for the Chinese military threat to be contra, as Beijing "tries to develop a nuclear power" - and in doing should ignore the fact that China has been in possession of military nuclear weapons since 1964!
Texian Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and calls for military strikes against Iran, in site declares willingness to put the American army into service against drug cartels in Mexico.
The recourse to tortural measures against suspected terrorists prohibited by Obama in 2009 was also endorsed by Cain, Perry as well as candidato Michele Bachmann.
One is far from John McCain, the Republican nominee in 2008, a forensic heavyweight.
Contrary to the current presidential opponents, Reagan also had simple ideas and, despite all this, won the Cold War.
They recommend that Obama himself is also an amateur and had to give water into his wine in terms of counterterrorism.
They rightly insist on the failure of his naïve dialogue with Iran, the rash withdrawal of his "boys" from Iraq, his indecisiveness in Libya, which also earned him the reputation of weakness.
However, this hostility is not so casual.
The population is more likely to cherify the positive balance set of national security under Obama, which Bin Laden has turned off.
Since Iraq, she has been wary of military interventions that lead to slamming.
And Obama surprises more appropriately by arguing that America needs to be economically rebuilt to bring his leadership position back to life.
Nord Stream, new gateway to Russian gas in Europe
The Nord Stream gas pipeline, which runs at the bottom of the Baltic Sea and connects western Europe directly with Russia, has been put into operation.
It is intended to supply gas for an equivalent of 30 million households.
Russian gas giant Gazprom wanted to celebrate this event with splendor and glory.
More than 500 invited guests, including four heads of state and government, attended the inauguration of the new Nord Stream gas pipeline last Tuesday on the Baltic Sea coast under orchestral sonication and at gourmet buffet, which directly connects Russia and Germany below sea sea level.
German Chancellor Angelika Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his counterpart Mark Rutte from the Netherlands have symbolically jointly turned the helm of the valve transporting Russian gas into the Western European network.
With the new gas pipeline, 27.5 billion cubic meters of additional Russian gas per year are to be delivered through a first pipeline of 1,224 km in length, which connects the Saint Petersburg region with Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, to Western Europe.
A second pipeline, which allows a total supply of 55 billion cubic meters of gas - the equivalent of French total consumption - is to be completed at the end of 2012.
And one already hints at the eventuality of the construction of a third line on its own route.
For Gazprom, the timely and precise completion of this enormous building seems like a victory.
The project was highly controversial in Poland and the Baltic States.
The Nord Stream pipeline will enable direct gas delivery to Germany, while avoiding any route by third countries (Poland or Ukraine) by land.
For this reason, these two countries are losing a bit of their power: for them, it is now becoming more difficult to put pressure on Russia by threatening to close entry to Western European markets.
Warsaw, most strongly engaged in the debate, was not shunned away from comparing the Nord Stream pipeline with a "new German-Aso-Wiesh pact" in connection with the alliance between Hitler and Stalin for the purpose of Poland's occupation in 1939.
This means that, politically speaking, the issue is extremely sensitive.
Western Europe has gained an even greater security of supply by advocating this Russian project.
This, however, at the expense of invalidation of European energy, since the projection struck a breach between western and eastern Europeans.
In barely five years, Russia has managed to assert its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and the rich, Western countries, which have strong gas consumption and whose gas deposits are running out.
"This gas pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, it is a new artery that connects us in an organic way," François Fillon confirmed at the inauguration of gas pipeline, pointing out the degree to which the major gas contracts are also important from the political and economic side.
All this explains the broad smile of the Gazprom managers represented in Lubmin with regard to gas supply.
The construction of this gas pipeline is also an outstanding technology.
The underwater pipe is made of steel with concrete sheathing, which is intended to protect it from ship anchors.
It rests on the bottom of the sea at a depth of about 200 m.
During their construction, minefields dating from the Second World War had to be dodged.
"About hundreds of mines had to be removed or destroyed as a precaution," said Jens Müller, spokesman for Nord Stream.
In addition, increased measures had to be taken to protect fauna and flora.
The line is a long queue made of metal, heavy like 242 Eiffel towers, which is not interrupted by anything over the entire length: neither by valves, nor maintenance stations.
A control platform was envisaged off the coast of Sweden, but project managers refrained from it, as the Swedes were less eager to see a Russian state society settle at the height of their coasts in a permanent manner.
"In the event of an accident, we close the gas passage in less than a minute," Jens Müller clarified.
In the case of a leak, the gas located in the gas pipeline thus escapes into the air.
However, this case is regarded by the designers as little likely, as the welding seams have been estimated to have a resistance of one leach per 100,000 years.
The state monitoring of the gas pipeline is ensured both by an underwater robot, which is controlled for external observation purposes from a navail, as well as by a device circulating inside the gas pipeline.
It has the shape of a cigar, having a diameter of one meter, is inserted into the interior of the pipeline and moved through the gas pressure.
After three complete days of locomotion through the line at a speed of 3 meters per second, the device provides the most accurate information about any deformations of the metal upon its arrival.
The gas pipeline has been designed to function for fifty years without having to undergo even the slightest repair.
Gas circulation is easily ensured by pressure.
This amounts to 200 bar at the access point, 100 bar at the exit point.
Five companies have united their powers to realize this project.
However, management remains in the hands of Gazprom, which holds 51% of the shares.
Furthermore, the German companies E. ON and BASF are affiliated here (each to 15.5%), as well as the Dutch Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (each at 9%).
These five companies have formed a corporate community called Nord Stream, which is responsible for the construction and operation of gas pipeline.
It is based in Zug in Switzerland, where taxation is particularly favourable.
Gazprom has then rented out to this company the entire capacity of its gas transport, on an extendable twenty-year contract.
"Gazprom will pay for the total capacity, no matter how large the really transported amount of gas fails," Jens Müller clarified.
Financing of the gas pipeline construction was hedged at 30% by shareholders as well as 70% by bank loans.
The total labour costs should amount to 7.4 billion euros for both pipelines.
For GDF Suez, for example, the investment amount was 240 million.
However, the minority shareholders are sure to recover their expenses, as Gazprom alone bears the risk of liability.
The gas pipeline emerges on a sandy, pine-wooded coastline at Lubmin from the Baltic Sea.
The 4.5-meter-high valves form the gas outlet gate.
Sensors measure pressure, temperature and other key figures of the incoming gas before it is forwarded to the German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal has been supplied with one million cubic meters of Russian gas per passing hour.
At full capacity, this would be 3 million cubic meters per hour as well as 6 million in 2012.
Moleskine, a brand notebook.
Launched by an Italian company about fifteen years ago, Moleskine would now like to add a new style to the letter again.
It has a flair of adventure, hours spent by candlelight by writing.
Moleskine’s notebook is easily recognizable again, with its black, rectangular cover, its rubber band, egg-scarf-coloured sides, aged over time.
The Moleskine notebook, as it is known today, however, was created in 1997.
The italian company Modo Modo, which is based in Milan, decided at that time to bring a distinguished design notebook to the market.
Maria Sebregondi, at that time a specialist consultant for the company, is tasked with the market launch of the new product.
The notebook was inspired by a traditional production technique, called mole skin (mole skin), which refers to a cotton bag, which is used, for example, for upholstery covers.
This type of notebook was in high demand among writers and travelers.
"Moleskine has revived an old and almost forgotten object that was considered an icon of avant-garde artists until the 20th century," explains Maria Sebregondi, now responsible for brands at Modo Modo.
Success kicks in.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales increased by 26% and establish themselves with sales of 12.7 million pieces.
This is far removed from the usual production of the first few years, in which only 3,000 notebooks were designed.
Moleskine passes the Italian border and is exported to 70 countries from France to the United States.
It is difficult to make a phantom picture of the buyers, because the notebook inspires businessmen as well as students and professionals.
Moleskine has the image of cosmopolitan, cultivated and open-spirited customers.
The small Italian company, which had 12 employees in its beginnings, now employs over 100 people and has had two offices since 2008; one in Milan, the other in New York.
The company distributes its notebooks in bookshops, on internet portals such as Fnac, in small boutiques of the city centres and is committed to enforcing its identity.
"We have followed the zeitgeist and its new writing needs, which exists despite the upswing of the e-mails," Maria Sebregondi explained.
This cultural trait is accompanied by the development of the Moleskine company: Recently, the brand has launched an exposition called "Detour (Detour) ", in which architects and artists exhibited their own notebooks.
In August, on the occasion of the film festival in Locarno, the company also launched a leopard-painted cover.
Moleskine benefited from his success and this cultural zeitgeist and knew how to diversify its products: bags, appointment calendars, fillers, computer sleeves... The brand has the entire range of items.
In the fall of 2006, Modo Modo was bought by the investment fond SG Capital Europe, today’s Syntegra Capital, opening a new chapter in the history of Moleskine.
Why does the unemployment rate of disabled workers remain at such a high level?
Approximately 19% of disabled people are looking for a workplace.
Today begins "15th Week of Work for Disabled People".
Despite a targeted public policy introduced in 2005, the unemployment rate among disabled people is always twice as high as the average: 19% contrary to 9% unemployment of the total population.
Discourant?
"No, so emphasizes Éric Blanchet, director general of the association Adapt, which is hosting the "Week of Work for the Disabled."
"The progress made within fifteen years is considerable."
"At that time, the unemployment rate was not double, but three times as high."
"Nevertheless, there's an enormous amount left to do."
However, it must be said that there are still numerous obstacles for disabled unemployed people before they get a job.
More often it hits the elderly: 37% are older than 50 years (17% on average).
They have a low level of qualification: 80% of them don’t have a high school diploma and there are only 10,000 disabled people among the 2.3 million students with a higher education degree.
Ultimately, long-term unemployment is also very widespread: 53% among the disabled, compared to 38% overall.
In short, there are so many criteria that complicate a renewed start of work, and which "form the core of the problem," according to Christian Grapin, director-general of Tremplin, an association founded in 1992 that helps companies recruit disabled workers.
So much with the factual brakes.
Remains the unspoken, the attitudes of mind, which are certain, but still slowly evolving.
A study carried out in April by the IMS-Entreprendre association for the journal La Cité stated that the managers surveyed have "a weak level of knowledge regarding the disabled" that is "over-represented" "by persons in wheelchairs, psychologically or intellectually inferior investigators".
The result is that private companies with more than 20 employees still have a long way to go in order to achieve the employment target of 6% disabled workers, which was enshrined in a 1987 law.
These latter figures, which date from 2008, point to a quotient of 2.6%.
"We are now at around 3%," Pierre Blanc, director-general of Agefiph, reports the organization tasked with collecting contributions from companies that disregard this law.
Evidence has reduced the number "fees of paying" permanent establishments, as well as the sum of amounts deposited between 2007 and 2011, from 59,000 to 46,000 (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The 2005 bill, which provides for contribution increases, created strong pressure on the companies, Pierre Blanc explained."
"But this improvement also expresses a real insight, especially among the large company groups."
According to the Ministry of Labor, only 59% of permanent establishments between 20 and 49 employees employ disabled workers, while companies with 100 to 199 employees employ 82%.
Hazard: "How lucky"
Even before foreheading the "Blue Tuesday Evenings (20:45), Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens had not concealed their pleasure, which brought them the friendly game.
It would be too far-reached to claim that there has been a secret agreement between them, but the picture speaks symbolic volumes.
Sitting side by side on the spectator podium of the Stade de France, George Leekens and Eden Hazard would have liked to make peace.
Five months after the temperamental gesture of his attacking player against Turkey, the Belgian national coach had garnered nothing but praise regarding his person.
“I’m very happy with Eden, his progress and the fact that he’s Belgian.”
"I want to look forward," assures Liller.
As for the contrast between his portrayal in the club and the national team"It's a unity that justifies everything."
"In Lille, I'm all year, which isn't the case in Belgium."
"It's time for me to start."
Hazard will come to Saint-Denis, brought Friday night against Romania with regard to the match against France, to finally "shine".
Without, however, omitting the note of "pleasure."
He has been cheering the French national team since the age of 7, leading his victorious campaigns between 1998 and 2000.
"I've always been supported," he confirmed.
This inevitably contributes to amplifying the "unique" character of this encounter even more.
So much in advance: "Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami) will be there," he recalls.
"We've been crossing paths right now."
"To be able to play against them means great luck!"
Leekens, for his part, hinted "Pride" with good-naturedness.
"The Pride of Carrying Out a Galamatch."
"Proud" also to confirm - in its capacity as "neighbours" - that France "again has become a team at a very high level," and little more than a year after Knysna's trauma.
"Laurent Blanc did a damn good job," he said enthusiastically.
Although Belgium will be missing in the next European Championship, the technician sees himself sharing the plane with the Blues (the French national team) ... in the direction of Brazil in 2014.
"We little Belgians are coming out big," he insisted.
“We’re in the process of planning, the players are ambitious, they’re hungry.”
"You will notice that in the upcoming matches". The game against France included.
Portugal very confident
Paulo Bento's Portuguese team looks forward with great optimism to the return match against Bosnia (22:00 p.m.
At the first leg, both teams lacked objectivity.
"At the end of these 90 minutes, we will be a happy team and, above all, a happy country."
Paulo Bento already sees himself in Poland and Ukraine.
For him, there is not the slightest hint of doubt that "La Selecçao", his national eleven, for whom he has been responsible since September 2010, will qualify for the 2012 European Championships.
For Portugal, this only means 90 minutes of bliss.
But there's a match to play, and serving up an opponent, namely Bosnia.
In view of the first leg (0-0) on Friday in the hell of Zenica, it seems obvious that the dice have not fallen yet.
Portugal clearly dominated the game for more than an hour, but the squad was unable to impose such an important stamp on the matter, which would have taken the pressure off it.
"That's a good result, but it carries a certain risk because we didn't score an away goal."
At home, however, in their brightly shining Stade de la Luz in Lisbon, the Portuguese are convinced that they will get through in front of an audience, which should also be hot for the game, even if the game was of course announced as "very succinct", said the optimistic, but clear-sighted Bento.
"We expect a very close match, but are completely convinced to be able to win the upper hand," explained the 42-year-old Portuguese coach.
"We continue to have a 50% chance to be part of at the 2012 European Championships," said Carlos Queiroz.
Friday's draw in Bosnia forces the Portuguese to score a goal and win.
A draw can suffice for the Bosnians, who also need a goal.
"That's a good result, but that carries some risk, since we didn't score an away goal," Bento regrets.
"We will compete against a squad that possesses high technical qualities and has players with many individual qualities," he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese would intend to put maximum pressure on the Bosnians at the beginning "so as not to give them time to think."
A good idea, provided that it is put into action this time.
